{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120589150,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b7e22f327df21ec7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: status: accepted\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n6: \n7: Decided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\n8: appears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n9: \n10: The float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\n11: binary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\n12: the repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\n13: JavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\n14: established the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\n15: setting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\n16: have only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n17: \n18: Not decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\n19: agree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n20: `Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\n21: and reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\n22: associativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\n23: but those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\n24: [draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).\n\n(End of file - total 24 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\nappears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n\nThe float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\nbinary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\nthe repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\nJavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\nestablished the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\nsetting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\nhave only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n\nNot decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\nagree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n`Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md","text":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\nappears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n\nThe float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\nbinary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\nthe repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\nJavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\nestablished the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\nsetting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\nhave only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n\nNot decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\nagree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n`Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\nand reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\nassociativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\nbut those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\n[draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":24,"totalLines":24,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md","time":{"start":1788120589139,"end":1788120589149}},"id":"prt_0544ae1cb001DoexjrPUD4EikC","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","messageID":"msg_0544ad8f2001ir3AZZUnyqAvnr"}}
