{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788120634873,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"id":"prt_0544b95f60019WtZpTBDcVMUXn","messageID":"msg_0544b93510014vMX82BlagJ5Vn","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","snapshot":"94b6040ffbb3d9f05c4638216dc44bf3958c7c09","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120635996,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ae871cddba953ea2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/ty.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/ty.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use std::collections::HashMap;\n2: \n3: /// Every enum a program declares, resolved once and keyed by name, which is an enum's identity.\n4: ///\n5: /// A `Type::Enum` in hand is not always enough to work from. Where an enum's payload reaches\n6: /// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n7: /// variant list (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), since expanding it is what\n8: /// has no end. So nothing reads `variants` off a type directly: everything that needs the list\n9: /// asks `variants` below, which re-derives it here, one layer at a time and exactly as deep as\n10: /// the program itself navigates (kantord/toylang#94).\n11: pub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n12: \n13: #[derive(Debug, Clone)]\n14: pub enum Type {\n15:     Str,\n16:     Int,\n17:     /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n18:     /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n19:     /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n20:     /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\n21:     /// wherever one is expected, the `[]` rule applied to numbers. Barred from `input`, like\n22:     /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n23:     /// cannot yet read one faithfully (JS parses numbers into doubles), and that codec design\n24:     /// has not been done.\n25:     Int64,\n26:     Bool,\n27:     /// A single Unicode scalar value (kantord/toylang#75): never a surrogate half, so a\n28:     /// codepoint outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one `Char`, on every backend, even\n29:     /// where a backend's own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it. Produced only by\n30:     /// `chars`, which is also the only way one is compared against a boundary -- there is no\n31:     /// literal syntax, so a program spells `'a'` as `chars(\"a\")[0]!`. Barred from `input` (no\n32:     /// wire form to read one from) and from the program's own printed result (no wire form to\n33:     /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n34:     /// it is an ordinary type.\n35:     Char,\n36:     Vec(Box<Type>),\n37:     /// Fields in declaration order. Order is not part of the type's identity (kantord/toylang#60:\n38:     /// `{a: Int, b: Int}` and `{b: Int, a: Int}` are one type, so `PartialEq` below compares the\n39:     /// field set, not the spelling), but it survives as metadata on every concrete value: a\n40:     /// literal's own written order is what it prints in on every backend and what the\n41:     /// native/Go column layouts key on. Whichever type a value is checked against is the order\n42:     /// it ends up carrying (`check::reorder_record` rebuilds a value that arrives shuffled), so\n43:     /// two differently-ordered values only coexist when nothing ever placed them in the same\n44:     /// checked position.\n45:     Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n46:     /// Effect-layer multiplicity: the entries arrive one at a time as evaluation proceeds, and\n47:     /// no stream object ever exists as a value (ADR 0001). Spellable in function signatures,\n48:     /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n49:     /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n50:     /// exiting only through `collect`.\n51:     Stream(Box<Type>),\n52:     /// A declared enum: nominal, so the name is the identity. A name (and, for a generic enum,\n53:     /// its arguments) determines the variants within a program, so `PartialEq` below compares\n54:     /// only those and not `variants` itself -- which matters because a self-referential enum's\n55:     /// own payload carries a placeholder here in place of the real list\n56:     /// (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76) rather than the list every other\n57:     /// occurrence of the same name carries. A variant's payload is `None` for a unit variant,\n58:     /// in declaration order.\n59:     ///\n60:     /// The list riding along is a convenience, not the source of truth: read it and a\n61:     /// placeholder reads as an enum with no variants at all, which is how a nested recursive\n62:     /// value used to reach every backend's printer as something it could not render\n63:     /// (kantord/toylang#94). Ask `variants` instead.\n64:     Enum {\n65:         name: String,\n66:         /// The type arguments this instantiation was built with, in declaration order; empty\n67:         /// for an enum declared without parameters. Part of the identity: `Pair<Int>` and\n68:         /// `Pair<Str>` are different types even though both are `Pair`.\n69:         args: Vec<Type>,\n70:         variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n71:     },\n72:     /// A type parameter inside a generic enum's registry template, standing for whatever a\n73:     /// use site will supply. Never the type of any checked expression: instantiation\n74:     /// substitutes every one away before a type leaves resolution.\n75:     Param(String),\n76: }\n77: \n78: /// Names reserved for the built-in type constructors, on top of `Str`/`Int`/`Bool`\n79: /// (`Type::from_name`): a program cannot declare an alias or enum under any of these.\n80: const RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES: [&str; 2] = [\"Vec\", \"Stream\"];\n81: \n82: /// Whether `name` is a built-in type and so cannot be redefined as an alias or an enum.\n83: pub fn is_builtin_type_name(name: &str) -> bool {\n84:     Type::from_name(name).is_some() || RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES.contains(&name)\n85: }\n86: \n87: /// Whether `name` is one of the built-in type constructors with its own `TypeExpr` node.\n88: /// `Opt` is no longer here: it is the prelude's enum, reached through the ordinary generic\n89: /// path like any declared name.\n90: pub fn takes_type_arg(name: &str) -> bool {\n91:     matches!(name, \"Vec\" | \"Stream\")\n92: }\n93: \n94: impl Type {\n95:     pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n96:         match name {\n97:             \"Str\" => Some(Type::Str),\n98:             \"Int\" => Some(Type::Int),\n99:             \"Int64\" => Some(Type::Int64),\n100:             \"Bool\" => Some(Type::Bool),\n101:             \"Char\" => Some(Type::Char),\n102:             _ => None,\n103:         }\n104:     }\n105: \n106:     /// Whether `ty` could hold a stream anywhere inside it -- through a Vec or a record\n107:     /// field. A stream cannot be printed, so this is what the checker asks before letting a\n108:     /// type become the program's final result.\n109:     pub fn contains_stream(&self) -> bool {\n110:         match self {\n111:             Type::Stream(_) => true,\n112:             Type::Vec(t) => t.contains_stream(),\n113:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_stream()),\n114:             // An enum payload cannot hold a stream: resolve_enum refuses the declaration,\n115:             // and instantiation refuses a stream as a type argument.\n116:             _ => false,\n117:         }\n118:     }\n119: \n120:     pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n121:         match self {\n122:             Type::Vec(t) => Some(t),\n123:             _ => None,\n124:         }\n125:     }\n126: \n127:     pub fn field(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Type> {\n128:         match self {\n129:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == name).map(|(_, t)| t),\n130:             _ => None,\n131:         }\n132:     }\n133: \n134:     /// The prelude's `Opt<T>`, and what its `T` is. The name test is sound because `Opt` is\n135:     /// the prelude's declaration and a program redeclaring it is a duplicate-type error, so\n136:     /// no other enum can carry the name. This is what every place that used to match the\n137:     /// built-in `Type::Opt` keys on now: the unwrap operator, the producers' wrapping, and\n138:     /// the printers' bare-value-or-null serialization rule.\n139:     pub fn as_opt(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n140:         match self {\n141:             Type::Enum { name, args, .. } if name == \"Opt\" && args.len() == 1 => Some(&args[0]),\n142:             _ => None,\n143:         }\n144:     }\n145: \n146:     /// Whether `Opt` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of an input type:\n147:     /// absence has no ratified wire form (`null` reading as `none` is codec design nobody has\n148:     /// done), so an input cannot be Opt-typed anywhere in its shape.\n149:     pub fn contains_opt(&self) -> bool {\n150:         if self.as_opt().is_some() {\n151:             return true;\n152:         }\n153:         match self {\n154:             Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_opt(),\n155:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_opt()),\n156:             Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n157:                 args.iter().any(Type::contains_opt)\n158:                     || variants\n159:                         .iter()\n160:                         .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_opt))\n161:             }\n162:             _ => false,\n163:         }\n164:     }\n165: \n166:     /// Whether `Int64` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of an input type:\n167:     /// unlike `Char` this is one-directional -- an `Int64` result prints fine on every backend\n168:     /// -- but reading one back is codec design nobody has done (JS parses JSON numbers into\n169:     /// doubles and loses exactness past 2^53), so `input` refuses it, the same reversible\n170:     /// direction `contains_opt` takes for absence.\n171:     pub fn contains_int64(&self) -> bool {\n172:         match self {\n173:             Type::Int64 => true,\n174:             Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_int64(),\n175:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_int64()),\n176:             Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n177:                 args.iter().any(Type::contains_int64)\n178:                     || variants\n179:                         .iter()\n180:                         .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_int64))\n181:             }\n182:             _ => false,\n183:         }\n184:     }\n185: \n186:     /// Whether `Char` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of both `input`'s\n187:     /// type and the program's own printed result: neither has a wire form for a bare Unicode\n188:     /// scalar value, so both refuse it, the same reasoning `contains_opt` states for absence.\n189:     pub fn contains_char(&self) -> bool {\n190:         match self {\n191:             Type::Char => true,\n192:             Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_char(),\n193:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_char()),\n194:             Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n195:                 args.iter().any(Type::contains_char)\n196:                     || variants\n197:                         .iter()\n198:                         .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_char))\n199:             }\n200:             _ => false,\n201:         }\n202:     }\n203: \n204:     /// Whether a `Vec` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of a comparison's\n205:     /// operands: an operator applied to a Vec is Q2 -- broadcast, zip, or whole-value -- and Q2\n206:     /// is open, so a Vec reached through a record field or an enum payload is refused for the\n207:     /// same reason a bare one is, rather than being handed whole-value equality by the back\n208:     /// door (kantord/toylang#95).\n209:     pub fn contains_vec(&self) -> bool {\n210:         match self {\n211:             Type::Vec(_) | Type::Stream(_) => true,\n212:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_vec()),\n213:             Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n214:                 args.iter().any(Type::contains_vec)\n215:                     || variants\n216:                         .iter()\n217:                         .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_vec))\n218:             }\n219:             _ => false,\n220:         }\n221:     }\n222: \n223:     /// Whether a value of this type has an interior to walk. Equality on one is structural on\n224:     /// every backend (kantord/toylang#68), which four of them need spelled out because their\n225:     /// native `==` on the runtime shape means identity or compares a payload pointer.\n226:     pub fn is_composite(&self) -> bool {\n227:         matches!(self, Type::Record(_) | Type::Enum { .. })\n228:     }\n229: \n230:     /// A deterministic identifier fragment for this type, for backends whose targets are\n231:     /// nominally typed: `Pair<Int>` and `Pair<Str>` must become distinct emitted types, so\n232:     /// their names embed the arguments (`Pair_Int`). Record fields sort by name, because\n233:     /// field order is not part of a record type's identity (kantord/toylang#60). An enum\n234:     /// declared without parameters keeps its bare name, which is what every existing\n235:     /// snapshot pins.\n236:     pub fn ident(&self) -> String {\n237:         match self {\n238:             Type::Str => \"Str\".to_string(),\n239:             Type::Int => \"Int\".to_string(),\n240:             Type::Int64 => \"Int64\".to_string(),\n241:             Type::Bool => \"Bool\".to_string(),\n242:             Type::Char => \"Char\".to_string(),\n243:             Type::Vec(t) => format!(\"Vec_{}\", t.ident()),\n244:             Type::Stream(t) => format!(\"Stream_{}\", t.ident()),\n245:             Type::Record(fields) => {\n246:                 let mut parts: Vec<String> = fields\n247:                     .iter()\n248:                     .map(|(n, t)| format!(\"{n}_{}\", t.ident()))\n249:                     .collect();\n250:                 parts.sort();\n251:                 format!(\"R_{}\", parts.join(\"_\"))\n252:             }\n253:             Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n254:                 if args.is_empty() {\n255:                     name.clone()\n256:                 } else {\n257:                     let parts: Vec<String> = args.iter().map(Type::ident).collect();\n258:                     format!(\"{name}_{}\", parts.join(\"_\"))\n259:                 }\n260:             }\n261:             Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before any backend runs\"),\n262:         }\n263:     }\n264: \n265:     /// The name of `self`'s own printer function, shared by every backend that emits one\n266:     /// (kantord/toylang#94). Go camel-cases its own variant at the call site.\n267:     pub fn show_fn(&self) -> String {\n268:         format!(\"tl_show_{}\", self.ident())\n269:     }\n270: }\n271: \n272: impl std::fmt::Display for Type {\n273:     fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n274:         match self {\n275:             Type::Stream(t) => write!(f, \"Stream<{t}>\"),\n276:             Type::Str => write!(f, \"Str\"),\n277:             Type::Int => write!(f, \"Int\"),\n278:             Type::Int64 => write!(f, \"Int64\"),\n279:             Type::Bool => write!(f, \"Bool\"),\n280:             Type::Char => write!(f, \"Char\"),\n281:             Type::Vec(t) => write!(f, \"Vec<{t}>\"),\n282:             Type::Record(fields) => {\n283:                 let parts: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, t)| format!(\"{n}: {t}\")).collect();\n284:                 write!(f, \"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n285:             }\n286:             // The name is the identity, so the name is the display: spelling the variants out\n287:             // would print the definition where the reader wants the reference.\n288:             Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n289:                 write!(f, \"{name}\")?;\n290:                 if !args.is_empty() {\n291:                     let parts: Vec<String> = args.iter().map(|a| a.to_string()).collect();\n292:                     write!(f, \"<{}>\", parts.join(\", \"))?;\n293:                 }\n294:                 Ok(())\n295:             }\n296:             Type::Param(name) => write!(f, \"{name}\"),\n297:         }\n298:     }\n299: }\n300: \n301: impl PartialEq for Type {\n302:     /// Structural equality, except `Record`: its fields compare as a set, keyed by name and\n303:     /// type, ignoring position (kantord/toylang#60). A record's own field order is real data --\n304:     /// it drives printing and backend column layout -- but it is not part of what makes two\n305:     /// types the same type.\n306:     ///\n307:     /// `Enum` compares `name` and `args` only, not `variants`: those two already determine the\n308:     /// variant list within one program (a redeclaration is refused, and an instantiation's\n309:     /// payloads are `args` substituted into the one declaration), so comparing `variants` too\n310:     /// would be redundant on every ordinary type and actively wrong on a self-referential one,\n311:     /// whose nested occurrence of itself carries a placeholder rather than the list every other\n312:     /// occurrence of the same name carries (kantord/toylang#76).\n313:     fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n314:         match (self, other) {\n315:             (Type::Str, Type::Str)\n316:             | (Type::Int, Type::Int)\n317:             | (Type::Int64, Type::Int64)\n318:             | (Type::Bool, Type::Bool)\n319:             | (Type::Char, Type::Char) => true,\n320:             (Type::Vec(a), Type::Vec(b)) => a == b,\n321:             (Type::Stream(a), Type::Stream(b)) => a == b,\n322:             (Type::Record(a), Type::Record(b)) => {\n323:                 a.len() == b.len() && a.iter().all(|field| b.contains(field))\n324:             }\n325:             (\n326:                 Type::Enum {\n327:                     name: n1, args: a1, ..\n328:                 },\n329:                 Type::Enum {\n330:                     name: n2, args: a2, ..\n331:                 },\n332:             ) => n1 == n2 && a1 == a2,\n333:             (Type::Param(a), Type::Param(b)) => a == b,\n334:             _ => false,\n335:         }\n336:     }\n337: }\n338: \n339: /// A function takes at most one parameter; `None` is a nullary function.\n340: #[derive(Debug, Clone)]\n341: pub struct Sig {\n342:     pub param: Option<Type>,\n343:     pub ret: Type,\n344: }\n345: \n346: /// Replace every `Type::Param` in `t` with its binding. Together with the checker's `unify`,\n347: /// this is the whole of generic instantiation: nothing else ever sees a parameter.\n348: pub fn substitute(t: &Type, map: &HashMap<String, Type>) -> Type {\n349:     match t {\n350:         Type::Param(p) => map\n351:             .get(p)\n352:             .cloned()\n353:             .unwrap_or_else(|| unreachable!(\"substitute runs only once every param is bound\")),\n354:         Type::Vec(e) => Type::Vec(Box::new(substitute(e, map))),\n355:         Type::Stream(e) => Type::Stream(Box::new(substitute(e, map))),\n356:         Type::Record(fields) => Type::Record(\n357:             fields\n358:                 .iter()\n359:                 .map(|(n, t)| (n.clone(), substitute(t, map)))\n360:                 .collect(),\n361:         ),\n362:         Type::Enum {\n363:             name,\n364:             args,\n365:             variants,\n366:         } => Type::Enum {\n367:             name: name.clone(),\n368:             args: args.iter().map(|a| substitute(a, map)).collect(),\n369:             variants: variants\n370:                 .iter()\n371:                 .map(|(n, p)| (n.clone(), p.as_ref().map(|p| substitute(p, map))))\n372:                 .collect(),\n373:         },\n374:         Type::Str | Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool | Type::Char => t.clone(),\n375:     }\n376: }\n377: \n378: /// `name`'s variant list at `args`, re-derived from the registry rather than trusted from\n379: /// whichever `Type::Enum` a caller happened to be holding. One layer deep: a payload that is\n380: /// itself the same enum comes back carrying the placeholder in turn, so asking again is what\n381: /// descends, and nothing tries to build a list that has no bottom.\n382: pub fn variants_of(enums: &Enums, name: &str, args: &[Type]) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n383:     let Type::Enum {\n384:         args: template_args,\n385:         variants,\n386:         ..\n387:     } = &enums[name]\n388:     else {\n389:         unreachable!(\"the registry holds enum types\")\n390:     };\n391:     if template_args.is_empty() {\n392:         return variants.clone();\n393:     }\n394:     let bindings: HashMap<String, Type> = template_args\n395:         .iter()\n396:         .zip(args)\n397:         .filter_map(|(p, a)| match p {\n398:             Type::Param(p) => Some((p.clone(), a.clone())),\n399:             _ => None,\n400:         })\n401:         .collect();\n402:     variants\n403:         .iter()\n404:         .map(|(n, p)| (n.clone(), p.as_ref().map(|t| substitute(t, &bindings))))\n405:         .collect()\n406: }\n407: \n408: /// `variants_of` for an enum type in hand.\n409: pub fn variants(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n410:     let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = ty else {\n411:         unreachable!(\"only an enum has variants\")\n412:     };\n413:     variants_of(enums, name, args)\n414: }\n415: \n416: /// Whether a value of this enum type can hold another of the same type, however deep.\n417: ///\n418: /// Always through a `Vec`, since that is the only self-reference the checker allows, but not\n419: /// always directly: the path can run through a record field or a second enum. What it decides\n420: /// is whether a backend may expand this type inline -- a printer, a parser, a declaration\n421: /// written by walking the payloads -- or has to emit a named function it can call back into.\n422: /// Expanding a recursive one inline does not terminate.\n423: pub fn is_recursive(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n424:     fn reaches(enums: &Enums, from: &Type, target: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>) -> bool {\n425:         match from {\n426:             Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => reaches(enums, e, target, seen),\n427:             Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| reaches(enums, t, target, seen)),\n428:             Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n429:                 if from == target {\n430:                     return true;\n431:                 }\n432:                 if seen.contains(from) {\n433:                     return false;\n434:                 }\n435:                 seen.push(from.clone());\n436:                 args.iter().any(|a| reaches(enums, a, target, seen))\n437:                     || variants_of(enums, name, args)\n438:                         .iter()\n439:                         .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| reaches(enums, p, target, seen)))\n440:             }\n441:             _ => false,\n442:         }\n443:     }\n444:     if !matches!(ty, Type::Enum { .. }) {\n445:         return false;\n446:     }\n447:     let mut seen = vec![ty.clone()];\n448:     variants(enums, ty)\n449:         .iter()\n450:         .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| reaches(enums, p, ty, &mut seen)))\n451: }\n\n(End of file - total 451 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\n/// Every enum a program declares, resolved once and keyed by name, which is an enum's identity.\n///\n/// A `Type::Enum` in hand is not always enough to work from. Where an enum's payload reaches\n/// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n/// variant list (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), since expanding it is what\n/// has no end. So nothing reads `variants` off a type directly: everything that needs the list\n/// asks `variants` below, which re-derives it here, one layer at a time and exactly as deep as\n/// the program itself navigates (kantord/toylang#94).\npub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub enum Type {\n    Str,\n    Int,\n    /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n    /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n    /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n    /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/ty.rs","text":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\n/// Every enum a program declares, resolved once and keyed by name, which is an enum's identity.\n///\n/// A `Type::Enum` in hand is not always enough to work from. Where an enum's payload reaches\n/// back to itself through a `Vec`, that occurrence carries a placeholder in place of the real\n/// variant list (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), since expanding it is what\n/// has no end. So nothing reads `variants` off a type directly: everything that needs the list\n/// asks `variants` below, which re-derives it here, one layer at a time and exactly as deep as\n/// the program itself navigates (kantord/toylang#94).\npub type Enums = HashMap<String, Type>;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub enum Type {\n    Str,\n    Int,\n    /// A signed 64-bit integer that wraps, `Int`'s rules at twice the width (kantord/toylang#83,\n    /// extending ADR 0006). Deliberately a separate type rather than a widening of `Int`:\n    /// nothing converts implicitly, so mixing the two demands an explicit `i64(x)`. Literals\n    /// carry no suffix -- one that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\n    /// wherever one is expected, the `[]` rule applied to numbers. Barred from `input`, like\n    /// `Opt`: not because a 64-bit integer has no JSON spelling but because two backends\n    /// cannot yet read one faithfully (JS parses numbers into doubles), and that codec design\n    /// has not been done.\n    Int64,\n    Bool,\n    /// A single Unicode scalar value (kantord/toylang#75): never a surrogate half, so a\n    /// codepoint outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one `Char`, on every backend, even\n    /// where a backend's own strings need a surrogate pair to spell it. Produced only by\n    /// `chars`, which is also the only way one is compared against a boundary -- there is no\n    /// literal syntax, so a program spells `'a'` as `chars(\"a\")[0]!`. Barred from `input` (no\n    /// wire form to read one from) and from the program's own printed result (no wire form to\n    /// write one either); everywhere else -- function signatures, records, a `Vec<Char>` --\n    /// it is an ordinary type.\n    Char,\n    Vec(Box<Type>),\n    /// Fields in declaration order. Order is not part of the type's identity (kantord/toylang#60:\n    /// `{a: Int, b: Int}` and `{b: Int, a: Int}` are one type, so `PartialEq` below compares the\n    /// field set, not the spelling), but it survives as metadata on every concrete value: a\n    /// literal's own written order is what it prints in on every backend and what the\n    /// native/Go column layouts key on. Whichever type a value is checked against is the order\n    /// it ends up carrying (`check::reorder_record` rebuilds a value that arrives shuffled), so\n    /// two differently-ordered values only coexist when nothing ever placed them in the same\n    /// checked position.\n    Record(Vec<(String, Type)>),\n    /// Effect-layer multiplicity: the entries arrive one at a time as evaluation proceeds, and\n    /// no stream object ever exists as a value (ADR 0001). Spellable in function signatures,\n    /// and only there -- the type grammar refuses it inside a Vec, a record, or another Stream\n    /// -- born at a source (`lines` is `Stream<Str>`), consumed exactly once per binding, and\n    /// exiting only through `collect`.\n    Stream(Box<Type>),\n    /// A declared enum: nominal, so the name is the identity. A name (and, for a generic enum,\n    /// its arguments) determines the variants within a program, so `PartialEq` below compares\n    /// only those and not `variants` itself -- which matters because a self-referential enum's\n    /// own payload carries a placeholder here in place of the real list\n    /// (`check::types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76) rather than the list every other\n    /// occurrence of the same name carries. A variant's payload is `None` for a unit variant,\n    /// in declaration order.\n    ///\n    /// The list riding along is a convenience, not the source of truth: read it and a\n    /// placeholder reads as an enum with no variants at all, which is how a nested recursive\n    /// value used to reach every backend's printer as something it could not render\n    /// (kantord/toylang#94). Ask `variants` instead.\n    Enum {\n        name: String,\n        /// The type arguments this instantiation was built with, in declaration order; empty\n        /// for an enum declared without parameters. Part of the identity: `Pair<Int>` and\n        /// `Pair<Str>` are different types even though both are `Pair`.\n        args: Vec<Type>,\n        variants: Vec<(String, Option<Type>)>,\n    },\n    /// A type parameter inside a generic enum's registry template, standing for whatever a\n    /// use site will supply. Never the type of any checked expression: instantiation\n    /// substitutes every one away before a type leaves resolution.\n    Param(String),\n}\n\n/// Names reserved for the built-in type constructors, on top of `Str`/`Int`/`Bool`\n/// (`Type::from_name`): a program cannot declare an alias or enum under any of these.\nconst RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES: [&str; 2] = [\"Vec\", \"Stream\"];\n\n/// Whether `name` is a built-in type and so cannot be redefined as an alias or an enum.\npub fn is_builtin_type_name(name: &str) -> bool {\n    Type::from_name(name).is_some() || RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES.contains(&name)\n}\n\n/// Whether `name` is one of the built-in type constructors with its own `TypeExpr` node.\n/// `Opt` is no longer here: it is the prelude's enum, reached through the ordinary generic\n/// path like any declared name.\npub fn takes_type_arg(name: &str) -> bool {\n    matches!(name, \"Vec\" | \"Stream\")\n}\n\nimpl Type {\n    pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<Type> {\n        match name {\n            \"Str\" => Some(Type::Str),\n            \"Int\" => Some(Type::Int),\n            \"Int64\" => Some(Type::Int64),\n            \"Bool\" => Some(Type::Bool),\n            \"Char\" => Some(Type::Char),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether `ty` could hold a stream anywhere inside it -- through a Vec or a record\n    /// field. A stream cannot be printed, so this is what the checker asks before letting a\n    /// type become the program's final result.\n    pub fn contains_stream(&self) -> bool {\n        match self {\n            Type::Stream(_) => true,\n            Type::Vec(t) => t.contains_stream(),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_stream()),\n            // An enum payload cannot hold a stream: resolve_enum refuses the declaration,\n            // and instantiation refuses a stream as a type argument.\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n        match self {\n            Type::Vec(t) => Some(t),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn field(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Type> {\n        match self {\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == name).map(|(_, t)| t),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The prelude's `Opt<T>`, and what its `T` is. The name test is sound because `Opt` is\n    /// the prelude's declaration and a program redeclaring it is a duplicate-type error, so\n    /// no other enum can carry the name. This is what every place that used to match the\n    /// built-in `Type::Opt` keys on now: the unwrap operator, the producers' wrapping, and\n    /// the printers' bare-value-or-null serialization rule.\n    pub fn as_opt(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n        match self {\n            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } if name == \"Opt\" && args.len() == 1 => Some(&args[0]),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether `Opt` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of an input type:\n    /// absence has no ratified wire form (`null` reading as `none` is codec design nobody has\n    /// done), so an input cannot be Opt-typed anywhere in its shape.\n    pub fn contains_opt(&self) -> bool {\n        if self.as_opt().is_some() {\n            return true;\n        }\n        match self {\n            Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_opt(),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_opt()),\n            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n                args.iter().any(Type::contains_opt)\n                    || variants\n                        .iter()\n                        .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_opt))\n            }\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether `Int64` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of an input type:\n    /// unlike `Char` this is one-directional -- an `Int64` result prints fine on every backend\n    /// -- but reading one back is codec design nobody has done (JS parses JSON numbers into\n    /// doubles and loses exactness past 2^53), so `input` refuses it, the same reversible\n    /// direction `contains_opt` takes for absence.\n    pub fn contains_int64(&self) -> bool {\n        match self {\n            Type::Int64 => true,\n            Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_int64(),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_int64()),\n            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n                args.iter().any(Type::contains_int64)\n                    || variants\n                        .iter()\n                        .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_int64))\n            }\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether `Char` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of both `input`'s\n    /// type and the program's own printed result: neither has a wire form for a bare Unicode\n    /// scalar value, so both refuse it, the same reasoning `contains_opt` states for absence.\n    pub fn contains_char(&self) -> bool {\n        match self {\n            Type::Char => true,\n            Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => t.contains_char(),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_char()),\n            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n                args.iter().any(Type::contains_char)\n                    || variants\n                        .iter()\n                        .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_char))\n            }\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether a `Vec` appears anywhere in this type. What the checker asks of a comparison's\n    /// operands: an operator applied to a Vec is Q2 -- broadcast, zip, or whole-value -- and Q2\n    /// is open, so a Vec reached through a record field or an enum payload is refused for the\n    /// same reason a bare one is, rather than being handed whole-value equality by the back\n    /// door (kantord/toylang#95).\n    pub fn contains_vec(&self) -> bool {\n        match self {\n            Type::Vec(_) | Type::Stream(_) => true,\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| t.contains_vec()),\n            Type::Enum { args, variants, .. } => {\n                args.iter().any(Type::contains_vec)\n                    || variants\n                        .iter()\n                        .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(Type::contains_vec))\n            }\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether a value of this type has an interior to walk. Equality on one is structural on\n    /// every backend (kantord/toylang#68), which four of them need spelled out because their\n    /// native `==` on the runtime shape means identity or compares a payload pointer.\n    pub fn is_composite(&self) -> bool {\n        matches!(self, Type::Record(_) | Type::Enum { .. })\n    }\n\n    /// A deterministic identifier fragment for this type, for backends whose targets are\n    /// nominally typed: `Pair<Int>` and `Pair<Str>` must become distinct emitted types, so\n    /// their names embed the arguments (`Pair_Int`). Record fields sort by name, because\n    /// field order is not part of a record type's identity (kantord/toylang#60). An enum\n    /// declared without parameters keeps its bare name, which is what every existing\n    /// snapshot pins.\n    pub fn ident(&self) -> String {\n        match self {\n            Type::Str => \"Str\".to_string(),\n            Type::Int => \"Int\".to_string(),\n            Type::Int64 => \"Int64\".to_string(),\n            Type::Bool => \"Bool\".to_string(),\n            Type::Char => \"Char\".to_string(),\n            Type::Vec(t) => format!(\"Vec_{}\", t.ident()),\n            Type::Stream(t) => format!(\"Stream_{}\", t.ident()),\n            Type::Record(fields) => {\n                let mut parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                    .iter()\n                    .map(|(n, t)| format!(\"{n}_{}\", t.ident()))\n                    .collect();\n                parts.sort();\n                format!(\"R_{}\", parts.join(\"_\"))\n            }\n            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n                if args.is_empty() {\n                    name.clone()\n                } else {\n                    let parts: Vec<String> = args.iter().map(Type::ident).collect();\n                    format!(\"{name}_{}\", parts.join(\"_\"))\n                }\n            }\n            Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before any backend runs\"),\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The name of `self`'s own printer function, shared by every backend that emits one\n    /// (kantord/toylang#94). Go camel-cases its own variant at the call site.\n    pub fn show_fn(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"tl_show_{}\", self.ident())\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for Type {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        match self {\n            Type::Stream(t) => write!(f, \"Stream<{t}>\"),\n            Type::Str => write!(f, \"Str\"),\n            Type::Int => write!(f, \"Int\"),\n            Type::Int64 => write!(f, \"Int64\"),\n            Type::Bool => write!(f, \"Bool\"),\n            Type::Char => write!(f, \"Char\"),\n            Type::Vec(t) => write!(f, \"Vec<{t}>\"),\n            Type::Record(fields) => {\n                let parts: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, t)| format!(\"{n}: {t}\")).collect();\n                write!(f, \"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n            }\n            // The name is the identity, so the name is the display: spelling the variants out\n            // would print the definition where the reader wants the reference.\n            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n                write!(f, \"{name}\")?;\n                if !args.is_empty() {\n                    let parts: Vec<String> = args.iter().map(|a| a.to_string()).collect();\n                    write!(f, \"<{}>\", parts.join(\", \"))?;\n                }\n                Ok(())\n            }\n            Type::Param(name) => write!(f, \"{name}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl PartialEq for Type {\n    /// Structural equality, except `Record`: its fields compare as a set, keyed by name and\n    /// type, ignoring position (kantord/toylang#60). A record's own field order is real data --\n    /// it drives printing and backend column layout -- but it is not part of what makes two\n    /// types the same type.\n    ///\n    /// `Enum` compares `name` and `args` only, not `variants`: those two already determine the\n    /// variant list within one program (a redeclaration is refused, and an instantiation's\n    /// payloads are `args` substituted into the one declaration), so comparing `variants` too\n    /// would be redundant on every ordinary type and actively wrong on a self-referential one,\n    /// whose nested occurrence of itself carries a placeholder rather than the list every other\n    /// occurrence of the same name carries (kantord/toylang#76).\n    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n        match (self, other) {\n            (Type::Str, Type::Str)\n            | (Type::Int, Type::Int)\n            | (Type::Int64, Type::Int64)\n            | (Type::Bool, Type::Bool)\n            | (Type::Char, Type::Char) => true,\n            (Type::Vec(a), Type::Vec(b)) => a == b,\n            (Type::Stream(a), Type::Stream(b)) => a == b,\n            (Type::Record(a), Type::Record(b)) => {\n                a.len() == b.len() && a.iter().all(|field| b.contains(field))\n            }\n            (\n                Type::Enum {\n                    name: n1, args: a1, ..\n                },\n                Type::Enum {\n                    name: n2, args: a2, ..\n                },\n            ) => n1 == n2 && a1 == a2,\n            (Type::Param(a), Type::Param(b)) => a == b,\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// A function takes at most one parameter; `None` is a nullary function.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub struct Sig {\n    pub param: Option<Type>,\n    pub ret: Type,\n}\n\n/// Replace every `Type::Param` in `t` with its binding. Together with the checker's `unify`,\n/// this is the whole of generic instantiation: nothing else ever sees a parameter.\npub fn substitute(t: &Type, map: &HashMap<String, Type>) -> Type {\n    match t {\n        Type::Param(p) => map\n            .get(p)\n            .cloned()\n            .unwrap_or_else(|| unreachable!(\"substitute runs only once every param is bound\")),\n        Type::Vec(e) => Type::Vec(Box::new(substitute(e, map))),\n        Type::Stream(e) => Type::Stream(Box::new(substitute(e, map))),\n        Type::Record(fields) => Type::Record(\n            fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(n, t)| (n.clone(), substitute(t, map)))\n                .collect(),\n        ),\n        Type::Enum {\n            name,\n            args,\n            variants,\n        } => Type::Enum {\n            name: name.clone(),\n            args: args.iter().map(|a| substitute(a, map)).collect(),\n            variants: variants\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(n, p)| (n.clone(), p.as_ref().map(|p| substitute(p, map))))\n                .collect(),\n        },\n        Type::Str | Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool | Type::Char => t.clone(),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `name`'s variant list at `args`, re-derived from the registry rather than trusted from\n/// whichever `Type::Enum` a caller happened to be holding. One layer deep: a payload that is\n/// itself the same enum comes back carrying the placeholder in turn, so asking again is what\n/// descends, and nothing tries to build a list that has no bottom.\npub fn variants_of(enums: &Enums, name: &str, args: &[Type]) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n    let Type::Enum {\n        args: template_args,\n        variants,\n        ..\n    } = &enums[name]\n    else {\n        unreachable!(\"the registry holds enum types\")\n    };\n    if template_args.is_empty() {\n        return variants.clone();\n    }\n    let bindings: HashMap<String, Type> = template_args\n        .iter()\n        .zip(args)\n        .filter_map(|(p, a)| match p {\n            Type::Param(p) => Some((p.clone(), a.clone())),\n            _ => None,\n        })\n        .collect();\n    variants\n        .iter()\n        .map(|(n, p)| (n.clone(), p.as_ref().map(|t| substitute(t, &bindings))))\n        .collect()\n}\n\n/// `variants_of` for an enum type in hand.\npub fn variants(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n    let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = ty else {\n        unreachable!(\"only an enum has variants\")\n    };\n    variants_of(enums, name, args)\n}\n\n/// Whether a value of this enum type can hold another of the same type, however deep.\n///\n/// Always through a `Vec`, since that is the only self-reference the checker allows, but not\n/// always directly: the path can run through a record field or a second enum. What it decides\n/// is whether a backend may expand this type inline -- a printer, a parser, a declaration\n/// written by walking the payloads -- or has to emit a named function it can call back into.\n/// Expanding a recursive one inline does not terminate.\npub fn is_recursive(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n    fn reaches(enums: &Enums, from: &Type, target: &Type, seen: &mut Vec<Type>) -> bool {\n        match from {\n            Type::Vec(e) | Type::Stream(e) => reaches(enums, e, target, seen),\n            Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| reaches(enums, t, target, seen)),\n            Type::Enum { name, args, .. } => {\n                if from == target {\n                    return true;\n                }\n                if seen.contains(from) {\n                    return false;\n                }\n                seen.push(from.clone());\n                args.iter().any(|a| reaches(enums, a, target, seen))\n                    || variants_of(enums, name, args)\n                        .iter()\n                        .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| reaches(enums, p, target, seen)))\n            }\n            _ => false,\n        }\n    }\n    if !matches!(ty, Type::Enum { .. }) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    let mut seen = vec![ty.clone()];\n    variants(enums, ty)\n        .iter()\n        .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| reaches(enums, p, ty, &mut seen)))\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":451,"totalLines":451,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/ty.rs","time":{"start":1788120635985,"end":1788120635991}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me understand how Int is implemented end to end. The key files: src/ty.rs, src/parse.rs, src/tir.rs, src/check/*, and the emit_*.rs files. Let me look at how Int is done, since Float should mirror it.\n\nLet me first look at the type system.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0544b9915001k6HZCDBigUfXkp","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","messageID":"msg_0544b93510014vMX82BlagJ5Vn"}}
