r} -> r * r`.\n283:     /// The subject is not part of the node; a match reads `.` the way `select` does, so it\n284:     /// appears as a pipe stage.\n285:     Match {\n286:         arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n287:         span: Span,\n288:     },\n289:     /// `Shape.circle` or `Shape.circle{r: 1}`: a variant constructor spelled through its enum.\n290:     /// Only the qualified form needs its own node; a bare `circle{r: 1}` is an ordinary `Call`\n291:     /// and a bare `active` an ordinary `Var`, resolved through the enum registry by the checker.\n292:     Variant {\n293:         enum_name: String,\n294:         enum_span: Span,\n295:         variant: String,\n296:         variant_span: Span,\n297:         payload: Option<Box<Expr>>,\n298:         span: Span,\n299:     },\n300:     /// `lhs | rhs`, which binds `.` in `rhs` to the value of `lhs`.\n301:     Pipe {\n302:         lhs: Box<Expr>,\n303:         rhs: Box<Expr>,\n304:         span: Span,\n305:     },\n306:     Binary {\n307:         op: BinOp,\n308:         lhs: Box<Expr>,\n309:         rhs: Box<Expr>,\n310:         span: Span,\n311:     },\n312:     /// `lhs and rhs`, `lhs or rhs`. A separate node from `Binary` for the same reason `LogicOp`\n313:     /// is a separate enum: nothing about how these are typed or evaluated follows `Binary`'s\n314:     /// rules.\n315:     Logic {\n316:         op: LogicOp,\n317:         lhs: Box<Expr>,\n318:         rhs: Box<Expr>,\n319:         span: Span,\n320:     },\n321: }\n322: \n323: #[derive(Debug)]\n324: pub struct MatchArm {\n325:     pub pattern: Pattern,\n326:     pub body: Expr,\n327:     pub span: Span,\n328: }\n329: \n330: #[derive(Debug)]\n331: pub enum Pattern {\n332:     /// A variant name, optionally destructuring its payload's fields. Bare names inside the\n333:     /// braces bind fresh, and `rest` is the `..` marker that permits naming only some of them.\n334:     Variant {\n335:         name: String,\n336:         span: Span,\n337:         fields: Option<FieldsPattern>,\n338:     },\n339:     /// A Bool guard: the arm matches when the expression is true. `.` inside it (and inside\n340:     /// the arm's body) is still the chain's subject, unlike a variant arm, which rebinds it.\n341:     Guard(Expr),\n342:     /// The default arm: `any()`, or the bare trailing expression the parser desugars into one\n343:     /// (its body doubles as its span). Matches whatever is left.\n344:     Default { span: Span },\n345: }\n346: \n347: impl Pattern {\n348:     pub fn span(&self) -> Span {\n349:         match self {\n350:             Pattern::Variant { span, .. } | Pattern::Default { span } => *span,\n351:             Pattern::Guard(e) => e.span(),\n352:         }\n353:     }\n354: }\n355: \n356: #[derive(Debug)]\n357: pub struct FieldsPattern {\n358:     pub names: Vec<(String, Span)>,\n359:     pub rest: bool,\n360:     pub span: Span,\n361: }\n362: \n363: impl Expr {\n364:     pub fn span(&self) -> Span {\n365:         match self {\n366:             Expr::Str { span, .. }\n367:             | Expr::Int { span, .. }\n368:             | Expr::VecLit { span, .. }\n369:             | Expr::Subject { span }\n370:             | Expr::Var { span, .. }\n371:             | Expr::Call { span, .. }\n372:             | Expr::Project { span, .. }\n373:             | Expr::Index { span, .. }\n374:             | Expr::Unwrap { span, .. }\n375:             | Expr::RecordLit { span, .. }\n376:             | Expr::Neg { span, .. }\n377:             | Expr::Not { span, .. }\n378:             | Expr::Cond { span, .. }\n379:             | Expr::Field { span, .. }\n380:             | Expr::Input { span }\n381:             | Expr::Inputs { span }\n382:             | Expr::Lines { span }\n383:             | Expr::Variant { span, .. }\n384:             | Expr::Match { span, .. }\n385:             | Expr::Pipe { span, .. }\n386:             | Expr::Binary { span, .. }\n387:             | Expr::Logic { span, .. } => *span,\n388:         }\n389:     }\n390: }\n\n(End of file - total 390 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]\npub struct Span {\n    pub start: usize,\n    pub end: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Span {\n    pub fn new(start: usize, end: usize) -> Self {\n        Span { start, end }\n    }\n\n    pub fn to(self, other: Span) -> Span {\n        Span::new(self.start, other.end)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\npub enum BinOp {\n    Add,\n    Sub,","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/ast.rs","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]\npub struct Span {\n    pub start: usize,\n    pub end: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Span {\n    pub fn new(start: usize, end: usize) -> Self {\n        Span { start, end }\n    }\n\n    pub fn to(self, other: Span) -> Span {\n        Span::new(self.start, other.end)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\npub enum BinOp {\n    Add,\n    Sub,\n    Mul,\n    Div,\n    Rem,\n    Eq,\n    Ne,\n    Lt,\n    Le,\n    Gt,\n    Ge,\n}\n\nimpl BinOp {\n    pub fn is_comparison(self) -> bool {\n        matches!(\n            self,\n            BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne | BinOp::Lt | BinOp::Le | BinOp::Gt | BinOp::Ge\n        )\n    }\n\n    /// True for the operators that only ever mean arithmetic. `+` is missing because it also\n    /// concatenates, which is the one place an operator's meaning depends on its operands.\n    pub fn is_arithmetic(self) -> bool {\n        matches!(self, BinOp::Sub | BinOp::Mul | BinOp::Div | BinOp::Rem)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for BinOp {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        let s = match self {\n            BinOp::Add => \"+\",\n            BinOp::Sub => \"-\",\n            BinOp::Mul => \"*\",\n            BinOp::Div => \"/\",\n            BinOp::Rem => \"%\",\n            BinOp::Eq => \"==\",\n            BinOp::Ne => \"!=\",\n            BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n            BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n            BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n            BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n        };\n        write!(f, \"{s}\")\n    }\n}\n\n/// The two Bool connectives. Kept out of `BinOp` because they share none of its rules: their\n/// operands are Bool rather than a matched pair of anything, and they are the only operators\n/// that may leave their right side unevaluated.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\npub enum LogicOp {\n    And,\n    Or,\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for LogicOp {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        let s = match self {\n            LogicOp::And => \"and\",\n            LogicOp::Or => \"or\",\n        };\n        write!(f, \"{s}\")\n    }\n}\n\n/// A type as written in source, before it is resolved to a `Type`.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub enum TypeExpr {\n    Named {\n        name: String,\n        /// `Pair<Int>`: the arguments a declared generic enum is applied to. Empty for a\n        /// plain name. `Vec`/`Stream` keep their own variants below.\n        args: Vec<TypeExpr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    Vec {\n        elem: Box<TypeExpr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `Stream<T>`, legal only as the whole of a parameter or return annotation: a stream is\n    /// not a value, so the checker refuses it anywhere a type would describe something stored.\n    Stream {\n        elem: Box<TypeExpr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    Record {\n        fields: Vec<(String, TypeExpr)>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n}\n\nimpl TypeExpr {\n    pub fn span(&self) -> Span {\n        match self {\n            TypeExpr::Named { span, .. }\n            | TypeExpr::Vec { span, .. }\n            | TypeExpr::Stream { span, .. }\n            | TypeExpr::Record { span, .. } => *span,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Param {\n    pub name: String,\n    pub ty: TypeExpr,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Def {\n    pub name: String,\n    /// `None` for a nullary function (`fn name() -> T = body`).\n    pub param: Option<Param>,\n    pub ret: TypeExpr,\n    pub body: Expr,\n    pub span: Span,\n    /// Whether a module's prelude includes this definition when compiling a program. Meaningless\n    /// outside a module today, since nothing yet imports from a program file.\n    pub is_pub: bool,\n}\n\n/// `enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }`. The first declaration that creates a type identity\n/// rather than abbreviating one: the name is what exhaustiveness will be proved against.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct EnumDecl {\n    pub name: String,\n    /// Type parameters, in declaration order: `enum Opt<T> { ... }`. Capitalized names,\n    /// bound only inside this declaration's payloads.\n    pub params: Vec<(String, Span)>,\n    pub variants: Vec<Variant>,\n    pub span: Span,\n    /// Same meaning as `Def::is_pub`: whether a module exports this declaration.\n    pub is_pub: bool,\n}\n\n/// One alternative of an enum. Variant names are data (they appear as JSON keys and strings),\n/// so they are exempt from the capital-means-type casing rule, like record fields.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Variant {\n    pub name: String,\n    pub span: Span,\n    /// `None` for a unit variant. Any single type, spelled the way a call spells its argument:\n    /// a record type directly in braces (`circle{r: Int}`), any type in parens (`celsius(Int)`).\n    pub payload: Option<TypeExpr>,\n}\n\n/// Zero or more definitions followed by the expression that is the program.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct File {\n    /// `type Db = {users: Vec<User>}`. An abbreviation and nothing more: the name and what it\n    /// stands for are one type, so nothing distinguishes them once resolved.\n    pub aliases: Vec<Alias>,\n    pub enums: Vec<EnumDecl>,\n    pub defs: Vec<Def>,\n    pub body: Expr,\n}\n\n/// What a module file holds: declarations only, no trailing expression.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Module {\n    pub defs: Vec<Def>,\n    pub enums: Vec<EnumDecl>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Alias {\n    pub name: String,\n    pub ty: TypeExpr,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub enum Expr {\n    Str {\n        text: String,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    Int {\n        value: i64,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    VecLit {\n        items: Vec<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `{name: expr, age: expr}`. A record literal, the inverse of a projection. Each field\n    /// keeps its own span so a repeated one can be pointed at rather than described.\n    RecordLit {\n        fields: Vec<(String, Span, Expr)>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `.`, the value the enclosing pipeline or filter is currently working on.\n    Subject {\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    Var {\n        name: String,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    Call {\n        func: String,\n        func_span: Span,\n        /// `None` for a nullary call (`name()`).\n        arg: Option<Box<Expr>>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `base[]`. A spec that keeps a dimension.\n    Project {\n        base: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `base[i]`. A spec that collapses a dimension, so the entry may not be there.\n    Index {\n        base: Box<Expr>,\n        index: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `base!`. Insist the value is there, and stop the program if it is not.\n    Unwrap {\n        base: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `-base`.\n    Neg {\n        base: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `not base`. Looser than every comparison, so `not a == b` negates the comparison, and\n    /// tighter than `and`/`or`, so `not a and b` negates only `a`.\n    Not {\n        base: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `then if cond else otherwise`. An expression, in a language that has only those.\n    Cond {\n        then: Box<Expr>,\n        cond: Box<Expr>,\n        otherwise: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `base.name`. Distributes over a Vec rather than needing a map.\n    Field {\n        base: Box<Expr>,\n        name: String,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// The value read from stdin. It has no type of its own and can only be checked against an\n    /// expected one, which is the same rule the draft gives for lambdas.\n    Input {\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, collected eagerly into a `Vec<T>`.\n    /// Like `input`, its element type comes only from where it is used.\n    Inputs {\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// The stream of lines read from stdin, born `Stream<Str>`. The checker rejects a second\n    /// use rather than accepting a second stream, since there is only ever one real stdin.\n    Lines {\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// An `or` chain of match arms over the subject `.`: `point -> 0 or circle{r} -> r * r`.\n    /// The subject is not part of the node; a match reads `.` the way `select` does, so it\n    /// appears as a pipe stage.\n    Match {\n        arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `Shape.circle` or `Shape.circle{r: 1}`: a variant constructor spelled through its enum.\n    /// Only the qualified form needs its own node; a bare `circle{r: 1}` is an ordinary `Call`\n    /// and a bare `active` an ordinary `Var`, resolved through the enum registry by the checker.\n    Variant {\n        enum_name: String,\n        enum_span: Span,\n        variant: String,\n        variant_span: Span,\n        payload: Option<Box<Expr>>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `lhs | rhs`, which binds `.` in `rhs` to the value of `lhs`.\n    Pipe {\n        lhs: Box<Expr>,\n        rhs: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    Binary {\n        op: BinOp,\n        lhs: Box<Expr>,\n        rhs: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n    /// `lhs and rhs`, `lhs or rhs`. A separate node from `Binary` for the same reason `LogicOp`\n    /// is a separate enum: nothing about how these are typed or evaluated follows `Binary`'s\n    /// rules.\n    Logic {\n        op: LogicOp,\n        lhs: Box<Expr>,\n        rhs: Box<Expr>,\n        span: Span,\n    },\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct MatchArm {\n    pub pattern: Pattern,\n    pub body: Expr,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub enum Pattern {\n    /// A variant name, optionally destructuring its payload's fields. Bare names inside the\n    /// braces bind fresh, and `rest` is the `..` marker that permits naming only some of them.\n    Variant {\n        name: String,\n        span: Span,\n        fields: Option<FieldsPattern>,\n    },\n    /// A Bool guard: the arm matches when the expression is true. `.` inside it (and inside\n    /// the arm's body) is still the chain's subject, unlike a variant arm, which rebinds it.\n    Guard(Expr),\n    /// The default arm: `any()`, or the bare trailing expression the parser desugars into one\n    /// (its body doubles as its span). Matches whatever is left.\n    Default { span: Span },\n}\n\nimpl Pattern {\n    pub fn span(&self) -> Span {\n        match self {\n            Pattern::Variant { span, .. } | Pattern::Default { span } => *span,\n            Pattern::Guard(e) => e.span(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct FieldsPattern {\n    pub names: Vec<(String, Span)>,\n    pub rest: bool,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\nimpl Expr {\n    pub fn span(&self) -> Span {\n        match self {\n            Expr::Str { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Int { span, .. }\n            | Expr::VecLit { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Subject { span }\n            | Expr::Var { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Call { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Project { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Index { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Unwrap { span, .. }\n            | Expr::RecordLit { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Neg { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Not { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Cond { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Field { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Input { span }\n            | Expr::Inputs { span }\n            | Expr::Lines { span }\n            | Expr::Variant { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Match { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Pipe { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Binary { span, .. }\n            | Expr::Logic { span, .. } => *span,\n        }\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":390,"totalLines":390,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/ast.rs","time":{"start":1788120643773,"end":1788120643777}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_0544bb7c0001Cv7kZkNRbM1Ewn","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","messageID":"msg_0544bb0e0001HNGTCDPHJYIiWK"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788120643839,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"id":"prt_0544bb8fa001Murjy6mQVz44rd","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"94b6040ffbb3d9f05c4638216dc44bf3958c7c09","messageID":"msg_0544bb0e0001HNGTCDPHJYIiWK","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":51268,"input":7171,"output":65,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":44032}},"cost":0.001182327}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788120644793,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"id":"prt_0544bbc88001Pr6huMDnsNlmMb","messageID":"msg_0544bb90d001G1lzKb8OXLD5m9","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","snapshot":"94b6040ffbb3d9f05c4638216dc44bf3958c7c09","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120646620,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8ee65fed56e963ae","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/check/types.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/check/types.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! Type resolution: turns the surface syntax of types (`TypeExpr`, `EnumDecl`) into `ty::Type`.\n2: //! Runs once, eagerly, before any expression is checked, and touches neither `Expr` nor `Tir`.\n3: \n4: use std::collections::HashMap;\n5: \n6: use crate::ast::{Alias, Def, EnumDecl, Span, TypeExpr};\n7: use crate::error::Error;\n8: use crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n9: \n10: use super::BUILTIN_NAMES;\n11: \n12: /// Capitalised names are types and lowercase names are values.\n13: ///\n14: /// Every name in the language already followed this, unenforced. Making it a rule is what lets a\n15: /// constructor share a spelling with a call without sharing a namespace: `User {..}` builds and\n16: /// `area {..}` calls, decided by the first letter rather than by looking either one up.\n17: ///\n18: /// Field names are deliberately not covered. They come from data, and JSON objects are entitled\n19: /// to a key spelled `Name`.\n20: fn value_name(name: &str, span: Span, what: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n21:     if name.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n22:         return Err(Error::new(\n23:             span,\n24:             format!(\"a {what} starts with a lowercase letter, and `{name}` reads as a type\"),\n25:         ));\n26:     }\n27:     Ok(())\n28: }\n29: \n30: /// What a type name stands for. An alias is an abbreviation, so this maps to the written form\n31: /// and `resolve` expands it; nothing downstream ever learns a name was involved.\n32: type Aliases<'a> = HashMap<String, &'a TypeExpr>;\n33: \n34: /// The named types a written annotation can refer to. Aliases expand away; an enum resolves to\n35: /// the identity its declaration created.\n36: pub(super) struct TypeEnv<'a> {\n37:     pub(super) aliases: Aliases<'a>,\n38:     pub(super) enums: HashMap<String, &'a EnumDecl>,\n39: }\n40: \n41: pub(super) fn enum_map(enums: &[EnumDecl]) -> Result<HashMap<String, &EnumDecl>, Error> {\n42:     let mut map: HashMap<String, &EnumDecl> = HashMap::new();\n43:     for e in enums {\n44:         if !e.name.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n45:             return Err(Error::new(\n46:                 e.span,\n47:                 format!(\n48:                     \"a type name starts with a capital letter, and `{}` reads as a value\",\n49:                     e.name\n50:                 ),\n51:             ));\n52:         }\n53:         if ty::is_builtin_type_name(&e.name) {\n54:             return Err(Error::new(\n55:                 e.span,\n56:                 format!(\"`{}` is a built-in type and cannot be redefined\", e.name),\n57:             ));\n58:         }\n59:         for (i, v) in e.variants.iter().enumerate() {\n60:             if e.variants[..i].iter().any(|earlier| earlier.name == v.name) {\n61:                 return Err(Error::new(\n62:                     v.span,\n63:                     format!(\"variant `{}` is declared twice in `{}`\", v.name, e.name),\n64:                 ));\n65:             }\n66:         }\n67:         if map.insert(e.name.clone(), e).is_some() {\n68:             return Err(Error::new(\n69:                 e.span,\n70:                 format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n71:             ));\n72:         }\n73:     }\n74:     Ok(map)\n75: }\n76: \n77: /// Resolve one enum declaration to its type. `seen` carries the names being expanded and the\n78: /// arguments each was expanded with, exactly as for aliases (whose entry carries no arguments),\n79: /// so an enum whose payload mentions itself is refused rather than expanded forever -- unless it\n80: /// does so behind a `Vec`, which `resolve_named` treats as legal (kantord/toylang#76) and stops\n81: /// expanding at, rather than looping. The arguments travel alongside the name so that a boxed\n82: /// self-reference can be checked against the instantiation it reappears inside\n83: /// (kantord/toylang#117): reusing them is the only way `resolve_named` avoids expanding one\n84: /// forever, so a self-reference that reappears with different arguments -- `Nest<Vec<T>>` inside\n85: /// `Nest<T>` -- has no honest placeholder to return and is refused instead.\n86: ///\n87: /// `args` instantiates a generic declaration: payloads resolve with each parameter bound to\n88: /// its argument, already resolved (an argument never re-enters this enum's `seen` chain, so\n89: /// `Opt<Opt<Int>>` is two honest levels, not a false cycle). `None` builds the registry\n90: /// template instead, each parameter standing for itself as `Type::Param` -- which is also the\n91: /// eager pass that validates a declaration nothing uses.\n92: pub(super) fn resolve_enum(\n93:     decl: &EnumDecl,\n94:     env: &TypeEnv,\n95:     seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n96:     args: Option<&[Type]>,\n97: ) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n98:     for (i, (p, span)) in decl.params.iter().enumerate() {\n99:         if !p.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n100:             return Err(Error::new(\n101:                 *span,\n102:                 format!(\n103:                     \"a type parameter starts with a capital letter, and `{p}` reads as a value\"\n104:                 ),\n105:             ));\n106:         }\n107:         // A parameter may shadow a declared enum or alias -- resolve_named consults the\n108:         // parameter bindings first, so inside the declaration the name means the parameter,\n109:         // unambiguously, the same scoping Rust gives struct Foo<E> beside an enum E. Refusing\n110:         // the collision instead broke every program declaring enum E the moment the prelude\n111:         // gained Result<T, E> (kantord/toylang#85). Builtins stay off limits: Vec-the-parameter\n112:         // would make every Vec<...> in the payload mean the wrong thing at a distance.\n113:         if ty::is_builtin_type_name(p) {\n114:             return Err(Error::new(\n115:                 *span,\n116:                 format!(\"type parameter `{p}` takes the name of a built-in type\"),\n117:             ));\n118:         }\n119:         if decl.params[..i].iter().any(|(earlier, _)| earlier == p) {\n120:             return Err(Error::new(\n121:                 *span,\n122:                 format!(\"type parameter `{p}` is declared twice in `{}`\", decl.name),\n123:             ));\n124:         }\n125:     }\n126:     let bound: Vec<Type> = match args {\n127:         Some(args) => args.to_vec(),\n128:         None => decl\n129:             .params\n130:             .iter()\n131:             .map(|(p, _)| Type::Param(p.clone()))\n132:             .collect(),\n133:     };\n134:     let params: HashMap<&str, &Type> = decl\n135:         .params\n136:         .iter()\n137:         .map(|(p, _)| p.as_str())\n138:         .zip(bound.iter())\n139:         .collect();\n140:     seen.push((decl.name.clone(), bound.clone()));\n141:     let mut variants = Vec::new();\n142:     for v in &decl.variants {\n143:         let payload = match &v.payload {\n144:             Some(ty) => {\n145:                 // `boxed` starts false at the top of every variant: whether this one payload\n146:                 // reaches back to `decl.name` through a `Vec` is assessed fresh each time, so\n147:                 // `enum E { safe(Vec<E>), bad(E) }` accepts `safe` and still refuses `bad`.\n148:                 let resolved = resolve_bound(ty, env, seen, &params, false)?;\n149:                 // The record and Vec spellings refuse a stream inside themselves, but the\n150:                 // parens spelling can put one directly in payload position, so the ban has to\n151:                 // be stated here too: an enum value is storable, and a stream is not.\n152:                 if resolved.contains_stream() {\n153:                     return Err(Error::new(\n154:                         ty.span(),\n155:                         format!(\n156:                             \"the payload of `{}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\",\n157:                             v.name\n158:                         ),\n159:                     ));\n160:                 }\n161:                 Some(resolved)\n162:             }\n163:             None => None,\n164:         };\n165:         variants.push((v.name.clone(), payload));\n166:     }\n167:     seen.pop();\n168:     Ok(Type::Enum {\n169:         name: decl.name.clone(),\n170:         args: bound,\n171:         variants,\n172:     })\n173: }\n174: \n175: pub(super) fn alias_map(aliases: &[Alias]) -> Result<Aliases<'_>, Error> {\n176:     let mut map: Aliases = HashMap::new();\n177:     for a in aliases {\n178:         if !a.name.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n179:             return Err(Error::new(\n180:                 a.span,\n181:                 format!(\n182:                     \"a type name starts with a capital letter, and `{}` reads as a value\",\n183:                     a.name\n184:                 ),\n185:             ));\n186:         }\n187:         if ty::is_builtin_type_name(&a.name) {\n188:             return Err(Error::new(\n189:                 a.span,\n190:                 format!(\"`{}` is a built-in type and cannot be redefined\", a.name),\n191:             ));\n192:         }\n193:         if map.insert(a.name.clone(), &a.ty).is_some() {\n194:             return Err(Error::new(\n195:                 a.span,\n196:                 format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", a.name),\n197:             ));\n198:         }\n199:     }\n200:     Ok(map)\n201: }\n202: \n203: pub(super) fn signatures(defs: &[Def], env: &TypeEnv) -> Result<HashMap<String, Sig>, Error> {\n204:     let mut sigs = HashMap::new();\n205:     for def in defs {\n206:         value_name(&def.name, def.span, \"function name\")?;\n207:         if let Some(param) = &def.param {\n208:             value_name(&param.name, param.span, \"parameter name\")?;\n209:         }\n210:         if BUILTIN_NAMES.contains(&def.name.as_str()) {\n211:             return Err(Error::new(\n212:                 def.span,\n213:                 format!(\"`{}` is a builtin and cannot be redefined\", def.name),\n214:             ));\n215:         }\n216:         if sigs.contains_key(&def.name) {\n217:             return Err(Error::new(\n218:                 def.span,\n219:                 format!(\"`{}` is defined twice\", def.name),\n220:             ));\n221:         }\n222:         let sig = Sig {\n223:             param: match &def.param {\n224:                 Some(param) => Some(resolve(&param.ty, env, &mut Vec::new())?),\n225:                 None => None,\n226:             },\n227:             ret: resolve(&def.ret, env, &mut Vec::new())?,\n228:         };\n229:         // A stream is born only at a source, so a function cannot conjure one: a stream result\n230:         // flows in through a stream parameter, and the pipeline stays one chain fusion can\n231:         // read. Refusing is the reversible direction.\n232:         if matches!(sig.ret, Type::Stream(_)) && !matches!(sig.param, Some(Type::Stream(_))) {\n233:             return Err(Error::new(\n234:                 def.span,\n235:                 format!(\n236:                     \"`{}` returns {} without taking a stream; a stream is born only at a source\",\n237:                     def.name, sig.ret\n238:                 ),\n239:             ));\n240:         }\n241:         sigs.insert(def.name.clone(), sig);\n242:     }\n243:     Ok(sigs)\n244: }\n245: \n246: /// `seen` is the chain of names currently being expanded, each with the arguments it was\n247: /// expanded with -- aliases and enums share it -- so a type written in terms of itself is\n248: /// caught rather than expanded forever.\n249: pub(super) fn resolve(\n250:     ty: &TypeExpr,\n251:     env: &TypeEnv,\n252:     seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n253: ) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n254:     resolve_bound(ty, env, seen, &HashMap::new(), false)\n255: }\n256: \n257: /// The arity-checked, resolved type arguments for `name`'s `<...>`, shared between an ordinary\n258: /// instantiation and a self-reference caught below -- both need the same check against\n259: /// `decl.params.len()`, one on the way to expanding the declaration and the other on the way to\n260: /// a placeholder for it. An argument is a fresh type position, not a continuation of whatever\n261: /// `Vec` this occurrence of `name` itself sat behind, so it resolves with `boxed` reset to\n262: /// `false` rather than threading the caller's through.\n263: fn resolve_args(\n264:     name: &str,\n265:     args: &[TypeExpr],\n266:     decl_params: usize,\n267:     span: Span,\n268:     env: &TypeEnv,\n269:     seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n270:     params: &HashMap<&str, &Type>,\n271: ) -> Result<Vec<Type>, Error> {\n272:     if args.len() != decl_params {\n273:         let wants = match decl_params {\n274:             0 => format!(\"`{name}` takes no type argument\"),\n275:             1 => format!(\"`{name}` takes one type argument\"),\n276:             n => format!(\"`{name}` takes {n} type arguments\"),\n277:         };\n278:         let found = match args.len() {\n279:             0 => String::new(),\n280:             n => format!(\", found {n}\"),\n281:         };\n282:         return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"{wants}{found}\")));\n283:     }\n284:     let mut resolved_args = Vec::new();\n285:     for arg in args {\n286:         let resolved = resolve_bound(arg, env, seen, params, false)?;\n287:         if resolved.contains_stream() {\n288:             return Err(Error::new(\n289:                 arg.span(),\n290:                 format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\"),\n291:             ));\n292:         }\n293:         resolved_args.push(resolved);\n294:     }\n295:     Ok(resolved_args)\n296: }\n297: \n298: /// One named reference: a bound parameter, a built-in scalar, an alias, or an enum -- with\n299: /// the arity of its `<...>` arguments held here, where the declaration is in hand.\n300: ///\n301: /// `boxed` is whether this occurrence has already passed through a `Vec` since the innermost\n302: /// enum currently being expanded (`seen`'s top) started -- reset at the top of each variant's\n303: /// payload by `resolve_enum`, set by the `Vec` arm below, and otherwise carried through\n304: /// unchanged, because once a value's storage is a heap indirection every field nested inside it\n305: /// stays indirect too.\n306: fn resolve_named(\n307:     name: &str,\n308:     args: &[TypeExpr],\n309:     span: Span,\n310:     env: &TypeEnv,\n311:     seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n312:     params: &HashMap<&str, &Type>,\n313:     boxed: bool,\n314: ) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n315:     if let Some(bound) = params.get(name) {\n316:         if !args.is_empty() {\n317:             return Err(Error::new(\n318:                 span,\n319:                 format!(\"`{name}` is a type parameter and takes no type argument\"),\n320:             ));\n321:         }\n322:         return Ok((*bound).clone());\n323:     }\n324:     if let Some(built_in) = Type::from_name(name) {\n325:         if !args.is_empty() {\n326:             return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"`{name}` takes no type argument\")));\n327:         }\n328:         return Ok(built_in);\n329:     }\n330:     if let Some(at) = seen.iter().position(|(s, _)| s == name) {\n331:         // Behind a `Vec`, a self-reference is a heap indirection rather than an infinite\n332:         // layout, the same reason `Vec<Json>` is an ordinary field and not a contradiction\n333:         // (kantord/toylang#76: Json's array case). Nothing downstream can expand `name`'s own\n334:         // variants without looping forever -- `name` is still on `seen` -- so this stops one\n335:         // layer short and returns a placeholder instead; whichever consumer actually needs the\n336:         // variants (`check::enum_variants`) re-derives them from the registry, one layer at a\n337:         // time, exactly as deep as the program itself ever navigates.\n338:         if boxed {\n339:             let decl_params = env.enums.get(name).map_or(0, |d| d.params.len());\n340:             let resolved_args = resolve_args(name, args, decl_params, span, env, seen, params)?;\n341:             // The placeholder above stands in for `name`'s own variant list, re-derived later\n342:             // from the registry at whatever arguments this occurrence carries. That only works\n343:             // if those arguments are the ones already being expanded: `Nest<T>` referring to\n344:             // itself as `Nest<Vec<T>>` names a second, larger instantiation the registry never\n345:             // builds, and re-deriving from it recurses one `Vec` deeper every time -- forever,\n346:             // since no two of `Nest<T>`, `Nest<Vec<T>>`, `Nest<Vec<Vec<T>>>`, ... ever coincide\n347:             // (kantord/toylang#117). Requiring an exact repeat closes that off: a self-reference\n348:             // is a cycle back to the instantiation already in progress, not a new one.\n349:             if resolved_args != seen[at].1 {\n350:                 let expected = Type::Enum {\n351:                     name: name.to_string(),\n352:                     args: seen[at].1.clone(),\n353:                     variants: Vec::new(),\n354:                 };\n355:                 return Err(Error::new(\n356:                     span,\n357:                     format!(\n358:                         \"`{name}` refers to itself with different type arguments; a \\\n359:                          self-reference must repeat `{expected}` unchanged\"\n360:                     ),\n361:                 ));\n362:             }\n363:             return Ok(Type::Enum {\n364:                 name: name.to_string(),\n365:                 args: resolved_args,\n366:                 variants: Vec::new(),\n367:             });\n368:         }\n369:         // The names expanded since this one last appeared are the cycle, and naming them\n370:         // is the difference between knowing there is one and finding it.\n371:         let through: Vec<String> = seen[at + 1..]\n372:             .iter()\n373:             .map(|(s, _)| format!(\"`{s}`\"))\n374:             .collect();\n375:         let path = if through.is_empty() {\n376:             String::new()\n377:         } else {\n378:             format!(\", through {}\", through.join(\" and \"))\n379:         };\n380:         return Err(Error::new(\n381:             span,\n382:             format!(\"type `{name}` is written in terms of itself{path}\"),\n383:         ));\n384:     }\n385:     if let Some(written) = env.aliases.get(name) {\n386:         if !args.is_empty() {\n387:             return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"`{name}` takes no type argument\")));\n388:         }\n389:         seen.push((name.to_string(), Vec::new()));\n390:         let expanded = resolve_bound(written, env, seen, params, boxed);\n391:         seen.pop();\n392:         return expanded;\n393:     }\n394:     if let Some(decl) = env.enums.get(name) {\n395:         let resolved_args = resolve_args(name, args, decl.params.len(), span, env, seen, params)?;\n396:         return resolve_enum(decl, env, seen, Some(&resolved_args));\n397:     }\n398:     Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"unknown type `{name}`\")))\n399: }\n400: \n401: /// `resolve` with a generic enum's parameters in scope: inside `enum Opt<T>`'s payloads,\n402: /// `T` names whatever the binding holds -- the argument at an instantiation, or the\n403: /// parameter itself in the registry template. Everywhere else the binding map is empty.\n404: fn resolve_bound(\n405:     ty: &TypeExpr,\n406:     env: &TypeEnv,\n407:     seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n408:     params: &HashMap<&str, &Type>,\n409:     boxed: bool,\n410: ) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n411:     match ty {\n412:         TypeExpr::Named { name, args, span } => {\n413:             resolve_named(name, args, *span, env, seen, params, boxed)\n414:         }\n415:         // The containment bans hold in the grammar itself, not just at value construction\n416:         // sites: a stream is not a value, so no annotation may describe one as stored.\n417:         TypeExpr::Vec { elem, .. } => {\n418:             let inner = resolve_bound(elem, env, seen, params, true)?;\n419:             if inner.contains_stream() {\n420:                 return Err(Error::new(\n421:                     elem.span(),\n422:                     \"a Vec cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\".to_string(),\n423:                 ));\n424:             }\n425:             Ok(Type::Vec(Box::new(inner)))\n426:         }\n427:         TypeExpr::Stream { elem, .. } => {\n428:             let inner = resolve_bound(elem, env, seen, params, boxed)?;\n429:             if inner.contains_stream() {\n430:                 return Err(Error::new(\n431:                     elem.span(),\n432:                     \"a Stream cannot hold another stream; there is nothing it could yield\"\n433:                         .to_string(),\n434:                 ));\n435:             }\n436:             Ok(Type::Stream(Box::new(inner)))\n437:         }\n438:         TypeExpr::Record { fields, span } => {\n439:             let mut out = Vec::new();\n440:             for (name, ty) in fields {\n441:                 if out.iter().any(|(n, _): &(String, Type)| n == name) {\n442:                     return Err(Error::new(\n443:                         *span,\n444:                         format!(\"field `{name}` is declared twice\"),\n445:                     ));\n446:                 }\n447:                 let field = resolve_bound(ty, env, seen, params, boxed)?;\n448:                 if field.contains_stream() {\n449:                     return Err(Error::new(\n450:                         ty.span(),\n451:                         format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\"),\n452:                     ));\n453:                 }\n454:                 out.push((name.clone(), field));\n455:             }\n456:             Ok(Type::Record(out))\n457:         }\n458:     }\n459: }\n\n(End of file - total 459 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! Type resolution: turns the surface syntax of types (`TypeExpr`, `EnumDecl`) into `ty::Type`.\n//! Runs once, eagerly, before any expression is checked, and touches neither `Expr` nor `Tir`.\n\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\n\nuse crate::ast::{Alias, Def, EnumDecl, Span, TypeExpr};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nuse super::BUILTIN_NAMES;\n\n/// Capitalised names are types and lowercase names are values.\n///\n/// Every name in the language already followed this, unenforced. Making it a rule is what lets a\n/// constructor share a spelling with a call without sharing a namespace: `User {..}` builds and\n/// `area {..}` calls, decided by the first letter rather than by looking either one up.\n///\n/// Field names are deliberately not covered. They come from data, and JSON objects are entitled\n/// to a key spelled `Name`.\nfn value_name(name: &str, span: Span, what: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/check/types.rs","text":"//! Type resolution: turns the surface syntax of types (`TypeExpr`, `EnumDecl`) into `ty::Type`.\n//! Runs once, eagerly, before any expression is checked, and touches neither `Expr` nor `Tir`.\n\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\n\nuse crate::ast::{Alias, Def, EnumDecl, Span, TypeExpr};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nuse super::BUILTIN_NAMES;\n\n/// Capitalised names are types and lowercase names are values.\n///\n/// Every name in the language already followed this, unenforced. Making it a rule is what lets a\n/// constructor share a spelling with a call without sharing a namespace: `User {..}` builds and\n/// `area {..}` calls, decided by the first letter rather than by looking either one up.\n///\n/// Field names are deliberately not covered. They come from data, and JSON objects are entitled\n/// to a key spelled `Name`.\nfn value_name(name: &str, span: Span, what: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    if name.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\"a {what} starts with a lowercase letter, and `{name}` reads as a type\"),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// What a type name stands for. An alias is an abbreviation, so this maps to the written form\n/// and `resolve` expands it; nothing downstream ever learns a name was involved.\ntype Aliases<'a> = HashMap<String, &'a TypeExpr>;\n\n/// The named types a written annotation can refer to. Aliases expand away; an enum resolves to\n/// the identity its declaration created.\npub(super) struct TypeEnv<'a> {\n    pub(super) aliases: Aliases<'a>,\n    pub(super) enums: HashMap<String, &'a EnumDecl>,\n}\n\npub(super) fn enum_map(enums: &[EnumDecl]) -> Result<HashMap<String, &EnumDecl>, Error> {\n    let mut map: HashMap<String, &EnumDecl> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in enums {\n        if !e.name.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                e.span,\n                format!(\n                    \"a type name starts with a capital letter, and `{}` reads as a value\",\n                    e.name\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        if ty::is_builtin_type_name(&e.name) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                e.span,\n                format!(\"`{}` is a built-in type and cannot be redefined\", e.name),\n            ));\n        }\n        for (i, v) in e.variants.iter().enumerate() {\n            if e.variants[..i].iter().any(|earlier| earlier.name == v.name) {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    v.span,\n                    format!(\"variant `{}` is declared twice in `{}`\", v.name, e.name),\n                ));\n            }\n        }\n        if map.insert(e.name.clone(), e).is_some() {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                e.span,\n                format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(map)\n}\n\n/// Resolve one enum declaration to its type. `seen` carries the names being expanded and the\n/// arguments each was expanded with, exactly as for aliases (whose entry carries no arguments),\n/// so an enum whose payload mentions itself is refused rather than expanded forever -- unless it\n/// does so behind a `Vec`, which `resolve_named` treats as legal (kantord/toylang#76) and stops\n/// expanding at, rather than looping. The arguments travel alongside the name so that a boxed\n/// self-reference can be checked against the instantiation it reappears inside\n/// (kantord/toylang#117): reusing them is the only way `resolve_named` avoids expanding one\n/// forever, so a self-reference that reappears with different arguments -- `Nest<Vec<T>>` inside\n/// `Nest<T>` -- has no honest placeholder to return and is refused instead.\n///\n/// `args` instantiates a generic declaration: payloads resolve with each parameter bound to\n/// its argument, already resolved (an argument never re-enters this enum's `seen` chain, so\n/// `Opt<Opt<Int>>` is two honest levels, not a false cycle). `None` builds the registry\n/// template instead, each parameter standing for itself as `Type::Param` -- which is also the\n/// eager pass that validates a declaration nothing uses.\npub(super) fn resolve_enum(\n    decl: &EnumDecl,\n    env: &TypeEnv,\n    seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n    args: Option<&[Type]>,\n) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n    for (i, (p, span)) in decl.params.iter().enumerate() {\n        if !p.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                *span,\n                format!(\n                    \"a type parameter starts with a capital letter, and `{p}` reads as a value\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        // A parameter may shadow a declared enum or alias -- resolve_named consults the\n        // parameter bindings first, so inside the declaration the name means the parameter,\n        // unambiguously, the same scoping Rust gives struct Foo<E> beside an enum E. Refusing\n        // the collision instead broke every program declaring enum E the moment the prelude\n        // gained Result<T, E> (kantord/toylang#85). Builtins stay off limits: Vec-the-parameter\n        // would make every Vec<...> in the payload mean the wrong thing at a distance.\n        if ty::is_builtin_type_name(p) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                *span,\n                format!(\"type parameter `{p}` takes the name of a built-in type\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        if decl.params[..i].iter().any(|(earlier, _)| earlier == p) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                *span,\n                format!(\"type parameter `{p}` is declared twice in `{}`\", decl.name),\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    let bound: Vec<Type> = match args {\n        Some(args) => args.to_vec(),\n        None => decl\n            .params\n            .iter()\n            .map(|(p, _)| Type::Param(p.clone()))\n            .collect(),\n    };\n    let params: HashMap<&str, &Type> = decl\n        .params\n        .iter()\n        .map(|(p, _)| p.as_str())\n        .zip(bound.iter())\n        .collect();\n    seen.push((decl.name.clone(), bound.clone()));\n    let mut variants = Vec::new();\n    for v in &decl.variants {\n        let payload = match &v.payload {\n            Some(ty) => {\n                // `boxed` starts false at the top of every variant: whether this one payload\n                // reaches back to `decl.name` through a `Vec` is assessed fresh each time, so\n                // `enum E { safe(Vec<E>), bad(E) }` accepts `safe` and still refuses `bad`.\n                let resolved = resolve_bound(ty, env, seen, &params, false)?;\n                // The record and Vec spellings refuse a stream inside themselves, but the\n                // parens spelling can put one directly in payload position, so the ban has to\n                // be stated here too: an enum value is storable, and a stream is not.\n                if resolved.contains_stream() {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        ty.span(),\n                        format!(\n                            \"the payload of `{}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\",\n                            v.name\n                        ),\n                    ));\n                }\n                Some(resolved)\n            }\n            None => None,\n        };\n        variants.push((v.name.clone(), payload));\n    }\n    seen.pop();\n    Ok(Type::Enum {\n        name: decl.name.clone(),\n        args: bound,\n        variants,\n    })\n}\n\npub(super) fn alias_map(aliases: &[Alias]) -> Result<Aliases<'_>, Error> {\n    let mut map: Aliases = HashMap::new();\n    for a in aliases {\n        if !a.name.chars().next().is_some_and(char::is_uppercase) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                a.span,\n                format!(\n                    \"a type name starts with a capital letter, and `{}` reads as a value\",\n                    a.name\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        if ty::is_builtin_type_name(&a.name) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                a.span,\n                format!(\"`{}` is a built-in type and cannot be redefined\", a.name),\n            ));\n        }\n        if map.insert(a.name.clone(), &a.ty).is_some() {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                a.span,\n                format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", a.name),\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(map)\n}\n\npub(super) fn signatures(defs: &[Def], env: &TypeEnv) -> Result<HashMap<String, Sig>, Error> {\n    let mut sigs = HashMap::new();\n    for def in defs {\n        value_name(&def.name, def.span, \"function name\")?;\n        if let Some(param) = &def.param {\n            value_name(&param.name, param.span, \"parameter name\")?;\n        }\n        if BUILTIN_NAMES.contains(&def.name.as_str()) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                def.span,\n                format!(\"`{}` is a builtin and cannot be redefined\", def.name),\n            ));\n        }\n        if sigs.contains_key(&def.name) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                def.span,\n                format!(\"`{}` is defined twice\", def.name),\n            ));\n        }\n        let sig = Sig {\n            param: match &def.param {\n                Some(param) => Some(resolve(&param.ty, env, &mut Vec::new())?),\n                None => None,\n            },\n            ret: resolve(&def.ret, env, &mut Vec::new())?,\n        };\n        // A stream is born only at a source, so a function cannot conjure one: a stream result\n        // flows in through a stream parameter, and the pipeline stays one chain fusion can\n        // read. Refusing is the reversible direction.\n        if matches!(sig.ret, Type::Stream(_)) && !matches!(sig.param, Some(Type::Stream(_))) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                def.span,\n                format!(\n                    \"`{}` returns {} without taking a stream; a stream is born only at a source\",\n                    def.name, sig.ret\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        sigs.insert(def.name.clone(), sig);\n    }\n    Ok(sigs)\n}\n\n/// `seen` is the chain of names currently being expanded, each with the arguments it was\n/// expanded with -- aliases and enums share it -- so a type written in terms of itself is\n/// caught rather than expanded forever.\npub(super) fn resolve(\n    ty: &TypeExpr,\n    env: &TypeEnv,\n    seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n    resolve_bound(ty, env, seen, &HashMap::new(), false)\n}\n\n/// The arity-checked, resolved type arguments for `name`'s `<...>`, shared between an ordinary\n/// instantiation and a self-reference caught below -- both need the same check against\n/// `decl.params.len()`, one on the way to expanding the declaration and the other on the way to\n/// a placeholder for it. An argument is a fresh type position, not a continuation of whatever\n/// `Vec` this occurrence of `name` itself sat behind, so it resolves with `boxed` reset to\n/// `false` rather than threading the caller's through.\nfn resolve_args(\n    name: &str,\n    args: &[TypeExpr],\n    decl_params: usize,\n    span: Span,\n    env: &TypeEnv,\n    seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n    params: &HashMap<&str, &Type>,\n) -> Result<Vec<Type>, Error> {\n    if args.len() != decl_params {\n        let wants = match decl_params {\n            0 => format!(\"`{name}` takes no type argument\"),\n            1 => format!(\"`{name}` takes one type argument\"),\n            n => format!(\"`{name}` takes {n} type arguments\"),\n        };\n        let found = match args.len() {\n            0 => String::new(),\n            n => format!(\", found {n}\"),\n        };\n        return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"{wants}{found}\")));\n    }\n    let mut resolved_args = Vec::new();\n    for arg in args {\n        let resolved = resolve_bound(arg, env, seen, params, false)?;\n        if resolved.contains_stream() {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                arg.span(),\n                format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        resolved_args.push(resolved);\n    }\n    Ok(resolved_args)\n}\n\n/// One named reference: a bound parameter, a built-in scalar, an alias, or an enum -- with\n/// the arity of its `<...>` arguments held here, where the declaration is in hand.\n///\n/// `boxed` is whether this occurrence has already passed through a `Vec` since the innermost\n/// enum currently being expanded (`seen`'s top) started -- reset at the top of each variant's\n/// payload by `resolve_enum`, set by the `Vec` arm below, and otherwise carried through\n/// unchanged, because once a value's storage is a heap indirection every field nested inside it\n/// stays indirect too.\nfn resolve_named(\n    name: &str,\n    args: &[TypeExpr],\n    span: Span,\n    env: &TypeEnv,\n    seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n    params: &HashMap<&str, &Type>,\n    boxed: bool,\n) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n    if let Some(bound) = params.get(name) {\n        if !args.is_empty() {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                span,\n                format!(\"`{name}` is a type parameter and takes no type argument\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        return Ok((*bound).clone());\n    }\n    if let Some(built_in) = Type::from_name(name) {\n        if !args.is_empty() {\n            return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"`{name}` takes no type argument\")));\n        }\n        return Ok(built_in);\n    }\n    if let Some(at) = seen.iter().position(|(s, _)| s == name) {\n        // Behind a `Vec`, a self-reference is a heap indirection rather than an infinite\n        // layout, the same reason `Vec<Json>` is an ordinary field and not a contradiction\n        // (kantord/toylang#76: Json's array case). Nothing downstream can expand `name`'s own\n        // variants without looping forever -- `name` is still on `seen` -- so this stops one\n        // layer short and returns a placeholder instead; whichever consumer actually needs the\n        // variants (`check::enum_variants`) re-derives them from the registry, one layer at a\n        // time, exactly as deep as the program itself ever navigates.\n        if boxed {\n            let decl_params = env.enums.get(name).map_or(0, |d| d.params.len());\n            let resolved_args = resolve_args(name, args, decl_params, span, env, seen, params)?;\n            // The placeholder above stands in for `name`'s own variant list, re-derived later\n            // from the registry at whatever arguments this occurrence carries. That only works\n            // if those arguments are the ones already being expanded: `Nest<T>` referring to\n            // itself as `Nest<Vec<T>>` names a second, larger instantiation the registry never\n            // builds, and re-deriving from it recurses one `Vec` deeper every time -- forever,\n            // since no two of `Nest<T>`, `Nest<Vec<T>>`, `Nest<Vec<Vec<T>>>`, ... ever coincide\n            // (kantord/toylang#117). Requiring an exact repeat closes that off: a self-reference\n            // is a cycle back to the instantiation already in progress, not a new one.\n            if resolved_args != seen[at].1 {\n                let expected = Type::Enum {\n                    name: name.to_string(),\n                    args: seen[at].1.clone(),\n                    variants: Vec::new(),\n                };\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    span,\n                    format!(\n                        \"`{name}` refers to itself with different type arguments; a \\\n                         self-reference must repeat `{expected}` unchanged\"\n                    ),\n                ));\n            }\n            return Ok(Type::Enum {\n                name: name.to_string(),\n                args: resolved_args,\n                variants: Vec::new(),\n            });\n        }\n        // The names expanded since this one last appeared are the cycle, and naming them\n        // is the difference between knowing there is one and finding it.\n        let through: Vec<String> = seen[at + 1..]\n            .iter()\n            .map(|(s, _)| format!(\"`{s}`\"))\n            .collect();\n        let path = if through.is_empty() {\n            String::new()\n        } else {\n            format!(\", through {}\", through.join(\" and \"))\n        };\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\"type `{name}` is written in terms of itself{path}\"),\n        ));\n    }\n    if let Some(written) = env.aliases.get(name) {\n        if !args.is_empty() {\n            return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"`{name}` takes no type argument\")));\n        }\n        seen.push((name.to_string(), Vec::new()));\n        let expanded = resolve_bound(written, env, seen, params, boxed);\n        seen.pop();\n        return expanded;\n    }\n    if let Some(decl) = env.enums.get(name) {\n        let resolved_args = resolve_args(name, args, decl.params.len(), span, env, seen, params)?;\n        return resolve_enum(decl, env, seen, Some(&resolved_args));\n    }\n    Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"unknown type `{name}`\")))\n}\n\n/// `resolve` with a generic enum's parameters in scope: inside `enum Opt<T>`'s payloads,\n/// `T` names whatever the binding holds -- the argument at an instantiation, or the\n/// parameter itself in the registry template. Everywhere else the binding map is empty.\nfn resolve_bound(\n    ty: &TypeExpr,\n    env: &TypeEnv,\n    seen: &mut Vec<(String, Vec<Type>)>,\n    params: &HashMap<&str, &Type>,\n    boxed: bool,\n) -> Result<Type, Error> {\n    match ty {\n        TypeExpr::Named { name, args, span } => {\n            resolve_named(name, args, *span, env, seen, params, boxed)\n        }\n        // The containment bans hold in the grammar itself, not just at value construction\n        // sites: a stream is not a value, so no annotation may describe one as stored.\n        TypeExpr::Vec { elem, .. } => {\n            let inner = resolve_bound(elem, env, seen, params, true)?;\n            if inner.contains_stream() {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    elem.span(),\n                    \"a Vec cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\".to_string(),\n                ));\n            }\n            Ok(Type::Vec(Box::new(inner)))\n        }\n        TypeExpr::Stream { elem, .. } => {\n            let inner = resolve_bound(elem, env, seen, params, boxed)?;\n            if inner.contains_stream() {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    elem.span(),\n                    \"a Stream cannot hold another stream; there is nothing it could yield\"\n                        .to_string(),\n                ));\n            }\n            Ok(Type::Stream(Box::new(inner)))\n        }\n        TypeExpr::Record { fields, span } => {\n            let mut out = Vec::new();\n            for (name, ty) in fields {\n                if out.iter().any(|(n, _): &(String, Type)| n == name) {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        *span,\n                        format!(\"field `{name}` is declared twice\"),\n                    ));\n                }\n                let field = resolve_bound(ty, env, seen, params, boxed)?;\n                if field.contains_stream() {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        ty.span(),\n                        format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\"),\n                    ));\n                }\n                out.push((name.clone(), field));\n            }\n            Ok(Type::Record(out))\n        }\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":459,"totalLines":459,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/check/types.rs","time":{"start":1788120646591,"end":1788120646615}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me look at the checker's types.rs to see how Int literals and arithmetic are type-checked, and how the `[]` rule works (literal resolves as Int64 wherever expected).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0544bbfb9001VTaFkghe0hhS75","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","messageID":"msg_0544bb90d001G1lzKb8OXLD5m9"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788120646631,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"id":"prt_0544bc3e4001jmVWJ5azpFY078","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"94b6040ffbb3d9f05c4638216dc44bf3958c7c09","messageID":"msg_0544bb90d001G1lzKb8OXLD5m9","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":55239,"input":3933,"output":67,"reasoning":39,"cache":{"write":0,"read":51200}},"cost":0.001093925}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788120648247,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"id":"prt_0544bca33001IXTAELOJoqaI3o","messageID":"msg_0544bc3f9001NmTZrcTOXjHjF9","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","snapshot":"94b6040ffbb3d9f05c4638216dc44bf3958c7c09","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120649028,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8276a7605ba4f923","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/check/mod.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\n2: use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n3: \n4: use crate::ast::{BinOp, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Param, Pattern, Span};\n5: use crate::error::Error;\n6: use crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\n7: use crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n8: \n9: mod linearity;\n10: mod types;\n11: \n12: use linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\n13: use types::{TypeEnv, alias_map, enum_map, resolve, resolve_enum, signatures};\n14: \n15: struct Ctx<'a> {\n16:     sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n17:     /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n18:     enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n19:     /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n20:     /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error\n21:     /// naming both, with `Shape.circle` as the qualified way out.\n22:     variant_owners: &'a HashMap<String, Vec<String>>,\n23:     /// Named bindings. At most one, since functions are unary and there is no `let`.\n24:     scope: Vec<(String, Type)>,\n25:     /// Names a match arm's record pattern bound, innermost last: reading one reads that field\n26:     /// off the arm's payload local, so nothing beyond ordinary `Field` nodes reaches the\n27:     /// backends. Each entry is the bound name, the payload's record type, and its local.\n28:     arm_fields: Vec<(String, Type, LocalId)>,\n29:     /// What `.` refers to here, if anything: its type and the local holding it.\n30:     subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n31:     /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n32:     input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n33:     /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n34:     /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n35:     inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n36:     /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n37:     /// read is refused rather than silently handed nothing, the way a second pass over an\n38:     /// already-consumed iterator would be in a language that let it compile.\n39:     lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n40:     /// Whether checking is inside a mapper's body (`map`'s, or `select`'s predicate), which\n41:     /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n42:     /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n43:     in_mapper: bool,\n44:     /// The name of the `fn` whose body is being checked; `None` in the program's own body. A\n45:     /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n46:     /// are refused whenever this is set.\n47:     in_fn: Option<&'a str>,\n48:     next_local: &'a Cell<LocalId>,\n49: }\n50: \n51: impl Ctx<'_> {\n52:     fn with(&self, subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>) -> Ctx<'_> {\n53:         Ctx {\n54:             sigs: self.sigs,\n55:             enums: self.enums,\n56:             variant_owners: self.variant_owners,\n57:             scope: self.scope.clone(),\n58:             arm_fields: self.arm_fields.clone(),\n59:             subject,\n60:             input: self.input,\n61:             inputs: self.inputs,\n62:             lines_used: self.lines_used,\n63:             in_mapper: self.in_mapper,\n64:             in_fn: self.in_fn,\n65:             next_local: self.next_local,\n66:         }\n67:     }\n68: \n69:     fn fresh(&self) -> LocalId {\n70:         let id = self.next_local.get();\n71:         self.next_local.set(id + 1);\n72:         id\n73:     }\n74: }\n75: \n76: pub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n77:     let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n78:     let aliases = alias_map(&file.aliases)?;\n79:     let env = TypeEnv {\n80:         aliases,\n81:         enums: enum_map(&file.enums)?,\n82:     };\n83:     for e in &file.enums {\n84:         if env.aliases.contains_key(&e.name) {\n85:             return Err(Error::new(\n86:                 e.span,\n87:                 format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n88:             ));\n89:         }\n90:     }\n91:     // Resolved eagerly so a broken declaration is an error even when nothing uses it, and so a\n92:     // cycle is found here rather than wherever it happened to be reached from.\n93:     for a in &file.aliases {\n94:         resolve(&a.ty, &env, &mut vec![(a.name.clone(), Vec::new())])?;\n95:     }\n96:     let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n97:     for e in &file.enums {\n98:         enums.insert(\n99:             e.name.clone(),\n100:             resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n101:         );\n102:     }\n103:     let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n104:     for e in &file.enums {\n105:         for v in &e.variants {\n106:             variant_owners\n107:                 .entry(v.name.clone())\n108:                 .or_default()\n109:                 .push(e.name.clone());\n110:         }\n111:     }\n112:     let sigs = signatures(&file.defs, &env)?;\n113:     let input = RefCell::new(None);\n114:     let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n115:     let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n116:     let ctx = Ctx {\n117:         sigs: &sigs,\n118:         enums: &enums,\n119:         variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n120:         scope: Vec::new(),\n121:         arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n122:         subject: None,\n123:         input: &input,\n124:         inputs: &inputs,\n125:         lines_used: &lines_used,\n126:         in_mapper: false,\n127:         in_fn: None,\n128:         next_local: &next_local,\n129:     };\n130: \n131:     // `prelude::inject` prepended prelude.toy's own defs to `file.defs`, which is what let\n132:     // `sigs` above resolve calls into them and is what catches a program that redefines one --\n133:     // but their bodies were already checked once, at build time (`build.rs`, via\n134:     // `prelude::checked`), and prelude.toy cannot have changed since. Rechecking them here would\n135:     // only repeat that work on every single compile.\n136:     let mut funcs = crate::prelude::checked();\n137:     let precompiled_names: HashSet<String> = funcs.iter().map(|f| f.name.clone()).collect();\n138:     let own_defs = file\n139:         .defs\n140:         .iter()\n141:         .filter(|d| !precompiled_names.contains(&d.name));\n142:     funcs.extend(check_defs(own_defs, &ctx)?);\n143: \n144:     let body = check_program_body(&ctx, &file.body)?;\n145:     // A stream cannot be printed, having nothing to show: it is not a value, and collect() is\n146:     // what turns it into one. A function body catches this for free, since its return\n147:     // annotation can never spell Stream and so can never match a body that contains one; the\n148:     // program's own result has no annotation to check against, so it needs asking directly.\n149:     if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n150:         return Err(Error::new(\n151:             file.body.span(),\n152:             \"the program's result contains a stream, which has nothing to print; pass it to \\\n153:              `collect` first\"\n154:                 .to_string(),\n155:         ));\n156:     }\n157:     // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n158:     // hand one to the printer either -- it has to build a Str first.\n159:     if body.ty.contains_char() {\n160:         return Err(Error::new(\n161:             file.body.span(),\n162:             \"the program's result contains a Char, which has no wire form to print\".to_string(),\n163:         ));\n164:     }\n165:     let input = input.into_inner();\n166:     let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n167:     // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n168:     // parsing, leaving nothing for anything else to read afterward, and jq needs a different\n169:     // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n170:     // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n171:     // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n172:     if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n173:         return Err(Error::new(\n174:             file.body.span(),\n175:             \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n176:              different ways\"\n177:                 .to_string(),\n178:         ));\n179:     }\n180:     if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n181:         return Err(Error::new(\n182:             file.body.span(),\n183:             \"a program cannot use both `input` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n184:              different ways\"\n185:                 .to_string(),\n186:         ));\n187:     }\n188:     if lines_used.get() && inputs.is_some() {\n189:         return Err(Error::new(\n190:             file.body.span(),\n191:             \"a program cannot use both `lines` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n192:              different ways\"\n193:                 .to_string(),\n194:         ));\n195:     }\n196:     Ok(tir::Program {\n197:         funcs: prune_unreachable(funcs, &body),\n198:         body,\n199:         input,\n200:         inputs,\n201:         uses_lines: lines_used.get(),\n202:         enums,\n203:     })\n204: }\n205: \n206: /// Signatures are collected before any body is checked, so a definition may call one that\n207: /// appears later in `defs`. This is also what recursion needs. No reachability pruning happens\n208: /// here: that needs the whole program's body, which `check_module` never has and `check` only\n209: /// gets once this returns, so both call this and prune afterward, over the combined result.\n210: /// `ctx`'s own per-def fields (`scope`, `subject`, `in_fn`, ...) are ignored -- each def gets its\n211: /// own, built from its signature exactly as `check` always has.\n212: fn check_defs<'a>(\n213:     defs: impl IntoIterator<Item = &'a crate::ast::Def>,\n214:     ctx: &Ctx<'a>,\n215: ) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n216:     let mut funcs = Vec::new();\n217:     for def in defs {\n218:         let sig = &ctx.sigs[&def.name];\n219:         let scope = match (&def.param, &sig.param) {\n220:             (Some(param), Some(param_ty)) => vec![(param.name.clone(), param_ty.clone())],\n221:             (None, None) => Vec::new(),\n222:             _ => unreachable!(\"a signature's param mirrors its definition's\"),\n223:         };\n224:         let def_ctx = Ctx {\n225:             sigs: ctx.sigs,\n226:             enums: ctx.enums,\n227:             variant_owners: ctx.variant_owners,\n228:             scope,\n229:             arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n230:             subject: None,\n231:             input: ctx.input,\n232:             inputs: ctx.inputs,\n233:             lines_used: ctx.lines_used,\n234:             in_mapper: false,\n235:             in_fn: Some(&def.name),\n236:             next_local: ctx.next_local,\n237:         };\n238:         // The declared return type flows into the body, so a form whose type comes from its\n239:         // position (`[]`, `input`, a variant-naming string) resolves against the annotation. A\n240:         // body that synthesises instead is compared below, where the error can name the\n241:         // function rather than just the two types.\n242:         let body = match expect_inner(&def_ctx, &def.body, &sig.ret)? {\n243:             Expected::Checked(body) => body,\n244:             Expected::Synthesised(body) => conform(&def_ctx, body, &sig.ret),\n245:         };\n246:         if let Some(param) = &def.param {\n247:             check_param(&body, param, &sig.param, &def.name, def.body.span())?;\n248:         }\n249:         if body.ty != sig.ret {\n250:             return Err(Error::new(\n251:                 def.body.span(),\n252:                 format!(\n253:                     \"`{}` declares it returns {}, but its body is {}\",\n254:                     def.name, sig.ret, body.ty\n255:                 ),\n256:             ));\n257:         }\n258:         funcs.push(tir::Func {\n259:             name: def.name.clone(),\n260:             param: def.param.as_ref().map(|p| p.name.clone()),\n261:             param_ty: sig.param.clone(),\n262:             body,\n263:         });\n264:     }\n265:     Ok(funcs)\n266: }\n267: \n268: /// Checks a module's own `pub` declarations in isolation -- `prelude.toy`'s, at build time\n269: /// (`build.rs`), before there is any file for them to be merged into and nothing yet calling any\n270: /// of it. Every declaration is kept: reachability is the calling file's question, decided once\n271: /// program and prelude are merged (`check` above, via `prune_unreachable`).\n272: pub fn check_module(module: &crate::ast::Module) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n273:     let env = TypeEnv {\n274:         aliases: HashMap::new(),\n275:         enums: enum_map(&module.enums)?,\n276:     };\n277:     let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n278:     for e in &module.enums {\n279:         enums.insert(\n280:             e.name.clone(),\n281:             resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n282:         );\n283:     }\n284:     let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n285:     for e in &module.enums {\n286:         for v in &e.variants {\n287:             variant_owners\n288:                 .entry(v.name.clone())\n289:                 .or_default()\n290:                 .push(e.name.clone());\n291:         }\n292:     }\n293:     let sigs = signatures(&module.defs, &env)?;\n294:     let input = RefCell::new(None);\n295:     let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n296:     let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n297:     let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n298:     let ctx = Ctx {\n299:         sigs: &sigs,\n300:         enums: &enums,\n301:         variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n302:         scope: Vec::new(),\n303:         arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n304:         subject: None,\n305:         input: &input,\n306:         inputs: &inputs,\n307:         lines_used: &lines_used,\n308:         in_mapper: false,\n309:         in_fn: None,\n310:         next_local: &next_local,\n311:     };\n312:     check_defs(module.defs.iter(), &ctx)\n313: }\n314: \n315: /// A signature may spell Stream now, which un-does the trick the Lines design leaned on (a\n316: /// return annotation could never match a body holding a stream), so what that trick guaranteed\n317: /// for free is checked for real here. Called only when `def` takes a parameter: a nullary\n318: /// function has none of this to check.\n319: fn check_param(\n320:     body: &Tir,\n321:     param: &Param,\n322:     param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n323:     func_name: &str,\n324:     body_span: Span,\n325: ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n326:     if matches!(param_ty, Some(Type::Stream(_))) {\n327:         check_linear(\n328:             body,\n329:             &StreamBinding::Param(&param.name),\n330:             &format!(\"`{}`\", param.name),\n331:             body_span,\n332:         )?;\n333:     }\n334:     if !param_used(body, &param.name) {\n335:         return Err(Error::new(\n336:             param.span,\n337:             format!(\n338:                 \"parameter `{}` is never used; delete it from `{}`'s definition and its call sites\",\n339:                 param.name, func_name\n340:             ),\n341:         ));\n342:     }\n343:     Ok(())\n344: }\n345: \n346: /// The program's own body, one call form special-cased ahead of `synth`: `jsonlines` is a sink,\n347: /// legal only here, as the outermost expression, taking a Vec or a Stream and having no result\n348: /// type at all, since nothing remains that could observe one. The Tir node still carries `Str`\n349: /// -- under eager lowering the emitted expression genuinely is the joined string every backend\n350: /// prints raw -- but no program can see that: `synth` refuses `jsonlines` everywhere else.\n351: fn check_program_body(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n352:     if let Expr::Call { func, arg, .. } = body\n353:         && func == \"jsonlines\"\n354:     {\n355:         let Some(arg) = arg else {\n356:             return Err(Error::new(\n357:                 body.span(),\n358:                 \"`jsonlines` needs a Vec or a stream, but was called with no argument\".to_string(),\n359:             ));\n360:         };\n361:         let arg_span = arg.span();\n362:         let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n363:         if !matches!(arg.ty, Type::Vec(_) | Type::Stream(_)) {\n364:             return Err(Error::new(\n365:                 arg_span,\n366:                 format!(\"`jsonlines` needs a Vec or a stream, found {}\", arg.ty),\n367:             ));\n368:         }\n369:         if arg.ty.contains_char() {\n370:             return Err(Error::new(\n371:                 arg_span,\n372:                 format!(\n373:                     \"`jsonlines` cannot print {}; Char has no wire form to write\",\n374:                     arg.ty\n375:                 ),\n376:             ));\n377:         }\n378:         return Ok(Tir::new(\n379:             Type::Str,\n380:             Kind::Builtin {\n381:                 which: tir::Builtin::JsonLines,\n382:                 arg: Box::new(arg),\n383:             },\n384:         ));\n385:     }\n386:     synth(ctx, body)\n387: }\n388: \n389: /// Every function name the language itself provides, and therefore reserves. `str`, `range`,\n390: /// `chars`, and `i64` live in `builtin()`'s fixed table; `jsonlines`, `length`, `flatten`,\n391: /// `tail`, `collect`, `fields`, `sort`, and `reverse` are polymorphic and checked from\n392: /// `synth`'s own arms; `select` and `map` rebind `.`. All fourteen are reserved the same way,\n393: /// and the docs harness (tests/docs.rs) reads this list to insist each one has a reference\n394: /// page.\n395: pub const BUILTIN_NAMES: [&str; 14] = [\n396:     \"chars\",\n397:     \"collect\",\n398:     \"length\",\n399:     \"fields\",\n400:     \"flatten\",\n401:     \"i64\",\n402:     \"jsonlines\",\n403:     \"map\",\n404:     \"range\",\n405:     \"reverse\",\n406:     \"select\",\n407:     \"sort\",\n408:     \"str\",\n409:     \"tail\",\n410: ];\n411: \n412: /// Functions the language provides. Unary like every other function, so they need no special\n413: /// call syntax and are looked up before user definitions.\n414: fn builtin(name: &str) -> Option<(tir::Builtin, Sig)> {\n415:     let vec_of = |t: Type| Type::Vec(Box::new(t));\n416:     Some(match name {\n417:         \"str\" => (\n418:             tir::Builtin::IntToStr,\n419:             Sig {\n420:                 param: Some(Type::Int),\n421:                 ret: Type::Str,\n422:             },\n423:         ),\n424:         \"range\" => (\n425:             tir::Builtin::Range,\n426:             Sig {\n427:                 param: Some(Type::Int),\n428:                 ret: vec_of(Type::Int),\n429:             },\n430:         ),\n431:         \"chars\" => (\n432:             tir::Builtin::Chars,\n433:             Sig {\n434:                 param: Some(Type::Str),\n435:                 ret: vec_of(Type::Char),\n436:             },\n437:         ),\n438:         // The one bridge between the integer types (kantord/toylang#83). Its argument is an\n439:         // Int, so `i64(9000000000)` is refused like any other too-big Int literal: a wide\n440:         // value enters as a literal only where an Int64 is already expected.\n441:         \"i64\" => (\n442:             tir::Builtin::IntToI64,\n443:             Sig {\n444:                 param: Some(Type::Int),\n445:                 ret: Type::Int64,\n446:             },\n447:         ),\n448:         _ => return None,\n449:     })\n450: }\n451: \n452: /// A chain split by `rebase`: the rebased body, and the stream source it was split from.\n453: struct Rebased {\n454:     body: Tir,\n455:     source: Tir,\n456: }\n457: \n458: /// Split an access chain whose outermost dimension is a stream from its source, rebasing the\n459: /// chain on `param` as a map body: every node's type loses its Stream wrapper, and an Index's\n460: /// stored depth drops by one, since the layer it counted is now the loop.\n461: ///\n462: /// Only Field, Unwrap, and Index appear in a chain, and the source is never one of them: a\n463: /// stream-typed access chain is normalized to a Map right here, so one can never be the base of\n464: /// another.\n465: fn rebase(t: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n466:     fn peel(ty: Type) -> Type {\n467:         match ty {\n468:             Type::Stream(t) => *t,\n469:             other => unreachable!(\n470:                 \"every node inside the stream dimension is stream-typed, found {other}\"\n471:             ),\n472:         }\n473:     }\n474:     fn is_chain(t: &Tir) -> bool {\n475:         matches!(\n476:             t.kind,\n477:             Kind::Field { .. } | Kind::Unwrap { .. } | Kind::Index { .. }\n478:         )\n479:     }\n480:     /// The base of one link in the chain: another link to recurse into, or the stream source\n481:     /// itself, in which case `param` stands in for the per-element value the map body reads.\n482:     fn split_base(base: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n483:         if is_chain(&base) {\n484:             rebase(base, param)\n485:         } else {\n486:             let elem = peel(base.ty.clone());\n487:             Rebased {\n488:                 body: Tir::new(elem, Kind::Local(param)),\n489:                 source: base,\n490:             }\n491:         }\n492:     }\n493:     match t.kind {\n494:         Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n495:             let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n496:             Rebased {\n497:                 body: Tir::new(\n498:                     peel(t.ty),\n499:                     Kind::Field {\n500:                         base: Box::new(base),\n501:                         name,\n502:                     },\n503:                 ),\n504:                 source,\n505:             }\n506:         }\n507:         Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n508:             let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n509:             Rebased {\n510:                 body: Tir::new(\n511:                     peel(t.ty),\n512:                     Kind::Unwrap {\n513:                         base: Box::new(base),\n514:                     },\n515:                 ),\n516:                 source,\n517:             }\n518:         }\n519:         Kind::Index {\n520:             base,\n521:             index,\n522:             depth,\n523:             elem_is_record,\n524:         } => {\n525:             let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n526:             let kind = Kind::Index {\n527:                 base: Box::new(base),\n528:                 index,\n529:                 depth: depth - 1,\n530:                 elem_is_record,\n531:             };\n532:             Rebased {\n533:                 body: Tir::new(peel(t.ty), kind),\n534:                 source,\n535:             }\n536:         }\n537:         other => unreachable!(\n538:             \"only an access chain is rebased, found {:?}\",\n539:             std::mem::discriminant(&other)\n540:         ),\n541:     }\n542: }\n543: \n544: /// The element `select` and `map` rebind `.` to: a Vec's, or a Stream's. Deliberately not\n545: /// `Type::elem`, which stays Vec-only so the reducers (`length`, `jsonlines` today) keep\n546: /// refusing a stream.\n547: fn mapper_elem(subject: &Type) -> Option<Type> {\n548:     match subject {\n549:         Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some((**t).clone()),\n550:         _ => None,\n551:     }\n552: }\n553: \n554: /// The context a mapper body (`select`'s predicate, `map`'s body) checks in: `.` rebound to the\n555: /// element a fresh `param` will hold at runtime, with a source read refused since the body runs\n556: /// once per element rather than once.\n557: fn mapper_ctx<'a>(ctx: &'a Ctx, elem: Type, param: LocalId) -> Ctx<'a> {\n558:     let mut inner = ctx.with(Some((elem, param)));\n559:     inner.in_mapper = true;\n560:     inner\n561: }\n562: \n563: /// The one enum a bare variant name refers to, or the error naming every candidate: guessing\n564: /// between two claimants would silently pick a type the program never wrote down.\n565: fn sole_owner<'a>(\n566:     ctx: &'a Ctx,\n567:     variant: &str,\n568:     owners: &[String],\n569:     span: Span,\n570: ) -> Result<&'a Type, Error> {\n571:     if owners.len() > 1 {\n572:         let named: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}`\")).collect();\n573:         let qualified: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}.{variant}`\")).collect();\n574:         return Err(Error::new(\n575:             span,\n576:             format!(\n577:                 \"`{variant}` is a variant of {}; qualify it as {}\",\n578:                 named.join(\" and \"),\n579:                 qualified.join(\" or \")\n580:             ),\n581:         ));\n582:     }\n583:     Ok(&ctx.enums[&owners[0]])\n584: }\n585: \n586: /// Every builtin and special-cased call form (`select`, `map`, `length`, ...) is unary; only a\n587: /// user-defined function can be nullary. This unwraps a call's optional argument for those\n588: /// forms, turning a bare `name()` at one of these names into the same \"not a function\" shape as\n589: /// any other arity mismatch would need, rather than a panic.\n590: fn need_arg<'a>(arg: &'a Option<Box<Expr>>, func: &str, span: Span) -> Result<&'a Expr, Error> {\n591:     arg.as_deref()\n592:         .ok_or_else(|| Error::new(span, format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument\")))\n593: }\n594: \n595: /// `expr` is a call to `name` carrying an argument, for `expect_inner`'s `collect`/`map`\n596: /// special cases: a bare `name()` (no argument) is not one of these, and falls through to\n597: /// `synth`, which owns the arity error for a name that is not actually nullary.\n598: fn call_named<'a>(expr: &'a Expr, name: &str) -> Option<&'a Expr> {\n599:     let Expr::Call { func, arg, .. } = expr else {\n600:         return None;\n601:     };\n602:     if func != name {\n603:         return None;\n604:     }\n605:     arg.as_deref()\n606: }\n607: \n608: /// `name`'s variant list at `args`. A thin wrapper over the shared re-derivation\n609: /// (`ty::variants_of`), which the backends reach for too: the checker's own `Type::Enum` in\n610: /// hand carries a placeholder wherever a self-reference sits behind a `Vec`\n611: /// (`types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), so every consumer that actually needs the\n612: /// variants comes through here instead, one layer at a time, exactly as deep as the program\n613: /// itself ever navigates into the value.\n614: fn enum_variants(ctx: &Ctx, name: &str, args: &[Type]) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n615:     ty::variants_of(ctx.enums, name, args)\n616: }\n617: \n618: /// The prelude's `Opt<T>` at a concrete `T`: what every Opt-producing site -- the collapsing\n619: /// index, `tail`, a partial guard chain -- wraps its result in. Instantiated from the\n620: /// registry template rather than spelled out, so the checker never re-states the prelude's\n621: /// declaration.\n622: fn opt_of(ctx: &Ctx, inner: Type) -> Type {\n623:     let template = ctx.enums.get(\"Opt\").expect(\"the prelude declares Opt\");\n624:     let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template else {\n625:         unreachable!(\"the registry holds enum types\")\n626:     };\n627:     let Type::Param(p) = &args[0] else {\n628:         unreachable!(\"Opt's template argument is its own parameter\")\n629:     };\n630:     ty::substitute(template, &HashMap::from([(p.clone(), inner)]))\n631: }\n632: \n633: /// Match a template type (parameters possibly inside) against a concrete one, binding each\n634: /// parameter to what it faces. Records match fields by name, not position, the same way type\n635: /// equality does (kantord/toylang#60); enums match on name and arguments, since the variants\n636: /// follow from those.\n637: fn unify(template: &Type, concrete: &Type, map: &mut HashMap<String, Type>) -> bool {\n638:     match (template, concrete) {\n639:         (Type::Param(p), c) => match map.get(p) {\n640:             Some(bound) => bound == c,\n641:             None => {\n642:                 map.insert(p.clone(), c.clone());\n643:                 true\n644:             }\n645:         },\n646:         (Type::Vec(a), Type::Vec(b)) | (Type::Stream(a), Type::Stream(b)) => unify(a, b, map),\n647:         (Type::Record(a), Type::Record(b)) => {\n648:             a.len() == b.len()\n649:                 && a.iter().all(|(n, t)| {\n650:                     b.iter()\n651:                         .find(|(bn, _)| bn == n)\n652:                         .is_some_and(|(_, bt)| unify(t, bt, map))\n653:                 })\n654:         }\n655:         (\n656:             Type::Enum {\n657:                 name: n1, args: a1, ..\n658:             },\n659:             Type::Enum {\n660:                 name: n2, args: a2, ..\n661:             },\n662:         ) => n1 == n2 && a1.len() == a2.len() && a1.iter().zip(a2).all(|(x, y)| unify(x, y, map)),\n663:         _ => template == concrete,\n664:     }\n665: }\n666: \n667: /// Build one variant of `enum_ty`, checking that the payload written matches the payload\n668: /// declared: a unit variant takes none, a payload variant requires one.\n669: ///\n670: /// `enum_ty` is a registry template for a generic enum reached by name, or a concrete type\n671: /// when the position already knows one (an expectation, or a written annotation). A template\n672: /// instantiates from the expectation when `expected` names the same enum; otherwise the\n673: /// payload's synthesised type binds the parameters, and a variant that leaves any parameter\n674: /// open -- every unit variant of a generic enum does -- is refused, the `[]` answer.\n675: fn construct(\n676:     ctx: &Ctx,\n677:     enum_ty: &Type,\n678:     variant: &str,\n679:     variant_span: Span,\n680:     payload: Option<&Expr>,\n681:     expected: Option<&Type>,\n682: ) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n683:     let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n684:         unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n685:     };\n686:     let is_template = args.iter().any(|a| matches!(a, Type::Param(_)));\n687:     let instantiated;\n688:     let enum_ty = if !is_template {\n689:         enum_ty\n690:     } else if let Some(want @ Type::Enum { name: wanted, .. }) = expected\n691:         && wanted == name\n692:     {\n693:         want\n694:     } else {\n695:         instantiated = infer_instantiation(ctx, enum_ty, variant, variant_span, payload)?;\n696:         return Ok(instantiated);\n697:     };\n698:     let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n699:         unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n700:     };\n701:     let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n702:     let Some((_, declared)) = variants.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == variant) else {\n703:         return Err(Error::new(\n704:             variant_span,\n705:             format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n706:         ));\n707:     };\n708:     let payload = match (declared, payload) {\n709:         (None, None) => None,\n710:         (Some(want), Some(expr)) => Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, expr, want)?)),\n711:         (None, Some(expr)) => {\n712:             return Err(Error::new(\n713:                 expr.span(),\n714:                 format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n715:             ));\n716:         }\n717:         (Some(want), None) => {\n718:             // The hint mirrors the declaration's two spellings: braces for a record payload,\n719:             // parens for any other type.\n720:             let spelled = match want {\n721:                 Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n722:                 _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n723:             };\n724:             return Err(Error::new(\n725:                 variant_span,\n726:                 format!(\n727:                     \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n728:                 ),\n729:             ));\n730:         }\n731:     };\n732:     Ok(Tir::new(\n733:         enum_ty.clone(),\n734:         Kind::EnumLit {\n735:             variant: variant.to_string(),\n736:             payload,\n737:         },\n738:     ))\n739: }\n740: \n741: /// A generic enum's constructor met with no expectation: the payload is synthesised and its\n742: /// type binds the parameters. The template's own display (`Pair<T>`) does the talking in the\n743: /// errors, since it shows the reader exactly which names are still open.\n744: fn infer_instantiation(\n745:     ctx: &Ctx,\n746:     template: &Type,\n747:     variant: &str,\n748:     variant_span: Span,\n749:     payload: Option<&Expr>,\n750: ) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n751:     let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = template else {\n752:         unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n753:     };\n754:     let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n755:     let Some((_, declared)) = variants.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == variant) else {\n756:         return Err(Error::new(\n757:             variant_span,\n758:             format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n759:         ));\n760:     };\n761:     let (declared, expr) = match (declared, payload) {\n762:         (Some(declared), Some(expr)) => (declared, expr),\n763:         (None, Some(expr)) => {\n764:             return Err(Error::new(\n765:                 expr.span(),\n766:                 format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n767:             ));\n768:         }\n769:         (Some(want), None) => {\n770:             let spelled = match want {\n771:                 Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n772:                 _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n773:             };\n774:             return Err(Error::new(\n775:                 variant_span,\n776:                 format!(\n777:                     \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n778:                 ),\n779:             ));\n780:         }\n781:         (None, None) => {\n782:             return Err(Error::new(\n783:                 variant_span,\n784:                 format!(\n785:                     \"cannot tell what `{template}` a bare `{variant}` builds; only a position \\\n786:                      that expects one can say\"\n787:                 ),\n788:             ));\n789:         }\n790:     };\n791:     let built = synth(ctx, expr)?;\n792:     let mut bindings = HashMap::new();\n793:     if !unify(declared, &built.ty, &mut bindings) {\n794:         return Err(Error::new(\n795:             expr.span(),\n796:             format!(\"expected {declared}, found {}\", built.ty),\n797:         ));\n798:     }\n799:     if let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template\n800:         && let Some(open) = args.iter().find_map(|a| match a {\n801:             Type::Param(p) if !bindings.contains_key(p) => Some(p),\n802:             _ => None,\n803:         })\n804:     {\n805:         return Err(Error::new(\n806:             variant_span,\n807:             format!(\n808:                 \"`{variant}`'s payload does not say what `{template}`'s `{open}` is; only a \\\n809:                  position that expects one can\"\n810:             ),\n811:         ));\n812:     }\n813:     Ok(Tir::new(\n814:         ty::substitute(template, &bindings),\n815:         Kind::EnumLit {\n816:             variant: variant.to_string(),\n817:             payload: Some(Box::new(built)),\n818:         },\n819:     ))\n820: }\n821: \n822: /// A source spelled inside a `fn` body. The rule exists because the mapper-body check alone had\n823: /// a cross-function hole: a function reading a source, called from a mapper's body, re-read\n824: /// stdin once per element with nothing at the call site to refuse. Refusing sources in every\n825: /// `fn` closes that everywhere; the alternative, tracking source consumption per function\n826: /// transitively, was rejected as an invisible effect on every signature.\n827: fn source_in_fn(span: Span, source: &str, func: &str) -> Error {\n828:     Error::new(\n829:         span,\n830:         format!(\n831:             \"`{source}` cannot be read inside `fn {func}`; a source is legal only in the \\\n832:              program's own body, so take the stream through a `Stream` parameter\"\n833:         ),\n834:     )\n835: }\n836: \n837: /// The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`. `want_elem` is `Some` in the checking\n838: /// direction, needed because `collect(inputs)` has no type to synthesise until the wanted\n839: /// Vec<T> pushes a Stream<T> expectation down onto `inputs`; it is `None` in the synthesising\n840: /// direction, where the argument's own type says what T is.\n841: fn collect(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, want_elem: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n842:     let (elem, arg) = match want_elem {\n843:         Some(elem) => (\n844:             elem.clone(),\n845:             expect(ctx, arg, &Type::Stream(Box::new(elem.clone())))?,\n846:         ),\n847:         None => {\n848:             let arg_span = arg.span();\n849:             let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n850:             let Type::Stream(elem) = &arg.ty else {\n851:                 return Err(Error::new(\n852:                     arg_span,\n853:                     format!(\"`collect` needs a stream, found {}\", arg.ty),\n854:                 ));\n855:             };\n856:             (elem.as_ref().clone(), arg)\n857:         }\n858:     };\n859:     Ok(Tir::new(\n860:         Type::Vec(Box::new(elem)),\n861:         Kind::Builtin {\n862:             which: tir::Builtin::Collect,\n863:             arg: Box::new(arg),\n864:         },\n865:     ))\n866: }\n867: \n868: /// A `map` call over the subject. `want_elem` is the element type the position expects of the\n869: /// result, when it expects one whose cardinality matches the subject's; the body is checked\n870: /// against it, which is what lets a mapper body hold `[]`, a variant-naming string, or the\n871: /// other checked-only forms. With `None` the body synthesises, as it always has.\n872: fn map_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, span: Span, want_elem: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n873:     let Some((subject, id)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n874:         return Err(Error::new(\n875:             span,\n876:             \"`map` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\",\n877:         ));\n878:     };\n879:     let Some(elem) = mapper_elem(&subject) else {\n880:         return Err(Error::new(\n881:             span,\n882:             format!(\"`map` needs a Vec or a stream, found {subject}\"),\n883:         ));\n884:     };\n885:     let param = ctx.fresh();\n886:     let inner = mapper_ctx(ctx, elem, param);\n887:     let body = match want_elem {\n888:         Some(want) => expect(&inner, arg, want)?,\n889:         None => synth(&inner, arg)?,\n890:     };\n891:     // The elements of the result are stored, and a stream is not storable: this is the same\n892:     // containment ban a Vec literal enforces, met here before the Vec or Stream of them could\n893:     // exist.\n894:     if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n895:         return Err(Error::new(\n896:             arg.span(),\n897:             \"a `map` body cannot be a stream, which has nothing to store\".to_string(),\n898:         ));\n899:     }\n900:     let out = match &subject {\n901:         Type::Stream(_) => Type::Stream(Box::new(body.ty.clone())),\n902:         _ => Type::Vec(Box::new(body.ty.clone())),\n903:     };\n904:     let source = Tir::new(subject, Kind::Local(id));\n905:     Ok(Tir::new(\n906:         out,\n907:         Kind::Map {\n908:             source: Box::new(source),\n909:             param,\n910:             body: Box::new(body),\n911:         },\n912:     ))\n913: }\n914: \n915: /// `|` binds `.` in the right side to the value of the left. It is composition, not a map:\n916: /// the operators that distribute over a Vec do so themselves. The pipe's value is the right\n917: /// side's, so an expectation flows into the right side whole -- which is how one reaches the\n918: /// subject-fed forms (`map`, `select`, a match chain), since they only exist after `|`.\n919: fn pipe(ctx: &Ctx, lhs: &Expr, rhs: &Expr, want: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Expected, Error> {\n920:     let value = synth(ctx, lhs)?;\n921:     let local = ctx.fresh();\n922:     let inner = ctx.with(Some((value.ty.clone(), local)));\n923:     let body = match want {\n924:         Some(want) => expect_inner(&inner, rhs, want)?,\n925:         None => Expected::Synthesised(synth(&inner, rhs)?),\n926:     };\n927:     let (checked, body) = match body {\n928:         Expected::Checked(tir) => (true, tir),\n929:         Expected::Synthesised(tir) => (false, tir),\n930:     };\n931:     // A stream on the right of `|` must have flowed in from the left: a source read beside an\n932:     // unrelated piped value is not one chain, and one chain from source to sink is the shape\n933:     // the whole effect layer keeps.\n934:     if matches!(body.ty, Type::Stream(_)) && !matches!(value.ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n935:         return Err(Error::new(\n936:             rhs.span(),\n937:             \"this stream does not flow in from the left of `|`; write the pipeline as \\\n938:              one chain from its source\"\n939:                 .to_string(),\n940:         ));\n941:     }\n942:     // The same linearity a stream-typed parameter gets: `|` is the one construct that can\n943:     // silently drop its left side, so a stream piped in must be consumed here.\n944:     if matches!(value.ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n945:         check_linear(\n946:             &body,\n947:             &StreamBinding::Local(local),\n948:             \"the stream piped in here\",\n949:             rhs.span(),\n950:         )?;\n951:     }\n952:     let tir = Tir::new(\n953:         body.ty.clone(),\n954:         Kind::Bind {\n955:             local,\n956:             value: Box::new(value),\n957:             body: Box::new(body),\n958:         },\n959:     );\n960:     Ok(if checked {\n961:         Expected::Checked(tir)\n962:     } else {\n963:         Expected::Synthesised(tir)\n964:     })\n965: }\n966: \n967: /// The hybrid totality the match-arms decision fixed. A chain with variant patterns is\n968: /// closed-world: the subject is a declared enum, and the arms are proved to cover every\n969: /// variant (or end in a default); guards do not count toward that coverage. A pure guard\n970: /// chain is open-world and may be honestly partial, yielding `Opt` -- except when the arm\n971: /// bodies are themselves `Opt`-typed, refused below. `.` narrows per arm: to the payload in\n972: /// a payload arm, to nothing in a unit arm, and it stays the subject in a guard or default\n973: /// arm.\n974: ///\n975: /// `body_want` is what every arm's body must be -- for a partial chain, the expectation with\n976: /// its Opt layer already peeled by the caller. With `None` the first arm's body decides, as\n977: /// it always has.\n978: /// The dead-arm rule, both ways a chain can already be finished: a default above matches\n979: /// everything, and arms covering every variant leave nothing for a later arm to see. (The\n980: /// second is also what lets every backend take a total chain's last arm without a test.)\n981: fn check_reachable(\n982:     ctx: &Ctx,\n983:     subject_ty: &Type,\n984:     covered: &[String],\n985:     default_seen: bool,\n986:     arm_span: Span,\n987: ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n988:     if default_seen {\n989:         return Err(Error::new(\n990:             arm_span,\n991:             \"this arm can never match; the `any()` arm above it already matches everything\"\n992:                 .to_string(),\n993:         ));\n994:     }\n995:     if let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = subject_ty\n996:         && !covered.is_empty()\n997:         && enum_variants(ctx, name, args)\n998:             .iter()\n999:             .all(|(n, _)| covered.contains(n))\n1000:     {\n1001:         return Err(Error::new(\n1002:             arm_span,\n1003:             format!(\n1004:                 \"this arm can never match; the arms above it already cover every variant of `{name}`\"\n1005:             ),\n1006:         ));\n1007:     }\n1008:     Ok(())\n1009: }\n1010: \n1011: /// The closed-world proof: every variant of the subject's enum is either covered by a pattern\n1012: /// arm or the chain ends in a default, which the caller has already ruled out.\n1013: fn check_coverage(\n1014:     ctx: &Ctx,\n1015:     subject_ty: &Type,\n1016:     covered: &[String],\n1017:     span: Span,\n1018: ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n1019:     let Type::Enum {\n1020:         name: enum_name,\n1021:         args,\n1022:         ..\n1023:     } = subject_ty\n1024:     else {\n1025:         unreachable!(\"a pattern arm was checked against an enum subject\")\n1026:     };\n1027:     let missing: Vec<String> = enum_variants(ctx, enum_name, args)\n1028:         .iter()\n1029:         .filter(|(n, _)| !covered.contains(n))\n1030:         .map(|(n, _)| format!(\"`{n}`\"))\n1031:         .collect();\n1032:     if missing.is_empty() {\n1033:         return Ok(());\n1034:     }\n1035:     Err(Error::new(\n1036:         span,\n1037:         format!(\n1038:             \"a match over `{enum_name}` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing {}\",\n1039:             missing.join(\" and \")\n1040:         ),\n1041:     ))\n1042: }\n1043: \n1044: /// One variant pattern against the subject: the variant must exist on the subject's enum, a\n1045: /// unit variant takes no field pattern, and a payload variant rebinds `.` to a fresh payload\n1046: /// local, with any destructured fields proved against the payload's record type. Returns the\n1047: /// payload local (`None` for a unit variant) and the context the arm's body checks in.\n1048: fn variant_arm<'a>(\n1049:     ctx: &'a Ctx,\n1050:     subject_ty: &Type,\n1051:     vname: &str,\n1052:     vspan: Span,\n1053:     fields: Option<&FieldsPattern>,\n1054: ) -> Result<(Option<LocalId>, Ctx<'a>), Error> {\n1055:     // Deliberately parameterized, not built: how `some`/`none` arms compose -- their\n1056:     // totality, and whether they flow through first-class matchers -- is what the pending\n1057:     // matcher-totality round decides (plans/opt-as-enum.md, \"Open points, owned elsewhere\").\n1058:     // Until it runs, `!` and the producers' own combinators are Opt's consumption surface.\n1059:     if subject_ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n1060:         return Err(Error::new(\n1061:             vspan,\n1062:             format!(\n1063:                 \"{subject_ty} cannot be matched by variant yet; how its arms compose is \\\n1064:                  still being decided, so insist with `!` or keep the value whole\"\n1065:             ),\n1066:         ));\n1067:     }\n1068:     let Type::Enum {\n1069:         name: enum_name,\n1070:         args,\n1071:         ..\n1072:     } = subject_ty\n1073:     else {\n1074:         return Err(Error::new(\n1075:             vspan,\n1076:             format!(\"a match needs an enum subject, found {subject_ty}\"),\n1077:         ));\n1078:     };\n1079:     let variants = enum_variants(ctx, enum_name, args);\n1080:     let Some((_, payload_ty)) = variants.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == vname) else {\n1081:         return Err(Error::new(\n1082:             vspan,\n1083:             format!(\"`{enum_name}` has no variant `{vname}`\"),\n1084:         ));\n1085:     };\n1086:     match payload_ty {\n1087:         None => {\n1088:             if let Some(f) = fields {\n1089:                 return Err(Error::new(\n1090:                     f.span,\n1091:                     format!(\n1092:                         \"`{vname}` is a unit variant of `{enum_name}` and has no payload to destructure\"\n1093:                     ),\n1094:                 ));\n1095:             }\n1096:             Ok((None, ctx.with(None)))\n1097:         }\n1098:         Some(pty) => {\n1099:             let pid = ctx.fresh();\n1100:             let mut arm_ctx = ctx.with(Some((pty.clone(), pid)));\n1101:             if let Some(f) = fields {\n1102:                 let Type::Record(pfields) = pty else {\n1103:                     return Err(Error::new(\n1104:                         f.span,\n1105:                         format!(\n1106:                             \"the payload of `{vname}` is {pty}, not a record, so there are no fields to destructure; the arm's `.` is the payload\"\n1107:                         ),\n1108:                     ));\n1109:                 };\n1110:                 for (i, (fname, fspan)) in f.names.iter().enumerate() {\n1111:                     if f.names[..i].iter().any(|(seen, _)| seen == fname) {\n1112:                         return Err(Error::new(\n1113:                             *fspan,\n1114:                             format!(\"`{fname}` is bound twice in this pattern\"),\n1115:                         ));\n1116:                     }\n1117:                     if !pfields.iter().any(|(n, _)| n == fname) {\n1118:                         return Err(Error::new(*fspan, format!(\"no field `{fname}` on {pty}\")));\n1119:                     }\n1120:                     arm_ctx.arm_fields.push((fname.clone(), pty.clone(), pid));\n1121:                 }\n1122:                 // Leaving fields out of a match against a closed type is a forgotten field\n1123:                 // until `..` says it was meant.\n1124:                 if !f.rest {\n1125:                     let missing: Vec<String> = pfields\n1126:                         .iter()\n1127:                         .filter(|(n, _)| !f.names.iter().any(|(m, _)| m == n))\n1128:                         .map(|(n, _)| format!(\"`{n}`\"))\n1129:                         .collect();\n1130:                     if !missing.is_empty() {\n1131:                         return Err(Error::new(\n1132:                             f.span,\n1133:                             format!(\n1134:                                 \"a `{vname}` pattern must name every payload field or end in `..`; missing {}\",\n1135:                                 missing.join(\" and \")\n1136:                             ),\n1137:                         ));\n1138:                     }\n1139:                 }\n1140:             }\n1141:             Ok((Some(pid), arm_ctx))\n1142:         }\n1143:     }\n1144: }\n1145: \n1146: fn match_chain(\n1147:     ctx: &Ctx,\n1148:     arms: &[MatchArm],\n1149:     span: Span,\n1150:     body_want: Option<&Type>,\n1151: ) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1152:     let Some((subject_ty, sid)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n1153:         return Err(Error::new(\n1154:             span,\n1155:             \"a match needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\".to_string(),\n1156:         ));\n1157:     };\n1158:     let mut covered: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n1159:     let mut default_seen = false;\n1160:     let mut has_pattern_arm = false;\n1161:     let mut result: Option<Type> = body_want.cloned();\n1162:     let mut out = Vec::new();\n1163:     for arm in arms {\n1164:         check_reachable(ctx, &subject_ty, &covered, default_seen, arm.span)?;\n1165:         let (variant, guard, payload, arm_ctx, fields) = match &arm.pattern {\n1166:             Pattern::Default { .. } => {\n1167:                 default_seen = true;\n1168:                 // A default matched the whole subject, so `.` stays the enum value.\n1169:                 (None, None, None, ctx.with(ctx.subject.clone()), None)\n1170:             }\n1171:             // The guard is a Bool over the unrebound subject, so it is checked in the\n1172:             // enclosing context, and the body keeps `.` as the subject too.\n1173:             Pattern::Guard(g) => {\n1174:                 let cond = expect(ctx, g, &Type::Bool)?;\n1175:                 (None, Some(cond), None, ctx.with(ctx.subject.clone()), None)\n1176:             }\n1177:             Pattern::Variant {\n1178:                 name: vname,\n1179:                 span: vspan,\n1180:                 fields,\n1181:             } => {\n1182:                 has_pattern_arm = true;\n1183:                 covered.push(vname.clone());\n1184:                 let (payload, arm_ctx) =\n1185:                     variant_arm(ctx, &subject_ty, vname, *vspan, fields.as_ref())?;\n1186:                 (Some(vname.clone()), None, payload, arm_ctx, fields.as_ref())\n1187:             }\n1188:         };\n1189:         let body = match &result {\n1190:             None => {\n1191:                 let body = synth(&arm_ctx, &arm.body)?;\n1192:                 // The same rule a conditional has: which arm runs is decided at\n1193:                 // runtime, and a pipeline's shape must not be.\n1194:                 if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n1195:                     return Err(Error::new(\n1196:                         arm.body.span(),\n1197:                         \"a match cannot yield a stream; pass each arm to `collect` first\"\n1198:                             .to_string(),\n1199:                     ));\n1200:                 }\n1201:                 result = Some(body.ty.clone());\n1202:                 body\n1203:             }\n1204:             Some(t) => expect(&arm_ctx, &arm.body, t)?,\n1205:         };\n1206:         // A bound field the arm's body never reads is the same dead-code smell as an\n1207:         // unused parameter; the fix is the same one the grammar already offers, naming\n1208:         // only what is read and closing the rest with `..`.\n1209:         if let (Some(fields), Some(pid)) = (fields, payload) {\n1210:             for (fname, fspan) in &fields.names {\n1211:                 if !field_used(&body, pid, fname) {\n1212:                     let hint = if fields.rest {\n1213:                         \"remove it from the pattern\".to_string()\n1214:                     } else {\n1215:                         \"remove it from the pattern and close it with `..`\".to_string()\n1216:                     };\n1217:                     return Err(Error::new(\n1218:                         *fspan,\n1219:                         format!(\"`{fname}` is bound here but never used in the arm's body; {hint}\"),\n1220:                     ));\n1221:                 }\n1222:             }\n1223:         }\n1224:         out.push(tir::MatchArm {\n1225:             variant,\n1226:             guard,\n1227:             payload,\n1228:             body,\n1229:         });\n1230:     }\n1231:     // Closed-world half: a chain with variant patterns keeps exhaustiveness, and only\n1232:     // patterns count toward it -- a guard is a runtime Bool the checker cannot see\n1233:     // through.\n1234:     if has_pattern_arm && !default_seen {\n1235:         check_coverage(ctx, &subject_ty, &covered, span)?;\n1236:     }\n1237:     let result = result.expect(\"the parser produces at least one arm\");\n1238:     // Open-world half: a pure guard chain with no default may decline every arm, so its\n1239:     // result is `Opt` -- of whatever the arms yield, `Opt`-typed arms included. A declined\n1240:     // chain is `none` and a matched-but-absent arm is `some(none)`, two different values now\n1241:     // that absence is tagged (kantord/toylang#62); both still print `null`, which is\n1242:     // serialization's documented lossiness, not a conflation to refuse.\n1243:     let partial = !has_pattern_arm && !default_seen;\n1244:     let result = if partial { opt_of(ctx, result) } else { result };\n1245:     let subject = Tir::new(subject_ty.clone(), Kind::Local(sid));\n1246:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1247:         result,\n1248:         Kind::Match {\n1249:             subject: Box::new(subject),\n1250:             arms: out,\n1251:             partial,\n1252:         },\n1253:     ))\n1254: }\n1255: \n1256: fn synth(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1257:     match expr {\n1258:         Expr::Str { text, .. } => Ok(Tir::new(Type::Str, Kind::Str(text.clone()))),\n1259:         Expr::Lines { span } => {\n1260:             if let Some(func) = ctx.in_fn {\n1261:                 return Err(source_in_fn(*span, \"lines\", func));\n1262:             }\n1263:             if ctx.in_mapper {\n1264:                 return Err(Error::new(\n1265:                     *span,\n1266:                     \"`lines` cannot be read inside a mapper body, which runs once per element\"\n1267:                         .to_string(),\n1268:                 ));\n1269:             }\n1270:             if ctx.lines_used.get() {\n1271:                 return Err(Error::new(\n1272:                     *span,\n1273:                     \"`lines` has already been read; there is only one stdin\".to_string(),\n1274:                 ));\n1275:             }\n1276:             ctx.lines_used.set(true);\n1277:             Ok(Tir::new(Type::Stream(Box::new(Type::Str)), Kind::Lines))\n1278:         }\n1279:         Expr::Int { value, span } => {\n1280:             // The literal is the one place a value could enter without meeting the 32-bit rule,\n1281:             // and four backends agreed on the wrong answer only because each held it in its own\n1282:             // wider representation until an operator wrapped it. Go refuses to compile such a\n1283:             // constant at all, which is what made the hole visible. A wider literal is not\n1284:             // guessed to be Int64 -- `expect_inner` resolves one only where an Int64 is\n1285:             // already expected, the same rule `[]` follows.\n1286:             if *value > i32::MAX as i64 {\n1287:                 return Err(Error::new(\n1288:                     *span,\n1289:                     format!(\n1290:                         \"integer `{value}` does not fit in Int, which is 32 bits; only a \\\n1291:                          position that expects Int64 can hold it\"\n1292:                     ),\n1293:                 ));\n1294:             }\n1295:             Ok(Tir::new(Type::Int, Kind::Int(*value)))\n1296:         }\n1297: \n1298:         Expr::Subject { span } => match &ctx.subject {\n1299:             Some((ty, id)) => Ok(Tir::new(ty.clone(), Kind::Local(*id))),\n1300:             None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"`.` is not bound here\")),\n1301:         },\n1302: \n1303:         // Innermost binding first: a pattern binding shadows a parameter, and a parameter\n1304:         // shadows a variant, the way any binding shadows a constant.\n1305:         Expr::Var { name, span } => {\n1306:             if let Some((_, payload_ty, pid)) =\n1307:                 ctx.arm_fields.iter().rev().find(|(n, _, _)| n == name)\n1308:             {\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-1308. Use offset=1309 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Param, Pattern, Span};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nmod linearity;\nmod types;\n\nuse linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\nuse types::{TypeEnv, alias_map, enum_map, resolve, resolve_enum, signatures};\n\nstruct Ctx<'a> {\n    sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n    /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n    enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n    /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n    /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/src/check/mod.rs","text":"use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Param, Pattern, Span};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nmod linearity;\nmod types;\n\nuse linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\nuse types::{TypeEnv, alias_map, enum_map, resolve, resolve_enum, signatures};\n\nstruct Ctx<'a> {\n    sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n    /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n    enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n    /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n    /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error\n    /// naming both, with `Shape.circle` as the qualified way out.\n    variant_owners: &'a HashMap<String, Vec<String>>,\n    /// Named bindings. At most one, since functions are unary and there is no `let`.\n    scope: Vec<(String, Type)>,\n    /// Names a match arm's record pattern bound, innermost last: reading one reads that field\n    /// off the arm's payload local, so nothing beyond ordinary `Field` nodes reaches the\n    /// backends. Each entry is the bound name, the payload's record type, and its local.\n    arm_fields: Vec<(String, Type, LocalId)>,\n    /// What `.` refers to here, if anything: its type and the local holding it.\n    subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n    /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n    input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n    /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n    /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n    inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n    /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n    /// read is refused rather than silently handed nothing, the way a second pass over an\n    /// already-consumed iterator would be in a language that let it compile.\n    lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n    /// Whether checking is inside a mapper's body (`map`'s, or `select`'s predicate), which\n    /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n    /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n    in_mapper: bool,\n    /// The name of the `fn` whose body is being checked; `None` in the program's own body. A\n    /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n    /// are refused whenever this is set.\n    in_fn: Option<&'a str>,\n    next_local: &'a Cell<LocalId>,\n}\n\nimpl Ctx<'_> {\n    fn with(&self, subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>) -> Ctx<'_> {\n        Ctx {\n            sigs: self.sigs,\n            enums: self.enums,\n            variant_owners: self.variant_owners,\n            scope: self.scope.clone(),\n            arm_fields: self.arm_fields.clone(),\n            subject,\n            input: self.input,\n            inputs: self.inputs,\n            lines_used: self.lines_used,\n            in_mapper: self.in_mapper,\n            in_fn: self.in_fn,\n            next_local: self.next_local,\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn fresh(&self) -> LocalId {\n        let id = self.next_local.get();\n        self.next_local.set(id + 1);\n        id\n    }\n}\n\npub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n    let aliases = alias_map(&file.aliases)?;\n    let env = TypeEnv {\n        aliases,\n        enums: enum_map(&file.enums)?,\n    };\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        if env.aliases.contains_key(&e.name) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                e.span,\n                format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    // Resolved eagerly so a broken declaration is an error even when nothing uses it, and so a\n    // cycle is found here rather than wherever it happened to be reached from.\n    for a in &file.aliases {\n        resolve(&a.ty, &env, &mut vec![(a.name.clone(), Vec::new())])?;\n    }\n    let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        enums.insert(\n            e.name.clone(),\n            resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n        );\n    }\n    let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        for v in &e.variants {\n            variant_owners\n                .entry(v.name.clone())\n                .or_default()\n                .push(e.name.clone());\n        }\n    }\n    let sigs = signatures(&file.defs, &env)?;\n    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n    let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n    let ctx = Ctx {\n        sigs: &sigs,\n        enums: &enums,\n        variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n        scope: Vec::new(),\n        arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n        subject: None,\n        input: &input,\n        inputs: &inputs,\n        lines_used: &lines_used,\n        in_mapper: false,\n        in_fn: None,\n        next_local: &next_local,\n    };\n\n    // `prelude::inject` prepended prelude.toy's own defs to `file.defs`, which is what let\n    // `sigs` above resolve calls into them and is what catches a program that redefines one --\n    // but their bodies were already checked once, at build time (`build.rs`, via\n    // `prelude::checked`), and prelude.toy cannot have changed since. Rechecking them here would\n    // only repeat that work on every single compile.\n    let mut funcs = crate::prelude::checked();\n    let precompiled_names: HashSet<String> = funcs.iter().map(|f| f.name.clone()).collect();\n    let own_defs = file\n        .defs\n        .iter()\n        .filter(|d| !precompiled_names.contains(&d.name));\n    funcs.extend(check_defs(own_defs, &ctx)?);\n\n    let body = check_program_body(&ctx, &file.body)?;\n    // A stream cannot be printed, having nothing to show: it is not a value, and collect() is\n    // what turns it into one. A function body catches this for free, since its return\n    // annotation can never spell Stream and so can never match a body that contains one; the\n    // program's own result has no annotation to check against, so it needs asking directly.\n    if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"the program's result contains a stream, which has nothing to print; pass it to \\\n             `collect` first\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    // A Char has no wire form (the reverse of `input`'s refusal above), so a program cannot\n    // hand one to the printer either -- it has to build a Str first.\n    if body.ty.contains_char() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"the program's result contains a Char, which has no wire form to print\".to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    let input = input.into_inner();\n    let inputs = inputs.into_inner();\n    // Forced by the backends, not chosen: Python's `input` reads all of stdin to EOF before\n    // parsing, leaving nothing for anything else to read afterward, and jq needs a different\n    // invocation flag for raw lines (`-R -n`) than for parsed JSON values (`-n` alone) -- one\n    // process cannot run with both. So all three ways of reading the same real stdin are\n    // mutually exclusive, not just `input` and `lines` as before.\n    if input.is_some() && lines_used.get() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `lines`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n             different ways\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    if input.is_some() && inputs.is_some() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"a program cannot use both `input` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n             different ways\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    if lines_used.get() && inputs.is_some() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            file.body.span(),\n            \"a program cannot use both `lines` and `inputs`; they read the same real stdin two \\\n             different ways\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(tir::Program {\n        funcs: prune_unreachable(funcs, &body),\n        body,\n        input,\n        inputs,\n        uses_lines: lines_used.get(),\n        enums,\n    })\n}\n\n/// Signatures are collected before any body is checked, so a definition may call one that\n/// appears later in `defs`. This is also what recursion needs. No reachability pruning happens\n/// here: that needs the whole program's body, which `check_module` never has and `check` only\n/// gets once this returns, so both call this and prune afterward, over the combined result.\n/// `ctx`'s own per-def fields (`scope`, `subject`, `in_fn`, ...) are ignored -- each def gets its\n/// own, built from its signature exactly as `check` always has.\nfn check_defs<'a>(\n    defs: impl IntoIterator<Item = &'a crate::ast::Def>,\n    ctx: &Ctx<'a>,\n) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n    let mut funcs = Vec::new();\n    for def in defs {\n        let sig = &ctx.sigs[&def.name];\n        let scope = match (&def.param, &sig.param) {\n            (Some(param), Some(param_ty)) => vec![(param.name.clone(), param_ty.clone())],\n            (None, None) => Vec::new(),\n            _ => unreachable!(\"a signature's param mirrors its definition's\"),\n        };\n        let def_ctx = Ctx {\n            sigs: ctx.sigs,\n            enums: ctx.enums,\n            variant_owners: ctx.variant_owners,\n            scope,\n            arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n            subject: None,\n            input: ctx.input,\n            inputs: ctx.inputs,\n            lines_used: ctx.lines_used,\n            in_mapper: false,\n            in_fn: Some(&def.name),\n            next_local: ctx.next_local,\n        };\n        // The declared return type flows into the body, so a form whose type comes from its\n        // position (`[]`, `input`, a variant-naming string) resolves against the annotation. A\n        // body that synthesises instead is compared below, where the error can name the\n        // function rather than just the two types.\n        let body = match expect_inner(&def_ctx, &def.body, &sig.ret)? {\n            Expected::Checked(body) => body,\n            Expected::Synthesised(body) => conform(&def_ctx, body, &sig.ret),\n        };\n        if let Some(param) = &def.param {\n            check_param(&body, param, &sig.param, &def.name, def.body.span())?;\n        }\n        if body.ty != sig.ret {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                def.body.span(),\n                format!(\n                    \"`{}` declares it returns {}, but its body is {}\",\n                    def.name, sig.ret, body.ty\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        funcs.push(tir::Func {\n            name: def.name.clone(),\n            param: def.param.as_ref().map(|p| p.name.clone()),\n            param_ty: sig.param.clone(),\n            body,\n        });\n    }\n    Ok(funcs)\n}\n\n/// Checks a module's own `pub` declarations in isolation -- `prelude.toy`'s, at build time\n/// (`build.rs`), before there is any file for them to be merged into and nothing yet calling any\n/// of it. Every declaration is kept: reachability is the calling file's question, decided once\n/// program and prelude are merged (`check` above, via `prune_unreachable`).\npub fn check_module(module: &crate::ast::Module) -> Result<Vec<tir::Func>, Error> {\n    let env = TypeEnv {\n        aliases: HashMap::new(),\n        enums: enum_map(&module.enums)?,\n    };\n    let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &module.enums {\n        enums.insert(\n            e.name.clone(),\n            resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n        );\n    }\n    let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &module.enums {\n        for v in &e.variants {\n            variant_owners\n                .entry(v.name.clone())\n                .or_default()\n                .push(e.name.clone());\n        }\n    }\n    let sigs = signatures(&module.defs, &env)?;\n    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n    let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n    let ctx = Ctx {\n        sigs: &sigs,\n        enums: &enums,\n        variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n        scope: Vec::new(),\n        arm_fields: Vec::new(),\n        subject: None,\n        input: &input,\n        inputs: &inputs,\n        lines_used: &lines_used,\n        in_mapper: false,\n        in_fn: None,\n        next_local: &next_local,\n    };\n    check_defs(module.defs.iter(), &ctx)\n}\n\n/// A signature may spell Stream now, which un-does the trick the Lines design leaned on (a\n/// return annotation could never match a body holding a stream), so what that trick guaranteed\n/// for free is checked for real here. Called only when `def` takes a parameter: a nullary\n/// function has none of this to check.\nfn check_param(\n    body: &Tir,\n    param: &Param,\n    param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n    func_name: &str,\n    body_span: Span,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    if matches!(param_ty, Some(Type::Stream(_))) {\n        check_linear(\n            body,\n            &StreamBinding::Param(&param.name),\n            &format!(\"`{}`\", param.name),\n            body_span,\n        )?;\n    }\n    if !param_used(body, &param.name) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            param.span,\n            format!(\n                \"parameter `{}` is never used; delete it from `{}`'s definition and its call sites\",\n                param.name, func_name\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// The program's own body, one call form special-cased ahead of `synth`: `jsonlines` is a sink,\n/// legal only here, as the outermost expression, taking a Vec or a Stream and having no result\n/// type at all, since nothing remains that could observe one. The Tir node still carries `Str`\n/// -- under eager lowering the emitted expression genuinely is the joined string every backend\n/// prints raw -- but no program can see that: `synth` refuses `jsonlines` everywhere else.\nfn check_program_body(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    if let Expr::Call { func, arg, .. } = body\n        && func == \"jsonlines\"\n    {\n        let Some(arg) = arg else {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                body.span(),\n                \"`jsonlines` needs a Vec or a stream, but was called with no argument\".to_string(),\n            ));\n        };\n        let arg_span = arg.span();\n        let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n        if !matches!(arg.ty, Type::Vec(_) | Type::Stream(_)) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                arg_span,\n                format!(\"`jsonlines` needs a Vec or a stream, found {}\", arg.ty),\n            ));\n        }\n        if arg.ty.contains_char() {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                arg_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"`jsonlines` cannot print {}; Char has no wire form to write\",\n                    arg.ty\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        return Ok(Tir::new(\n            Type::Str,\n            Kind::Builtin {\n                which: tir::Builtin::JsonLines,\n                arg: Box::new(arg),\n            },\n        ));\n    }\n    synth(ctx, body)\n}\n\n/// Every function name the language itself provides, and therefore reserves. `str`, `range`,\n/// `chars`, and `i64` live in `builtin()`'s fixed table; `jsonlines`, `length`, `flatten`,\n/// `tail`, `collect`, `fields`, `sort`, and `reverse` are polymorphic and checked from\n/// `synth`'s own arms; `select` and `map` rebind `.`. All fourteen are reserved the same way,\n/// and the docs harness (tests/docs.rs) reads this list to insist each one has a reference\n/// page.\npub const BUILTIN_NAMES: [&str; 14] = [\n    \"chars\",\n    \"collect\",\n    \"length\",\n    \"fields\",\n    \"flatten\",\n    \"i64\",\n    \"jsonlines\",\n    \"map\",\n    \"range\",\n    \"reverse\",\n    \"select\",\n    \"sort\",\n    \"str\",\n    \"tail\",\n];\n\n/// Functions the language provides. Unary like every other function, so they need no special\n/// call syntax and are looked up before user definitions.\nfn builtin(name: &str) -> Option<(tir::Builtin, Sig)> {\n    let vec_of = |t: Type| Type::Vec(Box::new(t));\n    Some(match name {\n        \"str\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::IntToStr,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Int),\n                ret: Type::Str,\n            },\n        ),\n        \"range\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::Range,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Int),\n                ret: vec_of(Type::Int),\n            },\n        ),\n        \"chars\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::Chars,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Str),\n                ret: vec_of(Type::Char),\n            },\n        ),\n        // The one bridge between the integer types (kantord/toylang#83). Its argument is an\n        // Int, so `i64(9000000000)` is refused like any other too-big Int literal: a wide\n        // value enters as a literal only where an Int64 is already expected.\n        \"i64\" => (\n            tir::Builtin::IntToI64,\n            Sig {\n                param: Some(Type::Int),\n                ret: Type::Int64,\n            },\n        ),\n        _ => return None,\n    })\n}\n\n/// A chain split by `rebase`: the rebased body, and the stream source it was split from.\nstruct Rebased {\n    body: Tir,\n    source: Tir,\n}\n\n/// Split an access chain whose outermost dimension is a stream from its source, rebasing the\n/// chain on `param` as a map body: every node's type loses its Stream wrapper, and an Index's\n/// stored depth drops by one, since the layer it counted is now the loop.\n///\n/// Only Field, Unwrap, and Index appear in a chain, and the source is never one of them: a\n/// stream-typed access chain is normalized to a Map right here, so one can never be the base of\n/// another.\nfn rebase(t: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n    fn peel(ty: Type) -> Type {\n        match ty {\n            Type::Stream(t) => *t,\n            other => unreachable!(\n                \"every node inside the stream dimension is stream-typed, found {other}\"\n            ),\n        }\n    }\n    fn is_chain(t: &Tir) -> bool {\n        matches!(\n            t.kind,\n            Kind::Field { .. } | Kind::Unwrap { .. } | Kind::Index { .. }\n        )\n    }\n    /// The base of one link in the chain: another link to recurse into, or the stream source\n    /// itself, in which case `param` stands in for the per-element value the map body reads.\n    fn split_base(base: Tir, param: LocalId) -> Rebased {\n        if is_chain(&base) {\n            rebase(base, param)\n        } else {\n            let elem = peel(base.ty.clone());\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(elem, Kind::Local(param)),\n                source: base,\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    match t.kind {\n        Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n            let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(\n                    peel(t.ty),\n                    Kind::Field {\n                        base: Box::new(base),\n                        name,\n                    },\n                ),\n                source,\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n            let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(\n                    peel(t.ty),\n                    Kind::Unwrap {\n                        base: Box::new(base),\n                    },\n                ),\n                source,\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Index {\n            base,\n            index,\n            depth,\n            elem_is_record,\n        } => {\n            let Rebased { body: base, source } = split_base(*base, param);\n            let kind = Kind::Index {\n                base: Box::new(base),\n                index,\n                depth: depth - 1,\n                elem_is_record,\n            };\n            Rebased {\n                body: Tir::new(peel(t.ty), kind),\n                source,\n            }\n        }\n        other => unreachable!(\n            \"only an access chain is rebased, found {:?}\",\n            std::mem::discriminant(&other)\n        ),\n    }\n}\n\n/// The element `select` and `map` rebind `.` to: a Vec's, or a Stream's. Deliberately not\n/// `Type::elem`, which stays Vec-only so the reducers (`length`, `jsonlines` today) keep\n/// refusing a stream.\nfn mapper_elem(subject: &Type) -> Option<Type> {\n    match subject {\n        Type::Vec(t) | Type::Stream(t) => Some((**t).clone()),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\n/// The context a mapper body (`select`'s predicate, `map`'s body) checks in: `.` rebound to the\n/// element a fresh `param` will hold at runtime, with a source read refused since the body runs\n/// once per element rather than once.\nfn mapper_ctx<'a>(ctx: &'a Ctx, elem: Type, param: LocalId) -> Ctx<'a> {\n    let mut inner = ctx.with(Some((elem, param)));\n    inner.in_mapper = true;\n    inner\n}\n\n/// The one enum a bare variant name refers to, or the error naming every candidate: guessing\n/// between two claimants would silently pick a type the program never wrote down.\nfn sole_owner<'a>(\n    ctx: &'a Ctx,\n    variant: &str,\n    owners: &[String],\n    span: Span,\n) -> Result<&'a Type, Error> {\n    if owners.len() > 1 {\n        let named: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}`\")).collect();\n        let qualified: Vec<String> = owners.iter().map(|e| format!(\"`{e}.{variant}`\")).collect();\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\n                \"`{variant}` is a variant of {}; qualify it as {}\",\n                named.join(\" and \"),\n                qualified.join(\" or \")\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(&ctx.enums[&owners[0]])\n}\n\n/// Every builtin and special-cased call form (`select`, `map`, `length`, ...) is unary; only a\n/// user-defined function can be nullary. This unwraps a call's optional argument for those\n/// forms, turning a bare `name()` at one of these names into the same \"not a function\" shape as\n/// any other arity mismatch would need, rather than a panic.\nfn need_arg<'a>(arg: &'a Option<Box<Expr>>, func: &str, span: Span) -> Result<&'a Expr, Error> {\n    arg.as_deref()\n        .ok_or_else(|| Error::new(span, format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument\")))\n}\n\n/// `expr` is a call to `name` carrying an argument, for `expect_inner`'s `collect`/`map`\n/// special cases: a bare `name()` (no argument) is not one of these, and falls through to\n/// `synth`, which owns the arity error for a name that is not actually nullary.\nfn call_named<'a>(expr: &'a Expr, name: &str) -> Option<&'a Expr> {\n    let Expr::Call { func, arg, .. } = expr else {\n        return None;\n    };\n    if func != name {\n        return None;\n    }\n    arg.as_deref()\n}\n\n/// `name`'s variant list at `args`. A thin wrapper over the shared re-derivation\n/// (`ty::variants_of`), which the backends reach for too: the checker's own `Type::Enum` in\n/// hand carries a placeholder wherever a self-reference sits behind a `Vec`\n/// (`types::resolve_named`, kantord/toylang#76), so every consumer that actually needs the\n/// variants comes through here instead, one layer at a time, exactly as deep as the program\n/// itself ever navigates into the value.\nfn enum_variants(ctx: &Ctx, name: &str, args: &[Type]) -> Vec<(String, Option<Type>)> {\n    ty::variants_of(ctx.enums, name, args)\n}\n\n/// The prelude's `Opt<T>` at a concrete `T`: what every Opt-producing site -- the collapsing\n/// index, `tail`, a partial guard chain -- wraps its result in. Instantiated from the\n/// registry template rather than spelled out, so the checker never re-states the prelude's\n/// declaration.\nfn opt_of(ctx: &Ctx, inner: Type) -> Type {\n    let template = ctx.enums.get(\"Opt\").expect(\"the prelude declares Opt\");\n    let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template else {\n        unreachable!(\"the registry holds enum types\")\n    };\n    let Type::Param(p) = &args[0] else {\n        unreachable!(\"Opt's template argument is its own parameter\")\n    };\n    ty::substitute(template, &HashMap::from([(p.clone(), inner)]))\n}\n\n/// Match a template type (parameters possibly inside) against a concrete one, binding each\n/// parameter to what it faces. Records match fields by name, not position, the same way type\n/// equality does (kantord/toylang#60); enums match on name and arguments, since the variants\n/// follow from those.\nfn unify(template: &Type, concrete: &Type, map: &mut HashMap<String, Type>) -> bool {\n    match (template, concrete) {\n        (Type::Param(p), c) => match map.get(p) {\n            Some(bound) => bound == c,\n            None => {\n                map.insert(p.clone(), c.clone());\n                true\n            }\n        },\n        (Type::Vec(a), Type::Vec(b)) | (Type::Stream(a), Type::Stream(b)) => unify(a, b, map),\n        (Type::Record(a), Type::Record(b)) => {\n            a.len() == b.len()\n                && a.iter().all(|(n, t)| {\n                    b.iter()\n                        .find(|(bn, _)| bn == n)\n                        .is_some_and(|(_, bt)| unify(t, bt, map))\n                })\n        }\n        (\n            Type::Enum {\n                name: n1, args: a1, ..\n            },\n            Type::Enum {\n                name: n2, args: a2, ..\n            },\n        ) => n1 == n2 && a1.len() == a2.len() && a1.iter().zip(a2).all(|(x, y)| unify(x, y, map)),\n        _ => template == concrete,\n    }\n}\n\n/// Build one variant of `enum_ty`, checking that the payload written matches the payload\n/// declared: a unit variant takes none, a payload variant requires one.\n///\n/// `enum_ty` is a registry template for a generic enum reached by name, or a concrete type\n/// when the position already knows one (an expectation, or a written annotation). A template\n/// instantiates from the expectation when `expected` names the same enum; otherwise the\n/// payload's synthesised type binds the parameters, and a variant that leaves any parameter\n/// open -- every unit variant of a generic enum does -- is refused, the `[]` answer.\nfn construct(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    enum_ty: &Type,\n    variant: &str,\n    variant_span: Span,\n    payload: Option<&Expr>,\n    expected: Option<&Type>,\n) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n        unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n    };\n    let is_template = args.iter().any(|a| matches!(a, Type::Param(_)));\n    let instantiated;\n    let enum_ty = if !is_template {\n        enum_ty\n    } else if let Some(want @ Type::Enum { name: wanted, .. }) = expected\n        && wanted == name\n    {\n        want\n    } else {\n        instantiated = infer_instantiation(ctx, enum_ty, variant, variant_span, payload)?;\n        return Ok(instantiated);\n    };\n    let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = enum_ty else {\n        unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n    };\n    let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n    let Some((_, declared)) = variants.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == variant) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let payload = match (declared, payload) {\n        (None, None) => None,\n        (Some(want), Some(expr)) => Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, expr, want)?)),\n        (None, Some(expr)) => {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                expr.span(),\n                format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        (Some(want), None) => {\n            // The hint mirrors the declaration's two spellings: braces for a record payload,\n            // parens for any other type.\n            let spelled = match want {\n                Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n                _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n            };\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                variant_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n    };\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        enum_ty.clone(),\n        Kind::EnumLit {\n            variant: variant.to_string(),\n            payload,\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// A generic enum's constructor met with no expectation: the payload is synthesised and its\n/// type binds the parameters. The template's own display (`Pair<T>`) does the talking in the\n/// errors, since it shows the reader exactly which names are still open.\nfn infer_instantiation(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    template: &Type,\n    variant: &str,\n    variant_span: Span,\n    payload: Option<&Expr>,\n) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = template else {\n        unreachable!(\"construct is only called with an enum type\")\n    };\n    let variants = enum_variants(ctx, name, args);\n    let Some((_, declared)) = variants.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == variant) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\"`{name}` has no variant `{variant}`\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let (declared, expr) = match (declared, payload) {\n        (Some(declared), Some(expr)) => (declared, expr),\n        (None, Some(expr)) => {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                expr.span(),\n                format!(\"`{variant}` is a unit variant of `{name}` and takes no payload\"),\n            ));\n        }\n        (Some(want), None) => {\n            let spelled = match want {\n                Type::Record(_) => format!(\"{variant}{{...}}\"),\n                _ => format!(\"{variant}(...)\"),\n            };\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                variant_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"`{variant}` of `{name}` carries a payload of {want}, so it is written `{spelled}`\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n        (None, None) => {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                variant_span,\n                format!(\n                    \"cannot tell what `{template}` a bare `{variant}` builds; only a position \\\n                     that expects one can say\"\n                ),\n            ));\n        }\n    };\n    let built = synth(ctx, expr)?;\n    let mut bindings = HashMap::new();\n    if !unify(declared, &built.ty, &mut bindings) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            expr.span(),\n            format!(\"expected {declared}, found {}\", built.ty),\n        ));\n    }\n    if let Type::Enum { args, .. } = template\n        && let Some(open) = args.iter().find_map(|a| match a {\n            Type::Param(p) if !bindings.contains_key(p) => Some(p),\n            _ => None,\n        })\n    {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            variant_span,\n            format!(\n                \"`{variant}`'s payload does not say what `{template}`'s `{open}` is; only a \\\n                 position that expects one can\"\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        ty::substitute(template, &bindings),\n        Kind::EnumLit {\n            variant: variant.to_string(),\n            payload: Some(Box::new(built)),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// A source spelled inside a `fn` body. The rule exists because the mapper-body check alone had\n/// a cross-function hole: a function reading a source, called from a mapper's body, re-read\n/// stdin once per element with nothing at the call site to refuse. Refusing sources in every\n/// `fn` closes that everywhere; the alternative, tracking source consumption per function\n/// transitively, was rejected as an invisible effect on every signature.\nfn source_in_fn(span: Span, source: &str, func: &str) -> Error {\n    Error::new(\n        span,\n        format!(\n            \"`{source}` cannot be read inside `fn {func}`; a source is legal only in the \\\n             program's own body, so take the stream through a `Stream` parameter\"\n        ),\n    )\n}\n\n/// The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`. `want_elem` is `Some` in the checking\n/// direction, needed because `collect(inputs)` has no type to synthesise until the wanted\n/// Vec<T> pushes a Stream<T> expectation down onto `inputs`; it is `None` in the synthesising\n/// direction, where the argument's own type says what T is.\nfn collect(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, want_elem: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let (elem, arg) = match want_elem {\n        Some(elem) => (\n            elem.clone(),\n            expect(ctx, arg, &Type::Stream(Box::new(elem.clone())))?,\n        ),\n        None => {\n            let arg_span = arg.span();\n            let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n            let Type::Stream(elem) = &arg.ty else {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    arg_span,\n                    format!(\"`collect` needs a stream, found {}\", arg.ty),\n                ));\n            };\n            (elem.as_ref().clone(), arg)\n        }\n    };\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        Type::Vec(Box::new(elem)),\n        Kind::Builtin {\n            which: tir::Builtin::Collect,\n            arg: Box::new(arg),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// A `map` call over the subject. `want_elem` is the element type the position expects of the\n/// result, when it expects one whose cardinality matches the subject's; the body is checked\n/// against it, which is what lets a mapper body hold `[]`, a variant-naming string, or the\n/// other checked-only forms. With `None` the body synthesises, as it always has.\nfn map_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, span: Span, want_elem: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Some((subject, id)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            \"`map` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\",\n        ));\n    };\n    let Some(elem) = mapper_elem(&subject) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\"`map` needs a Vec or a stream, found {subject}\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let param = ctx.fresh();\n    let inner = mapper_ctx(ctx, elem, param);\n    let body = match want_elem {\n        Some(want) => expect(&inner, arg, want)?,\n        None => synth(&inner, arg)?,\n    };\n    // The elements of the result are stored, and a stream is not storable: this is the same\n    // containment ban a Vec literal enforces, met here before the Vec or Stream of them could\n    // exist.\n    if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            arg.span(),\n            \"a `map` body cannot be a stream, which has nothing to store\".to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    let out = match &subject {\n        Type::Stream(_) => Type::Stream(Box::new(body.ty.clone())),\n        _ => Type::Vec(Box::new(body.ty.clone())),\n    };\n    let source = Tir::new(subject, Kind::Local(id));\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        out,\n        Kind::Map {\n            source: Box::new(source),\n            param,\n            body: Box::new(body),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// `|` binds `.` in the right side to the value of the left. It is composition, not a map:\n/// the operators that distribute over a Vec do so themselves. The pipe's value is the right\n/// side's, so an expectation flows into the right side whole -- which is how one reaches the\n/// subject-fed forms (`map`, `select`, a match chain), since they only exist after `|`.\nfn pipe(ctx: &Ctx, lhs: &Expr, rhs: &Expr, want: Option<&Type>) -> Result<Expected, Error> {\n    let value = synth(ctx, lhs)?;\n    let local = ctx.fresh();\n    let inner = ctx.with(Some((value.ty.clone(), local)));\n    let body = match want {\n        Some(want) => expect_inner(&inner, rhs, want)?,\n        None => Expected::Synthesised(synth(&inner, rhs)?),\n    };\n    let (checked, body) = match body {\n        Expected::Checked(tir) => (true, tir),\n        Expected::Synthesised(tir) => (false, tir),\n    };\n    // A stream on the right of `|` must have flowed in from the left: a source read beside an\n    // unrelated piped value is not one chain, and one chain from source to sink is the shape\n    // the whole effect layer keeps.\n    if matches!(body.ty, Type::Stream(_)) && !matches!(value.ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            rhs.span(),\n            \"this stream does not flow in from the left of `|`; write the pipeline as \\\n             one chain from its source\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    // The same linearity a stream-typed parameter gets: `|` is the one construct that can\n    // silently drop its left side, so a stream piped in must be consumed here.\n    if matches!(value.ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n        check_linear(\n            &body,\n            &StreamBinding::Local(local),\n            \"the stream piped in here\",\n            rhs.span(),\n        )?;\n    }\n    let tir = Tir::new(\n        body.ty.clone(),\n        Kind::Bind {\n            local,\n            value: Box::new(value),\n            body: Box::new(body),\n        },\n    );\n    Ok(if checked {\n        Expected::Checked(tir)\n    } else {\n        Expected::Synthesised(tir)\n    })\n}\n\n/// The hybrid totality the match-arms decision fixed. A chain with variant patterns is\n/// closed-world: the subject is a declared enum, and the arms are proved to cover every\n/// variant (or end in a default); guards do not count toward that coverage. A pure guard\n/// chain is open-world and may be honestly partial, yielding `Opt` -- except when the arm\n/// bodies are themselves `Opt`-typed, refused below. `.` narrows per arm: to the payload in\n/// a payload arm, to nothing in a unit arm, and it stays the subject in a guard or default\n/// arm.\n///\n/// `body_want` is what every arm's body must be -- for a partial chain, the expectation with\n/// its Opt layer already peeled by the caller. With `None` the first arm's body decides, as\n/// it always has.\n/// The dead-arm rule, both ways a chain can already be finished: a default above matches\n/// everything, and arms covering every variant leave nothing for a later arm to see. (The\n/// second is also what lets every backend take a total chain's last arm without a test.)\nfn check_reachable(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    subject_ty: &Type,\n    covered: &[String],\n    default_seen: bool,\n    arm_span: Span,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    if default_seen {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            arm_span,\n            \"this arm can never match; the `any()` arm above it already matches everything\"\n                .to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    if let Type::Enum { name, args, .. } = subject_ty\n        && !covered.is_empty()\n        && enum_variants(ctx, name, args)\n            .iter()\n            .all(|(n, _)| covered.contains(n))\n    {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            arm_span,\n            format!(\n                \"this arm can never match; the arms above it already cover every variant of `{name}`\"\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// The closed-world proof: every variant of the subject's enum is either covered by a pattern\n/// arm or the chain ends in a default, which the caller has already ruled out.\nfn check_coverage(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    subject_ty: &Type,\n    covered: &[String],\n    span: Span,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    let Type::Enum {\n        name: enum_name,\n        args,\n        ..\n    } = subject_ty\n    else {\n        unreachable!(\"a pattern arm was checked against an enum subject\")\n    };\n    let missing: Vec<String> = enum_variants(ctx, enum_name, args)\n        .iter()\n        .filter(|(n, _)| !covered.contains(n))\n        .map(|(n, _)| format!(\"`{n}`\"))\n        .collect();\n    if missing.is_empty() {\n        return Ok(());\n    }\n    Err(Error::new(\n        span,\n        format!(\n            \"a match over `{enum_name}` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing {}\",\n            missing.join(\" and \")\n        ),\n    ))\n}\n\n/// One variant pattern against the subject: the variant must exist on the subject's enum, a\n/// unit variant takes no field pattern, and a payload variant rebinds `.` to a fresh payload\n/// local, with any destructured fields proved against the payload's record type. Returns the\n/// payload local (`None` for a unit variant) and the context the arm's body checks in.\nfn variant_arm<'a>(\n    ctx: &'a Ctx,\n    subject_ty: &Type,\n    vname: &str,\n    vspan: Span,\n    fields: Option<&FieldsPattern>,\n) -> Result<(Option<LocalId>, Ctx<'a>), Error> {\n    // Deliberately parameterized, not built: how `some`/`none` arms compose -- their\n    // totality, and whether they flow through first-class matchers -- is what the pending\n    // matcher-totality round decides (plans/opt-as-enum.md, \"Open points, owned elsewhere\").\n    // Until it runs, `!` and the producers' own combinators are Opt's consumption surface.\n    if subject_ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            vspan,\n            format!(\n                \"{subject_ty} cannot be matched by variant yet; how its arms compose is \\\n                 still being decided, so insist with `!` or keep the value whole\"\n            ),\n        ));\n    }\n    let Type::Enum {\n        name: enum_name,\n        args,\n        ..\n    } = subject_ty\n    else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            vspan,\n            format!(\"a match needs an enum subject, found {subject_ty}\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let variants = enum_variants(ctx, enum_name, args);\n    let Some((_, payload_ty)) = variants.iter().find(|(n, _)| n == vname) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            vspan,\n            format!(\"`{enum_name}` has no variant `{vname}`\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    match payload_ty {\n        None => {\n            if let Some(f) = fields {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    f.span,\n                    format!(\n                        \"`{vname}` is a unit variant of `{enum_name}` and has no payload to destructure\"\n                    ),\n                ));\n            }\n            Ok((None, ctx.with(None)))\n        }\n        Some(pty) => {\n            let pid = ctx.fresh();\n            let mut arm_ctx = ctx.with(Some((pty.clone(), pid)));\n            if let Some(f) = fields {\n                let Type::Record(pfields) = pty else {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        f.span,\n                        format!(\n                            \"the payload of `{vname}` is {pty}, not a record, so there are no fields to destructure; the arm's `.` is the payload\"\n                        ),\n                    ));\n                };\n                for (i, (fname, fspan)) in f.names.iter().enumerate() {\n                    if f.names[..i].iter().any(|(seen, _)| seen == fname) {\n                        return Err(Error::new(\n                            *fspan,\n                            format!(\"`{fname}` is bound twice in this pattern\"),\n                        ));\n                    }\n                    if !pfields.iter().any(|(n, _)| n == fname) {\n                        return Err(Error::new(*fspan, format!(\"no field `{fname}` on {pty}\")));\n                    }\n                    arm_ctx.arm_fields.push((fname.clone(), pty.clone(), pid));\n                }\n                // Leaving fields out of a match against a closed type is a forgotten field\n                // until `..` says it was meant.\n                if !f.rest {\n                    let missing: Vec<String> = pfields\n                        .iter()\n                        .filter(|(n, _)| !f.names.iter().any(|(m, _)| m == n))\n                        .map(|(n, _)| format!(\"`{n}`\"))\n                        .collect();\n                    if !missing.is_empty() {\n                        return Err(Error::new(\n                            f.span,\n                            format!(\n                                \"a `{vname}` pattern must name every payload field or end in `..`; missing {}\",\n                                missing.join(\" and \")\n                            ),\n                        ));\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            Ok((Some(pid), arm_ctx))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn match_chain(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    arms: &[MatchArm],\n    span: Span,\n    body_want: Option<&Type>,\n) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Some((subject_ty, sid)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            \"a match needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\".to_string(),\n        ));\n    };\n    let mut covered: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n    let mut default_seen = false;\n    let mut has_pattern_arm = false;\n    let mut result: Option<Type> = body_want.cloned();\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\n    for arm in arms {\n        check_reachable(ctx, &subject_ty, &covered, default_seen, arm.span)?;\n        let (variant, guard, payload, arm_ctx, fields) = match &arm.pattern {\n            Pattern::Default { .. } => {\n                default_seen = true;\n                // A default matched the whole subject, so `.` stays the enum value.\n                (None, None, None, ctx.with(ctx.subject.clone()), None)\n            }\n            // The guard is a Bool over the unrebound subject, so it is checked in the\n            // enclosing context, and the body keeps `.` as the subject too.\n            Pattern::Guard(g) => {\n                let cond = expect(ctx, g, &Type::Bool)?;\n                (None, Some(cond), None, ctx.with(ctx.subject.clone()), None)\n            }\n            Pattern::Variant {\n                name: vname,\n                span: vspan,\n                fields,\n            } => {\n                has_pattern_arm = true;\n                covered.push(vname.clone());\n                let (payload, arm_ctx) =\n                    variant_arm(ctx, &subject_ty, vname, *vspan, fields.as_ref())?;\n                (Some(vname.clone()), None, payload, arm_ctx, fields.as_ref())\n            }\n        };\n        let body = match &result {\n            None => {\n                let body = synth(&arm_ctx, &arm.body)?;\n                // The same rule a conditional has: which arm runs is decided at\n                // runtime, and a pipeline's shape must not be.\n                if body.ty.contains_stream() {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        arm.body.span(),\n                        \"a match cannot yield a stream; pass each arm to `collect` first\"\n                            .to_string(),\n                    ));\n                }\n                result = Some(body.ty.clone());\n                body\n            }\n            Some(t) => expect(&arm_ctx, &arm.body, t)?,\n        };\n        // A bound field the arm's body never reads is the same dead-code smell as an\n        // unused parameter; the fix is the same one the grammar already offers, naming\n        // only what is read and closing the rest with `..`.\n        if let (Some(fields), Some(pid)) = (fields, payload) {\n            for (fname, fspan) in &fields.names {\n                if !field_used(&body, pid, fname) {\n                    let hint = if fields.rest {\n                        \"remove it from the pattern\".to_string()\n                    } else {\n                        \"remove it from the pattern and close it with `..`\".to_string()\n                    };\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        *fspan,\n                        format!(\"`{fname}` is bound here but never used in the arm's body; {hint}\"),\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        out.push(tir::MatchArm {\n            variant,\n            guard,\n            payload,\n            body,\n        });\n    }\n    // Closed-world half: a chain with variant patterns keeps exhaustiveness, and only\n    // patterns count toward it -- a guard is a runtime Bool the checker cannot see\n    // through.\n    if has_pattern_arm && !default_seen {\n        check_coverage(ctx, &subject_ty, &covered, span)?;\n    }\n    let result = result.expect(\"the parser produces at least one arm\");\n    // Open-world half: a pure guard chain with no default may decline every arm, so its\n    // result is `Opt` -- of whatever the arms yield, `Opt`-typed arms included. A declined\n    // chain is `none` and a matched-but-absent arm is `some(none)`, two different values now\n    // that absence is tagged (kantord/toylang#62); both still print `null`, which is\n    // serialization's documented lossiness, not a conflation to refuse.\n    let partial = !has_pattern_arm && !default_seen;\n    let result = if partial { opt_of(ctx, result) } else { result };\n    let subject = Tir::new(subject_ty.clone(), Kind::Local(sid));\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        result,\n        Kind::Match {\n            subject: Box::new(subject),\n            arms: out,\n            partial,\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\nfn synth(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    match expr {\n        Expr::Str { text, .. } => Ok(Tir::new(Type::Str, Kind::Str(text.clone()))),\n        Expr::Lines { span } => {\n            if let Some(func) = ctx.in_fn {\n                return Err(source_in_fn(*span, \"lines\", func));\n            }\n            if ctx.in_mapper {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    *span,\n                    \"`lines` cannot be read inside a mapper body, which runs once per element\"\n                        .to_string(),\n                ));\n            }\n            if ctx.lines_used.get() {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    *span,\n                    \"`lines` has already been read; there is only one stdin\".to_string(),\n                ));\n            }\n            ctx.lines_used.set(true);\n            Ok(Tir::new(Type::Stream(Box::new(Type::Str)), Kind::Lines))\n        }\n        Expr::Int { value, span } => {\n            // The literal is the one place a value could enter without meeting the 32-bit rule,\n            // and four backends agreed on the wrong answer only because each held it in its own\n            // wider representation until an operator wrapped it. Go refuses to compile such a\n            // constant at all, which is what made the hole visible. A wider literal is not\n            // guessed to be Int64 -- `expect_inner` resolves one only where an Int64 is\n            // already expected, the same rule `[]` follows.\n            if *value > i32::MAX as i64 {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    *span,\n                    format!(\n                        \"integer `{value}` does not fit in Int, which is 32 bits; only a \\\n                         position that expects Int64 can hold it\"\n                    ),\n                ));\n            }\n            Ok(Tir::new(Type::Int, Kind::Int(*value)))\n        }\n\n        Expr::Subject { span } => match &ctx.subject {\n            Some((ty, id)) => Ok(Tir::new(ty.clone(), Kind::Local(*id))),\n            None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"`.` is not bound here\")),\n        },\n\n        // Innermost binding first: a pattern binding shadows a parameter, and a parameter\n        // shadows a variant, the way any binding shadows a constant.\n        Expr::Var { name, span } => {\n            if let Some((_, payload_ty, pid)) =\n                ctx.arm_fields.iter().rev().find(|(n, _, _)| n == name)\n            {","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1308,"totalLines":1309,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788120649017,"end":1788120649024}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me look at the check/mod.rs to see how literals and binary ops are type-checked. This is the core checker.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0544bcb540013RYo5uFsVm6bIc","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","messageID":"msg_0544bc3f9001NmTZrcTOXjHjF9"}}
