{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120730707,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a3fc7f09692a1bc9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -l \"fn emit\" src/emit_*.rs src/lib.rs; echo ---; rg \"backend\" tests/support/mod.rs | head -40"},"output":"src/lib.rs\nsrc/emit_rs.rs\nsrc/emit_py.rs\nsrc/emit_toylang.rs\nsrc/emit_lua.rs\nsrc/emit_js.rs\nsrc/emit_llvm.rs\nsrc/emit_go.rs\nsrc/emit_jq.rs\n---\n//! Read by both the agreement harness and the native backend's tracker, which were two copies\n    /// Set when the program compiles but every backend has to refuse to run it. What each says\n    /// program prints is not the whole claim. Empty for most cases: running on every backend and\n/// What every backend has to do with the program. A case says exactly one of these, because a\n    /// Every backend prints this, and they have to agree.\n    /// Every backend refuses to run it.\n/// Runs `program` on every backend and reports how the run fell short of `expect`, one line per\n/// failure, empty when every backend did what the case asked. The corpus harness and the docs\n        for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n            if let Ok(out) = toylang::run_on(program, input, backend) {\n                    backend.name()\n    for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n        // A backend that cannot run is reported, never skipped. A report saying every backend\n        match toylang::run_on(program, input, backend) {\n            Ok(out) => outputs.push((backend.name(), out)),\n                backend.name()\n    // Disagreement first, and reported on its own. Which backend matches the expectation is not\n            \"WRONG   {name}: expected {want:?}, every backend gave {first:?}\"\n                        .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(\"{name}: `{n}` is not a backend\"))\n","metadata":{"output":"src/lib.rs\nsrc/emit_rs.rs\nsrc/emit_py.rs\nsrc/emit_toylang.rs\nsrc/emit_lua.rs\nsrc/emit_js.rs\nsrc/emit_llvm.rs\nsrc/emit_go.rs\nsrc/emit_jq.rs\n---\n//! Read by both the agreement harness and the native backend's tracker, which were two copies\n    /// Set when the program compiles but every backend has to refuse to run it. What each says\n    /// program prints is not the whole claim. Empty for most cases: running on every backend and\n/// What every backend has to do with the program. A case says exactly one of these, because a\n    /// Every backend prints this, and they have to agree.\n    /// Every backend refuses to run it.\n/// Runs `program` on every backend and reports how the run fell short of `expect`, one line per\n/// failure, empty when every backend did what the case asked. The corpus harness and the docs\n        for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n            if let Ok(out) = toylang::run_on(program, input, backend) {\n                    backend.name()\n    for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n        // A backend that cannot run is reported, never skipped. A report saying every backend\n        match toylang::run_on(program, input, backend) {\n            Ok(out) => outputs.push((backend.name(), out)),\n                backend.name()\n    // Disagreement first, and reported on its own. Which backend matches the expectation is not\n            \"WRONG   {name}: expected {want:?}, every backend gave {first:?}\"\n                        .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(\"{name}: `{n}` is not a backend\"))\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -l \"fn emit\" src/emit_*.rs src/lib.rs; echo ---; rg \"backend\" tests/support/mod.rs | head -40","time":{"start":1788120730703,"end":1788120730705}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good understanding of the JS backend. The other backends will have similar structure. This is a big task. Let me think about the scope carefully.\n\nThe task is to implement Float across all 7 backends. The 7 backends are: Go (emit_go.rs), jq (emit_jq.rs), JS (emit_js.rs), LLVM (emit_llvm.rs), Lua (emit_lua.rs), Python (emit_py.rs), Rust (emit_rs.rs), and toylang (emit_toylang.rs). Wait, that's 8. Let me check what the \"seven backends\" are.\n\nActually let me check: tests/backend_*.rs files: backend_go.rs, backend_jq.rs, backend_js.rs, backend_llvm.rs, backend_py.rs, backend_rust.rs. And the toylang backend (native)? Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0544d0b8b001RUjdvE7KODTnS6","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","messageID":"msg_0544cf70d001GJJ1V5aI82fiSq"}}
