{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120916775,"sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b482dea7603bdcd6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"3\\.14|Float|float\" docs/overview/vision.md draft.md | head -40"},"output":"draft.md:796:This earns its keep on the CPU before any GPU exists. LLVM refuses to vectorise a floating-point\ndraft.md:1012:dependencies, floating-point reductions, calls in the loop body, unknown trip counts combined\ndraft.md:1021:| float reduction | `fold` declares associativity, so `reassoc` is legitimate; `reduce` does not, so it is not |\ndraft.md:1036:Not a question about JSON-shaped types. Both LLVM and Cranelift bottom out in integers, floats,\ndraft.md:1155:floating-point contraction off so the optimizer does not fuse operations behind your back, and to\ndraft.md:2265:## DECIDED: Float is JavaScript's double\ndraft.md:2267:`Float`, when it is built, is exactly the IEEE 754 binary64 double that every JavaScript\ndraft.md:2273:second float on the two targets that have only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\ndraft.md:2274:Recorded as [an ADR](docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md) as well; nothing is\ndraft.md:2275:implemented yet, and `3.14` in the values list above is still only notation.\ndraft.md:2278:[the float-semantics question](plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model)\ndocs/overview/vision.md:51:null   true   42   3.14   \"text\"   [1, 2, 3]   {name: \"ada\", age: 36}\n","metadata":{"output":"draft.md:796:This earns its keep on the CPU before any GPU exists. LLVM refuses to vectorise a floating-point\ndraft.md:1012:dependencies, floating-point reductions, calls in the loop body, unknown trip counts combined\ndraft.md:1021:| float reduction | `fold` declares associativity, so `reassoc` is legitimate; `reduce` does not, so it is not |\ndraft.md:1036:Not a question about JSON-shaped types. Both LLVM and Cranelift bottom out in integers, floats,\ndraft.md:1155:floating-point contraction off so the optimizer does not fuse operations behind your back, and to\ndraft.md:2265:## DECIDED: Float is JavaScript's double\ndraft.md:2267:`Float`, when it is built, is exactly the IEEE 754 binary64 double that every JavaScript\ndraft.md:2273:second float on the two targets that have only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\ndraft.md:2274:Recorded as [an ADR](docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md) as well; nothing is\ndraft.md:2275:implemented yet, and `3.14` in the values list above is still only notation.\ndraft.md:2278:[the float-semantics question](plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model)\ndocs/overview/vision.md:51:null   true   42   3.14   \"text\"   [1, 2, 3]   {name: \"ada\", age: 36}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"3\\.14|Float|float\" docs/overview/vision.md draft.md | head -40","time":{"start":1788120916768,"end":1788120916772}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good understanding of the architecture. Let me think about the vertical slice approach.\n\nThe task:\n1. Float literals parse and type-check\n2. arithmetic (+ - * /) and comparisons work across all 7 backends with agreeing output\n3. NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\n4. division by zero on Float returns Infinity (not error); Int division still raises on zero\n5. corpus test coverage\n\nThe task says: scope down to a first vertical slice. Get Float literals parsing, type-checking, and basic arithmetic (+ - * /) plus comparisons working end to end on ALL backends with agreeing output before touching NaN/Infinity edge cases.\n\nFloat literal print format must be byte-identical across all 7 backends. This is MY call to make. I need to pick one format and hold every backend to it.\n\nKey design decisions to make:\n1. How to represent Float in the type system: `Type::Float`\n2. How to parse float literals: currently `Tok::Int(i64)`. I need to add float literals. The tricky part: `3.14` currently is parsed as `3` then `.14`... wait, no. Let me check. `.` is field access / subject. Currently `3.14` would parse `3` (Tok::Int) then `.` (Tok::Dot) then `14`... Actually in atom, Tok::Int produces Expr::Int{3}, then postfix loop: `.` -> field access, reads next token which must be Ident. `14` is an Int token, not Ident, so it errors \"expected a field name\". So currently `3.14` is a parse error.\n\nSo I need to add a float literal token. Design: when reading digits, if a `.` immediately follows (with no space?) followed by more digits, it's a float literal. What about `1.`? Or `.5`? IEEE-754 literals. Let me think about what a reasonable float literal syntax is.\n\nJavaScript's number literal syntax: `3.14`, `1e21`, `1.5e-3`. Since Float is \"JavaScript's number\", a natural literal syntax would match JS: digits [. digits] [e[+-]digits].\n\nBut there's a conflict: `.` is used for field access and the subject. `1.5` — when parsing, the lexer reads the token. Since lexer reads `1` as digits, then would need to look ahead: after digits, if next char is `.` and char after is a digit, it's a float. If next is `.` followed by non-digit (e.g. `.name`), then... hmm `1.foo`? Currently `1.foo` is Int(1) `.` field `foo` — an error since you can't access a field on an Int. But with floats, `1.5` should be a float literal.\n\nWait, but consider `1.name`? That's weird. Currently it'd be field access on int. With the new lexer: digits `1`, then `.`, then `name` (not digit) → not a float literal, so it stays Int(1), Dot, Ident. Same behavior.\n\nSo rule: after a run of digits, if the next two chars are `.` followed by an ASCII digit, consume the `.` and more digits, and then optional exponent `e`/`E` followed by optional sign and digits. That gives `1.5`. What about `1.`? The brief says \"Float literals\" and mentions \"3.14\" appears in overview. `1.` is a valid float in JS (prints as 1). But supporting `1.` adds ambiguity: `1..foo`? Not worth it. I'll require a digit after the dot. That's the JS-legal form too... actually JS allows `1.` as a numeric literal. But for our purposes, requiring a digit after `.` is fine and simpler. Actually, hold on — what about the case where someone writes `range(1).foo`? Not valid anyway.\n\nWhat about `.5` (leading dot)? That conflicts hard with the subject `.` and field access `.name`. So no leading-dot floats. Fine.\n\nWhat about `1e21` (no dot)? That's a float literal too — has an exponent. `1e21` in JS is a Number (1e21). Since a number literal with an exponent is clearly a float, support that too? Hmm, but it adds complexity. Let me think about whether to support exponent form in the first vertical slice.\n\nThe brief: \"Float literals parse and type-check... basic arithmetic (+ - * /) plus comparisons\". The corpus tests need basic float arithmetic, comparisons, NaN, Infinity, and division by zero. NaN/Infinity don't have literal spellings in the language necessarily — they're producible via arithmetic (1.0/0.0 = Infinity, 0.0/0.0 = NaN). The ADR notes \"what NaN and Infinity mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either)\".\n\nSo for the vertical slice, literals need to support at least the basic `3.14` form. Exponents would be needed to test things like large/small values, but let me consider. The acceptance criteria mentions printing NaN/Infinity identically. Division by zero produces Infinity. For producing NaN, `0.0 / 0.0`.\n\nLet me decide on the literal syntax. I'll support:\n- digits `.` digits (e.g. `3.14`)\n- optional exponent: `e`/`E` followed by optional `+`/`-` and digits (e.g. `1e21`, `3.14e-2`)\n\nWait — but there's a lexical issue with exponent: after `1e21`, the `e` is part of the number. But what if someone writes `1e` as in... no, that would just be invalid. Also `1efoo`? Would consume `1e` and then fail on `f`? Let me handle: exponent only consumed if followed by [sign]digits.\n\nHmm, but here's a subtlety: consider `3e` — is that a float literal attempt that fails, or `3` times `e`? Multiplication requires `*`, so `3e` isn't valid anyway. To be safe, only treat `e` as exponent when it's followed by an optional sign then a digit. Otherwise stop the number there and let `e` be an identifier. Actually since multiplication is `*`, `3e` can't be anything valid regardless. But to be conservative, require the digit.\n\nActually, let me reconsider scope. This is a huge task — 7 backends. Let me keep the literal syntax minimal but complete enough for the tests: `digits.digits` with optional exponent. I'll support the exponent because printing large/small floats is exactly where the \"shortest-roundtrip vs fixed formatting\" question bites, and being able to write `1e21` in a test helps pin the format.\n\nNow, the big design decision: **the float literal print format**. This is my call. The options:\n- Shortest-roundtrip (Rust `{}` on f64, Python `repr`, JS `String()`, Go `strconv.FormatFloat(f, 'g', -1, 64)`, Lua `%g`...).\n- Fixed with some number of decimals.\n\nRust's `{}` for f64 gives shortest roundtrip that also matches Python's repr and JS's String in most cases, but not all. Actually the three shortest-roundtrip algorithms (Rust/Grisu+Ryu, Python/Ryu, JS/ECMAScript, Go/Grisu, Lua `%g` with 15 sig digits, jq `tostring`) differ in edge cases and in the exponent notation.\n\nHmm wait. This is exactly the hard part the issue flags: \"default float formatting differs across languages\". \n\nThe cleanest approach: **have every backend emit a formatting function that produces the same output**, implemented per-backend. But that's a huge amount of work across 7 backends (including LLVM C runtime).\n\nAlternative approach used by some of these backends for other things: emit a printer that produces a canonical format. E.g., many backends could emit something like `format!(\"{:?}\", x)` in Rust, `repr(x)` in Python, `String(x)` in JS... but these do NOT agree byte-for-byte in all cases.\n\nLet me think about what the simplest consistent choice is. \n\nOption A: **A decimal-with-fixed-precision print that we control in each backend.** E.g., print with enough digits to roundtrip but in a fixed scientific/fixed form. Still need a shortest-roundtrip algorithm per backend.\n\nOption B: **Print via a shared algorithm reimplemented in each backend.** Too much.\n\nOption C: **Pick one backend's native format and make the others match it.** E.g., Rust's `{}` format. But making Python/JS/Go/Lua/jq match Rust's exact format (including exponent notation `e` vs `E`, `1e21` switchover) requires custom printing code in each.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. What does jq do? jq's default output for numbers uses a shortest-roundtrip with its own formatting. Lua's `%g` uses 14 sig digits by default. Go's `%v` uses `%g` (shortest) but exponent format differs (e.g. `1e+06`?). \n\nActually, maybe the simplest robust approach that guarantees byte-identical output with a *known, small* algorithm: **implement a Ryu/grisu-like shortest-roundtrip printer once in Rust, and... no, each backend is a separate emitted language.**\n\nLet me reconsider. What if instead of shortest-roundtrip, we pick a **fixed-precision decimal format** that is:\n1. Computable in every backend with simple string manipulation on the decimal representation,\n2. Byte-identical across backends for the same double.\n\nThe problem: to format an arbitrary binary64 to N decimal places requires exact decimal conversion, which backends do natively but differently. E.g. Python's `%.17g` vs JS's `toFixed(17)` vs Lua `%.17g` vs Go `%g` — these produce different digit strings for the same value because shortest-roundtrip algorithms differ from fixed-precision.\n\nActually, here's a thought: **`%.17g` (17 significant digits) is enough to roundtrip every double, and is what many languages produce for the same algorithm.** Let me check: Python `'%.17g' % x`, C `printf(\"%.17g\", x)`, Lua `string.format(\"%.17g\", x)` (Lua 5.3 uses C's printf for its integer? no, Lua's `%.17g` on a float is C printf), Go `strconv.FormatFloat(x, 'g', 17, 64)`, jq `tostring` (uses its own, different), JS `x.toPrecision(17)`.\n\nBut these don't all agree: JS `(0.1).toPrecision(17)` gives \"0.10000000000000001\". C `printf(\"%.17g\", 0.1)` gives \"0.10000000000000001\". Go gives \"0.10000000000000001\". Python same. So `%.17g` DOES agree for 0.1 across these. Hmm, but the *decimal-to-double* conversion path matters: as long as each prints 17 significant digits of the exact binary value, and each language's float->decimal at 17 sig digits is correctly rounded (all modern ones are), they agree.\n\nThe exponent format is the sticking point: C/Python/Lua `%g` use `e+06` style? Let me recall:\n- Python `'%g'`: uses `e+06`? Actually Python uses `1e+06` for large, `1e-05` for small — wait, Python `'%g' % 1000000.0` → `'1e+06'`. C printf: `1e+06`. Lua: same as C. Go `%g`: `1e+06`. JS `toPrecision`: `1.00000000000000e+6` (exponent without leading zero, and with trailing precision digits — no, toPrecision(17) of 1e6 gives \"1.0000000000000000e+6\"! because it keeps 17 sig figs). Hmm, JS toPrecision always keeps the full significant digits and uses `e+6` style. So `(1e21).toPrecision(17)` → \"1.0000000000000000e+21\". That disagrees with Python `'%.17g' % 1e21` → \"1.0000000000000000e+21\". Actually those agree! Python's %.17g of 1e21 = \"1.0000000000000000e+21\"? Let me think. C `%.17g` of 1e21: 21 digits needed, exponent → \"1.0000000000000000e+21\". Yes.\n\nBut JS `toPrecision` exponent has no leading zero: `1.0000000000000000e+21`. C/Python: `1.0000000000000000e+21`. Both same here. But for 1e-5: C `%.17g` → \"9.9999999999999995e-06\"? No wait, 1e-5 as double is 9.9999999999999995e-06, and %.17g prints \"9.9999999999999995e-06\"? Actually `%.17g` gives 17 sig digits: \"9.9999999999999995e-06\". JS toPrecision(17) of 1e-5 gives \"9.9999999999999995e-6\" — exponent without leading zero: `e-6` vs `e-06`. Disagreement!\n\nSo JS's `toPrecision` uses no leading zero in exponent, C/Python/Lua/Go use two-digit minimum exponent. And jq is yet another. And Rust `{}` (shortest, Ryu) uses `e-5`? Rust: `format!(\"{}\", 1e-5)` = \"0.00001\" (Rust switches to scientific at... Rust {} for f64 uses shortest and switches to exponent at 1e-5? Let me recall: Rust prints `1e-5`? Actually Rust {} of 1e-5 is \"0.00001\"? Hmm, Rust's shortest representation: for values with absolute value >= 1e-5 and < 1e21 it prints decimal, else scientific. So 1e-5 → \"0.00001\", 1e21 → \"1e21\". Yes.\n\nOK so this confirms: native formats disagree. The issue explicitly says pick a format and hold every backend to it. Given the effort, the practical answer used by languages that care about this (like Go's strconv, or jq's own) is a shortest-roundtrip via Ryu. But reimplementing Ryu in 7 languages is way too much for this slice.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe pick the **`%.17g`-style fixed-17-significant-digits** format with a consistent exponent notation, and implement it in each backend using what each language can do:\n\n- Python: `'%.17g' % x` then normalize exponent to a canonical form (strip leading zeros in exponent: `e+06` → `e+6`, or add? need one canonical). Actually simpler: get the decimal via `repr`? No, repr is shortest, not 17.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about what's actually achievable and robust.\n\n**Key insight:** The corpus harness runs each backend and compares stdout bytes. The tests I write will pin specific outputs. So the format I choose must be implementable in ALL 7 backends with byte-identical results. I get to pick which outputs appear in the tests, but the format must be a *general* function of the double, since arbitrary arithmetic results get printed.\n\nWait — but actually, do the corpus tests need arbitrary float results to print? Yes: `3.14 + 1.0` prints `4.14`. So I need a general printer.\n\nGiven the difficulty of shortest-roundtrip per backend, let me consider **fixed decimal places** (e.g. always N decimal places)? `3.14 + 1.0 = 4.140000`? That's ugly but unambiguous and EASY to implement in every backend — each can do `x + 0` then format to fixed decimals... but fixed decimal places loses precision for large/small numbers and isn't roundtrip, and `1e21` would print as `1000000000000000000000.000000` — huge but consistent. But it's not really a good \"float literal print format\" and it makes the NaN/Infinity question weirder (Infinity → what?). Also \"shortest-roundtrip is still open per-backend conformance work\" — the issue says the printing format is my call. Fixed-decimal is defensible but poor.\n\nLet me reconsider the actual magnitude of implementing a canonical format per backend. What if the canonical format is: **Rust's `format!(\"{}\", x)` shortest-roundtrip output, reimplemented via each backend's own shortest-roundtrip with normalization to match Rust's exact rules?**\n\nThe differences between shortest-roundtrip algorithms are extremely rare in the corpus tests I'd write (values like 3.14, 1.5, 2.0, 0.1, 0.2+0.1, 100.0, 1e21, etc.). The main differences are:\n1. Exponent notation: Rust uses `e` lowercase, no leading zeros, e.g. `1e21`, `1e-5`. Python repr: `1e+21`? Python repr of 1e21 is `1e+21`. JS String(1e21) = `1e+21`. Rust = `1e21`. Go %g = `1e+21`? Go `fmt.Sprintf(\"%v\", 1e21)` = `1e+21`. Lua tostring = `1e+21`. jq tostring = `1e+21`? Hmm jq outputs `1e+21`? Actually jq outputs numbers in a shortest form; jq prints `1e+21`? I recall jq prints `1e+21` for 1e21... not sure.\n\nSo Rust's `e21` without `+` is unusual. Python/JS/Go/Lua all use `e+21`. jq... jq uses `1e+21`.\n\n2. When to switch to exponent: Rust switches to scientific for >= 1e21 and < 1e-5. Python repr switches at >= 1e16 and < 1e-4. JS String() switches at >= 1e21 and < 1e-6. Go %g at >= 1e21 and < 1e-4 (or 1e-4?). These differ!\n\n3. Trailing `.0`: Rust prints `2.0` as `2`? No wait — Rust `format!(\"{}\", 2.0)` gives \"2\". Python `repr(2.0)` = \"2.0\". JS `String(2.0)` = \"2\". Go `%v` 2.0 = \"2\". Lua `tostring(2.0)` = \"2.0\". jq: 2.0 → \"2\"? jq prints \"2\" I think (JSON numbers).\n\nSo huge disagreement on integral floats. Rust/JS/Go give \"2\", Python/Lua give \"2.0\".\n\nThis is exactly why the issue says the printer is \"the only specification of output format\" and needs \"a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand\".\n\nOK here's my decision path. Given that reimplementing Ryu in 7 languages is not realistic, and native shortest formats disagree, the *pragmatic* choice that gets us byte-identical output with modest per-backend work:\n\n**Canonical format: 17 significant digits, fixed notation where the decimal exponent is in [-4, 20] (i.e. 1e-4 <= |x| < 1e21... let me pick the Rust-ish switchover), else scientific `d.ddddddde±dd` — no wait, I need each backend to produce the exact decimal digits.**\n\nHmm, the core issue remains: producing the *decimal digits of a double* correctly. Every backend CAN do this:\n- Rust: `format!(\"{}\", x)` is shortest; but I want 17-digit fixed? I can get 17 digits from `format!(\"{:.17e}\", x)` → scientific with 17 sig digits, then reformat.\n- Python: `\"{:.17e}\".format(x)` → same digits (C-style %.17e), correctly rounded.\n- JS: `x.toExponential(17)` → 17 digits after the point (18 sig figs? no, toExponential(17) = 17 digits after decimal = 18 sig figs). Hmm, JS toExponential(fractionDigits) gives fractionDigits digits after the point. So toExponential(16) = 17 sig figs. OK.\n- Lua: `string.format(\"%.17e\", x)`.\n- Go: `strconv.FormatFloat(x, 'e', 17, 64)`? That gives 17 digits after point too (18 sig). Go's 'e' format with precision 17 = 17 sig figs or 17 after point? Go's FormatFloat precision is the number of digits after the decimal point for 'e'... Let me recall: strconv.FormatFloat(123.456, 'e', 5, 64) = \"1.23456e+02\" — that's 5 digits after point = 6 sig figs. So precision = digits after point. So for 17 sig figs, use precision 16.\n- jq: jq can't do %.17e directly... jq has `tostring`, `@sh`, etc. jq doesn't have printf-style formatting of doubles with arbitrary precision. Hmm. jq's number formatting is its own shortest form.\n- LLVM (C runtime): `sprintf(buf, \"%.17e\", x)` — C gives this fine.\n- toylang native backend: it's LLVM, same thing.\n\nSo the painful ones are jq (and to a lesser extent JS/Go exponent conventions, which are easy to normalize).\n\nHonestly, I think the realistic, robust choice given 7 backends and the \"agreement harness\" is:\n\n**Use each backend's shortest-roundtrip native printing, then normalize only the small set of textual differences.** But the differences (switchover points, .0 suffix) are baked into the native format and can't be \"normalized\" trivially — e.g. Python gives \"2.0\" and there's no cheap way to strip \".0\" in general (well, you can: if the string ends in \".0\" strip it — that's exactly right for shortest repr since only integral values end in \".0\"). Python repr also gives \"0.30000000000000004\" for 0.1+0.2, Rust gives \"0.30000000000000004\", JS gives \"0.30000000000000004\". Good, those agree (shortest-roundtrip is unique! By the Ryu theorem, the shortest string that roundtrips to the same double is unique given a fixed correct algorithm, and modern all use correctly-rounded shortest → they agree on digits!). \n\nWait, that's a key point: **shortest-roundtrip decimal digits are essentially unique** across Python repr, Rust {}, JS String(), Go %v (which is %g shortest), jq tostring — they all produce the same digit string because there's only one shortest correctly-rounded representation. The differences are ONLY:\n1. Whether integral values print with `.0` (Python, Lua add it; Rust/JS/Go/jq don't).\n2. The exponent notation (e+21 vs e21) and the exact switchover threshold between decimal and scientific notation.\n3. Lua's tostring uses `%.14g`-ish (14 sig digits — NOT shortest, 14 < 17 needed for roundtrip) — Lua's tostring for doubles gives only 14 sig digits, which can lose precision! Actually Lua 5.3 tostring uses \"%.14g\". That produces 14-digit results that differ from shortest (which can need up to 17). E.g. 0.1+0.2 in Lua tostring = \"0.3\"! Because %.14g of 0.30000000000000004 = \"0.3\". Disagreement!\n\nSo Lua is the problem child — its default `tostring` is 14 sig digits.\n\nOK so the \"normalize native shortest\" approach requires at minimum:\n- Lua: reimplement shortest or use %.17g and then... %.17g of 3.14 = \"3.1400000000000001\" — not shortest. Disagree with others.\n\nSo Lua can't be \"shortest\" natively. Therefore a **fixed 17-sig-digit format** is actually the more uniform choice across ALL backends, since every backend can do 17 sig digits (Lua %.17g, Python %.17g, Rust, JS toPrecision(16)/toExponential(16), Go, C). And jq... jq remains the problem.\n\nLet me look at what jq can actually do for number formatting. jq has:\n- `tostring` → its own shortest.\n- sprintf: jq has `@base64`, and a `sprintf`/`format` via... jq 1.7 doesn't have a general sprintf for numbers with precision? Actually jq doesn't have printf. There's no way to get %.17g out of jq.\n\nHmm. Let me actually check what jq does with floats. jq uses its own JSON number output. For a float like 3.14 it prints 3.14. For 2.0 it prints 2 (jq's numbers are doubles but it prints 2.0 as 2? Let me verify — jq -n '2.0' prints `2`). For 1e21, `jq -n '1e21'` prints `1e+21`. For 0.1+0.2, `jq -n '0.1+0.2'` prints `0.30000000000000004`. \n\nSo jq actually produces shortest-roundtrip output (jq uses its own Ryu-ish). jq's digits agree with Rust/Python/JS/Go shortest digits. The exponent: jq prints `1e+21` (with +). And integral: `2`.\n\nSo the landscape:\n- Rust {} : shortest, no `.0`, exponent `e21` (no +), switch at <1e-5 or >=1e21.\n- Python repr: shortest, `.0` for integral, `e+21` (with +), switch at <1e-4 or >=1e16.\n- JS String(): shortest, no `.0`, `e+21`, switch at <1e-6 or >=1e21.\n- Go %v: shortest, no `.0`, `e+21`, switch at <1e-4 or >=1e21.\n- Lua tostring: 14 sig digits (not shortest!), `.0`, `e+21`-ish.\n- jq tostring: shortest, no `.0`, `e+21`, switch at... jq prints `1e-05`? or `0.00001`? Need to check.\n- LLVM/C: we control it fully.\n\nThis is genuinely messy. The cleanest path to byte-identical is to **define our own canonical printer** and implement it in each backend from the 17-sig-digit primitive each provides, with a fixed exponent/notation rule, then handle jq specially.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider jq. jq's `tostring` gives shortest. What if the canonical format IS \"shortest-roundtrip, integral floats print without `.0`, and scientific notation is used only where necessary with a canonical `e`+/- exponent, switchover points fixed\"? Then jq mostly matches if its switchover matches ours. The remaining fixable differences: Lua (14 digits → must fix by reimplementing shortest or by %.17g+normalize? No...).\n\nHmm, let me reconsider whether shortest or fixed-17 is better given Lua and jq.\n\nActually, you know what — let me look at how the existing backends already print and whether there's already some canonical printing infrastructure, and check the docs/overview for what 3.14 printing is specified as. The ADR references overview/vision.md values list. Let me check that, and also check if there's an existing spec of float printing anywhere (maybe in plans/).\n\nLet me also check the test harness for how many of the 7 backends actually run in `just check` (whether LLVM/Rust need toolchains — they do: cc and rustc; those are installed presumably).\n\nLet me look at more context: docs/overview/vision.md values, and grep for float/3.14 in docs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0544fe05f001AN5j1PLdgSU4f5","sessionID":"ses_fabb535ccffe1uMPqoZsBbW0jp","messageID":"msg_0544d4118001XPSTPdAoQm2rgZ"}}
